// Worked EXAMPLE problems (Hibbeler, Mechanics of Materials, 9th ed.) for the interviewer mode.
// Generated from the book; figures in figures/ex/. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
window.EXAMPLES=[{"id":"1.1","title":"Cantilever with triangular load: internal loadings at C","q":"Determine the resultant internal loadings acting on the cross section at C of the cantilevered beam shown in the figure.","figs":["ex1-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever AB fixed at A (left wall), free at B (right). Linearly varying distributed load, 270 N/m at A decreasing to 0 at B. Point C is 3 m from A; CB = 6 m.","sol":"1) Use segment CB so the support reactions at A aren\u0027t needed. 2) Load intensity at C by similar triangles: w = 270(6/9) = 180 N/m; resultant of the triangle on CB = ½(180)(6) = 540 N acting 2 m from C. 3) ΣFx = 0: N_C = 0. ΣFy = 0: V_C − 540 = 0. ΣM_C = 0: −M_C − 540(2) = 0. Ans: N_C = 0, V_C = 540 N, M_C = −1080 N·m (acts opposite to the assumed direction).","answers":["N_C = 0","V_C = 540 N","M_C = −1080 N·m"]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Engine hanging from crane boom: internal loadings at E","q":"The 500-kg engine is suspended from the crane boom shown in the figure. Determine the resultant internal loadings acting on the cross section of the boom at point E.","figs":["ex1-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical post on the left. Horizontal boom AB is pinned to the post at A and extends to the right to B; point E is 1 m from A, C is 2 m from A, B is 3 m from A. A two-force member CD runs from pin D on the post, 1.5 m above A, down to pin C on the boom. The 500-kg engine hangs from the hook at B.","sol":"1) Boom free-body first to get the pin reaction at A (CD is a two-force member along its 3-4-5 line, 3 vertical : 4 horizontal). 2) ΣM_A = 0: F_CD(3/5)(2 m) − 500(9.81)(3 m) = 0 → F_CD = 12 262.5 N. 3) ΣFx: A_x = F_CD(4/5) = 9810 N. ΣFy: −A_y + F_CD(3/5) − 500(9.81) = 0 → A_y = 2452.5 N. 4) Cut at E and use segment AE (A_x acting to the right, A_y downward, 1 m long): ΣFx: N_E + 9810 = 0 → N_E = −9810 N. ΣFy: −V_E − 2452.5 = 0 → V_E = −2452.5 N. ΣM_E: M_E + 2452.5(1) = 0 → M_E = −2452.5 N·m. Negative signs mean opposite to the assumed senses. Ans: N_E = −9.81 kN, V_E = −2.45 kN, M_E = −2.45 kN·m.","answers":["N_E = −9.81 kN","V_E = −2.45 kN","M_E = −2.45 kN·m"]},{"id":"1.3","title":"Pin-connected beam and links: internal loadings at G","q":"Determine the resultant internal loadings acting on the cross section at G of the beam shown in the figure. Each joint is pin connected.","figs":["ex1-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AE, pinned to a wall at E (right end), free at A (left end). A 1500 lb downward force acts at A. Point G is 2 ft from A and D is 4 ft from A. A triangular distributed load of 300 lb/ft at D decreases linearly to zero at E over 6 ft. Two-force links: AB (inclined from A up to B, 3 ft above the beam, 4 ft horizontally from A, a 3-4-5 line), BD (vertical from B down to D), and BC (horizontal from B to the wall at C). C is 3 ft above E.","sol":"1) Whole structure: triangular load = ½(6)(300) = 900 lb at 4 ft from E. Moments about E and force balance give F_BC = 6200 lb (BC horizontal), E_x = 6200 lb, E_y = 2400 lb. 2) Joint B (BA, BD, BC two-force members): F_BA = 7750 lb (3-4-5 direction), F_BD = 4650 lb. 3) Segment AG (2 ft long) with 1500 lb down at A and F_BA = 7750 lb at A acting up and to the right (4/5 horizontal, 3/5 vertical). ΣFx: 7750(4/5) + N_G = 0 → N_G = −6200 lb. ΣFy: −1500 + 7750(3/5) − V_G = 0 → V_G = 3150 lb. ΣM_G: M_G − 7750(3/5)(2) + 1500(2) = 0 → M_G = 6300 lb·ft. Ans: N_G = −6200 lb, V_G = 3150 lb, M_G = 6300 lb·ft.","answers":["N_G = −6200 lb","V_G = 3150 lb","M_G = 6300 lb·ft"]},{"id":"1.4","title":"Bent pipe under end loads: internal loadings at B","q":"Determine the resultant internal loadings acting on the cross section at B of the pipe shown in the figure. End A is subjected to a vertical force of 50 N, a horizontal force of 30 N, and a couple moment of 70 N·m. Neglect the pipe\u0027s mass.","figs":["ex1-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Pipe fixed to a wall flange at C. From C the pipe runs 0.75 m to point B, then 0.5 m further to elbow D, then turns 90° horizontally for 1.25 m to the free end A. At A: a 50 N force acting vertically downward, a 30 N horizontal force, and a 70 N·m couple moment (shown with a curved arrow about a horizontal axis).","sol":"1) Use segment AB so the reactions at C are not needed. Set x, y, z axes at B (z vertical, y along the C-B-D leg, x along the 1.25 m leg) and assume all force/moment components at B positive. 2) Forces: ΣFx: (F_B)x = 0. ΣFy: (F_B)y + 30 = 0 → −30 N. ΣFz: (F_B)z − 50 = 0 → 50 N. 3) Moments: Σ(M_B)x: (M_B)x + 70 − 50(0.5) = 0 → −45 N·m. Σ(M_B)y: (M_B)y + 50(1.25) = 0 → −62.5 N·m. Σ(M_B)z: (M_B)z + 30(1.25) = 0 → −37.5 N·m. Negative signs mean opposite to assumed direction. Note: N_B = 30 N, V_B = 50 N, torsion T_B = 62.5 N·m, bending moment M_B = √(45² + 37.5²) = 58.6 N·m. Ans: (F_B)x = 0, (F_B)y = −30 N, (F_B)z = 50 N, (M_B)x = −45 N·m, (M_B)y = −62.5 N·m, (M_B)z = −37.5 N·m.","answers":["(F_B)x = 0","(F_B)y = −30 N","(F_B)z = 50 N","(M_B)x = −45 N·m","(M_B)y = −62.5 N·m","(M_B)z = −37.5 N·m"]},{"id":"1.5","title":"Stepped axial loading on a bar: maximum average normal stress","q":"The bar shown in the figure has a constant width of 35 mm and a thickness of 10 mm. Determine the maximum average normal stress in the bar when it is subjected to the loading shown.","figs":["ex1-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal flat bar with points A, B, C, D from left to right. A 12 kN tension pull acts to the left at A (eyelet) and a 22 kN pull acts to the right at D. At collar B there are two 9 kN forces pointing left (one on each side of the bar); at collar C there are two 4 kN forces pointing right. Bar width 35 mm (thickness 10 mm given in the text).","sol":"1) Cut each region and find the internal axial force: P_AB = 12 kN; P_BC = 12 + 9 + 9 = 30 kN; P_CD = 22 kN. 2) Area is constant (0.035 m)(0.010 m), so the maximum stress is in BC, where the force is largest. 3) σ_BC = P_BC/A = 30(10³) N / [(0.035)(0.010) m²] = 85.7 MPa (tension). Ans: σ_max = 85.7 MPa in region BC.","answers":["σ_BC = 85.7 MPa"]},{"id":"1.6","title":"Lamp hung by two rods: average normal stress in each","q":"The 80-kg lamp is supported by two rods AB and BC as shown in the figure. If AB has a diameter of 10 mm and BC has a diameter of 8 mm, determine the average normal stress in each rod.","figs":["ex1-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Lamp hangs at joint B. Rod BA goes up and to the left to wall hook A, making 60° with the horizontal. Rod BC goes up and to the right to wall hook C, along a 3-4-5 slope (3 vertical : 4 horizontal). Rod AB is 10 mm diameter, BC is 8 mm diameter.","sol":"1) FBD of joint B with weight W = 80(9.81) = 784.8 N. 2) ΣFx: F_BC(4/5) − F_BA cos 60° = 0. ΣFy: F_BC(3/5) + F_BA sin 60° − 784.8 = 0. Solving: F_BC = 395.2 N, F_BA = 632.4 N (both tension). 3) σ = F/A. σ_BC = 395.2 / [π(0.004)²] = 7.86 MPa. σ_BA = 632.4 / [π(0.005)²] = 8.05 MPa. Ans: σ_BC = 7.86 MPa, σ_BA = 8.05 MPa.","answers":["σ_BC = 7.86 MPa","σ_BA = 8.05 MPa"]},{"id":"1.7","title":"Steel casting: average compressive stress at points A and B","q":"The casting shown in the figure is made of steel having a specific weight of γ_st = 490 lb/ft³. Determine the average compressive stress acting at points A and B.","figs":["ex1-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Solid vertical circular cylinder of radius 0.75 ft with axes x, y, z; z is the cylinder axis. A horizontal section plane lies 0.75 ft above the base and 2.75 ft below the top face. Points A and B lie in that section plane, located by offsets of 0.75 ft and 0.4 ft from the axis in the x and y directions.","sol":"1) Cut the casting at the plane through A and B and take the top segment as the free body: the weight W_st = γ_st V_st acts down, internal axial force P acts up. 2) ΣFz: P − W_st = 0 → P = (490 lb/ft³)(2.75 ft)[π(0.75 ft)²] = 2381 lb. 3) Section area A = π(0.75 ft)² (the cross section is the full circle), so σ = P/A = 2381/[π(0.75)²] = 1347.5 lb/ft² = 1347.5/144 = 9.36 psi (compressive), the same at A and B because stress is uniform over the section. Ans: σ = 9.36 psi.","answers":["σ_A = σ_B = 9.36 psi (compression)"]},{"id":"1.8","title":"Position of load for equal tension and compression stresses","q":"Member AC shown in the figure is subjected to a vertical force of 3 kN. Determine the position x of this force so that the average compressive stress at the smooth support C is equal to the average tensile stress in the tie rod AB. The rod has a cross-sectional area of 400 mm² and the contact area at C is 650 mm².","figs":["ex1-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AC, 200 mm long, with a vertical tie rod AB pinned at A and hanging from a ceiling at B. A smooth support at C (right end) pushes up on the beam. A downward 3 kN force acts at a distance x measured from A (along the beam, from the tie-rod line).","sol":"1) Work in N and mm. FBD of AC: tie rod pulls up F_AB at A, support pushes up F_C at C. 2) ΣFy: F_AB + F_C − 3000 = 0. ΣM_A: −3000(x) + F_C(200) = 0. 3) Equal stresses: F_AB/400 = F_C/650 → F_C = 1.625 F_AB. 4) Substitute: F_AB = 3000/2.625 = 1143 N, F_C = 1857 N. 5) x = F_C(200)/3000 = 124 mm. Check 0 \u003c x \u003c 200 mm. Ans: x = 124 mm.","answers":["x = 124 mm"]},{"id":"1.9","title":"Beam supported by pins: average shear stress in pins","q":"Determine the average shear stress in the 20-mm-diameter pin at A and the 30-mm-diameter pin at B that support the beam shown in the figure. The beam has a 30 kN downward load.","figs":["ex1-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AB pinned at A (left, to a bracket holding the pin on two fixed leaves) and at B (right) to an inclined cable BC that goes up and to the right to an overhead support C on a 3-4-5 slope (4 vertical : 3 horizontal). A 30 kN downward force acts 2 m from A; B is 6 m from A (2 m + 4 m). Cable end at B is connected with a pin to a bracket on the beam.","sol":"1) Beam equilibrium: ΣM_A: F_B(4/5)(6 m) − 30(2) = 0 → F_B = 12.5 kN. ΣFx: A_x = 12.5(3/5) = 7.50 kN. ΣFy: A_y + 12.5(4/5) − 30 = 0 → A_y = 20 kN. 2) Pin A resultant: F_A = √(7.5² + 20²) = 21.36 kN. Pin A is in double shear (two shear surfaces), so V_A = F_A/2 = 10.68 kN. Pin B is in single shear: V_B = F_B = 12.5 kN. 3) τ_A = 10.68(10³) / [π/4 (0.02)²] = 34.0 MPa. τ_B = 12.5(10³) / [π/4 (0.03)²] = 17.7 MPa. Ans: τ_A = 34.0 MPa, τ_B = 17.7 MPa.","answers":["(τ_A)avg = 34.0 MPa","(τ_B)avg = 17.7 MPa"]},{"id":"1.10","title":"Wood joint: average shear stress on planes a-a and b-b","q":"If the wood joint shown in the figure has a width of 150 mm, determine the average shear stress developed along shear planes a-a and b-b. For each plane, represent the state of stress on an element of the material. The joint carries a 6 kN tensile load.","figs":["ex1-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Two wood members joined by a slotted (notched) connection. The left member has a rectangular slot into which the end of the right member (a block with a narrower tongue extending right) fits. A 6 kN pull acts to the left on the left member and a 6 kN pull to the right on the tongue. Two horizontal shear planes are marked: a-a (upper, along a length of 0.1 m) and b-b (lower, along a length of 0.125 m). Joint width 150 mm.","sol":"1) Free body of the inner member: the 6 kN pull is resisted by two equal forces F, one on each side: 6 − F − F = 0 → F = 3 kN. 2) Cut along each shear plane: V_a = 3 kN and V_b = 3 kN (ΣFx = 0). 3) Shear areas: A_a = (0.1 m)(0.15 m), A_b = (0.125 m)(0.15 m). τ_a = 3(10³)/[(0.1)(0.15)] = 200 kPa. τ_b = 3(10³)/[(0.125)(0.15)] = 160 kPa. 4) Each is drawn as a pure-shear element: complementary shear stresses on perpendicular faces with the sense matching V. Ans: τ_a = 200 kPa, τ_b = 160 kPa.","answers":["(τ_a)avg = 200 kPa","(τ_b)avg = 160 kPa"]},{"id":"1.11","title":"Inclined timber member: contact and shear stresses","q":"The inclined member shown in the figure is subjected to a compressive force of 600 lb. Determine the average compressive stress along the smooth areas of contact defined by AB and BC, and the average shear stress along the horizontal plane defined by DB.","figs":["ex1-11.webp"],"figDesc":"A wooden inclined member rests in a notch on a horizontal member. The 600 lb compressive force acts along the inclined member\u0027s axis, direction a 3-4-5 triangle (3 horizontal : 4 vertical). Contact areas: AB is the vertical face (1 in. by 1.5 in.) and BC is the horizontal face (2 in. by 1.5 in.). Plane DB is the horizontal plane through the lower member, 3 in. long by 1.5 in. wide. Member width is 1.5 in.","sol":"1) FBD of the inclined member: the 600 lb is balanced by F_AB (horizontal, from face AB) and F_BC (vertical, from face BC). ΣFx: F_AB − 600(3/5) = 0 → 360 lb. ΣFy: F_BC − 600(4/5) = 0 → 480 lb. 2) Top segment ABD of the lower member: horizontal ΣFx: V − 360 = 0 → V = 360 lb on plane DB. 3) σ_AB = 360/[(1)(1.5)] = 240 psi. σ_BC = 480/[(2)(1.5)] = 160 psi. τ_avg = 360/[(3)(1.5)] = 80 psi. Ans: σ_AB = 240 psi, σ_BC = 160 psi, τ_avg = 80 psi.","answers":["σ_AB = 240 psi","σ_BC = 160 psi","τ_avg = 80 psi"]},{"id":"1.12","title":"Control arm pins: required diameters from allowable shear","q":"The control arm is subjected to the loading shown in the figure. Determine to the nearest 1/4 in. the required diameters of the steel pins at A and C if the allowable shear stress for the steel is τ_allow = 8 ksi. The pin at A is in single shear and the pin at C is in double shear.","figs":["ex1-12.webp"],"figDesc":"L-shaped control arm. Link AB is horizontal, from a wall pin at B to the top of the arm at A. The vertical leg is 8 in. long from A down to the pin C at the bottom corner, which sits in a clevis on a support (double shear). Along the horizontal bottom leg, from C: a vertical 3 kip downward force 3 in. to the right of C, and a 5 kip force 2 in. further right, directed down and right on a 3-4-5 slope (3 vertical : 4 horizontal). Insets show pin A in single shear and pin C in double shear.","sol":"1) FBD of the arm; link AB is a two-force member so it pulls horizontally with F_AB at A. 2) ΣM_C: F_AB(8) − 3(3) − 5(3/5)(5) = 0 → F_AB = 3 kip. ΣFx: −3 − C_x + 5(4/5) = 0 → C_x = 1 kip. ΣFy: C_y − 3 − 5(3/5) = 0 → C_y = 6 kip. F_C = √(1² + 6²) = 6.083 kip. 3) Pin A (single shear, V = 3 kip): A = V/τ_allow; π(d_A/2)² = 3/8 → d_A = 0.691 in. → use 3/4 in. 4) Pin C (double shear, V = 6.083/2 = 3.041 kip): π(d_C/2)² = 3.041/8 → d_C = 0.696 in. → use 3/4 in. Ans: d_A = 3/4 in., d_C = 3/4 in.","answers":["d_A = 3/4 in.","d_C = 3/4 in."]},{"id":"1.13","title":"Suspender rod and disk: minimum rod diameter and disk thickness","q":"The suspender rod is supported at its end by a fixed-connected circular disk as shown in the figure. If the rod passes through a 40-mm-diameter hole, determine the minimum required diameter of the rod and the minimum thickness of the disk needed to support the 20-kN load. The allowable normal stress for the rod is σ_allow = 60 MPa, and the allowable shear stress for the disk is τ_allow = 35 MPa.","figs":["ex1-13.webp"],"figDesc":"A circular disk rests on a surface around a 40 mm diameter hole (dashed circle on the disk top). A vertical rod of diameter d is attached to the center of the disk and hangs down through the hole, carrying a 20 kN downward load at its lower end. The disk has thickness t.","sol":"1) Rod: axial force = 20 kN. A = P/σ_allow: (π/4)d² = 20(10³)/[60(10⁶)] → d = 0.0206 m = 20.6 mm. 2) Disk: the load tries to punch a cylinder of the disk through the hole. The shear surface is the perimeter of the 40 mm hole times t, with V = 20 kN. A = V/τ_allow: 2π(0.02 m)(t) = 20(10³)/[35(10⁶)] → t = 4.55(10⁻³) m = 4.55 mm. Ans: d = 20.6 mm, t = 4.55 mm.","answers":["d = 20.6 mm","t = 4.55 mm"]},{"id":"1.14","title":"Shaft with collar: largest allowable load P","q":"The shaft shown in the figure is supported by the collar at C, which is attached to the shaft and located on the right side of the bearing at B. Determine the largest value of P for the axial forces at E and F so that the bearing stress on the collar does not exceed an allowable stress of (σ_b)allow = 75 MPa and the average normal stress in the shaft does not exceed an allowable stress of (σ_t)allow = 55 MPa.","figs":["ex1-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal shaft of 60 mm diameter on two bearings (A on the left, B on the right). A 2P force pulls left at the left end F; a force P acts leftward at point E (a collar near the middle); a collar C at the right end of the shaft, just right of bearing B, has diameter 80 mm and a thickness of 20 mm.","sol":"1) Check each failure mode, then take the smaller P. 2) Axial loads by sections: region FE carries 2P; region EC carries 3P (largest). Area is constant, A = π(0.03 m)². Shaft stress: π(0.03)² = 3P/[55(10⁶)] → P = 51.8 kN. 3) Collar bearing: load 3P over the annular bearing area A_b = π(0.04)² − π(0.03)² = 2.199(10⁻³) m² (80 mm collar on 60 mm shaft). 2.199(10⁻³) = 3P/[75(10⁶)] → P = 55.0 kN. 4) The smaller value controls: P = 51.8 kN (shaft normal stress). Shear failure of the collar is not considered. Ans: P = 51.8 kN.","answers":["P_max = 51.8 kN (shaft normal stress governs; bearing stress gives 55.0 kN)"]},{"id":"1.15","title":"LRFD design of a steel rod supporting a bar","q":"The 400-kg uniform bar AB shown in the figure is supported by a steel rod AC and a roller at B. If it supports a live distributed loading of 3 kN/m, determine the required diameter of the rod. The failure stress for the steel is σ_fail = 345 MPa. Use the LRFD method, where the resistance factor for tension is φ = 0.9 and the load factors for the dead and live loads are γ_D = 1.2 and γ_L = 1.6, respectively.","figs":["ex1-15.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal uniform bar AB, 2 m long. Steel rod AC is vertical, attached at A (left end) and hanging from a support at C above. The right end B rests on a roller. A uniformly distributed live load of 3 kN/m acts downward over the full 2 m length.","sol":"1) Factored loads. Dead load (bar weight): D = 400(9.81) = 3.924 kN, factored 1.2D = 4.709 kN (acts at midspan). Live load: L = 3(2) = 6 kN, factored 1.6L = 9.60 kN (acts at midspan, 1 m from B). 2) ΣM_B = 0: 9.60(1) + 4.709(1) − F_AC(2) = 0 → F_AC = 7.154 kN. 3) Design: φP_n ≥ F_AC with P_n = σ_fail A: 0.9[345(10⁶)] A_AC = 7.154(10³) → A = 23.04(10⁻⁶) m² = 23.04 mm². 4) (π/4)d² = 23.04 mm² → d = 5.42 mm. Ans: d_AC = 5.42 mm.","answers":["F_AC = 7.154 kN (factored)","d_AC = 5.42 mm"]},{"id":"2.1","title":"Rod with temperature-induced strain: end displacement and average strain","q":"The slender rod shown in the figure is subjected to an increase of temperature along its axis, which creates a normal strain in the rod of ε_z = 40(10⁻³)z^(1/2), where z is measured in meters. Determine (a) the displacement of the end B of the rod due to the temperature increase, and (b) the average normal strain in the rod.","figs":["ex2-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Slender vertical rod hanging from a support at A at the top; B is the free bottom end. Length 200 mm. The coordinate z is measured down from A; a differential segment dz is shown at position z.","sol":"1) A differential segment dz stretches to dz\u0027 = dz + ε_z dz = [1 + 40(10⁻³)z^(1/2)] dz. 2) Integrate for the deformed length: z\u0027 = ∫₀^0.2 [1 + 40(10⁻³)z^(1/2)] dz = [z + 40(10⁻³)(2/3)z^(3/2)] from 0 to 0.2 m = 0.20239 m. 3) (a) Displacement of end B = 0.20239 − 0.2 = 0.00239 m = 2.39 mm (downward). 4) (b) Average strain = Δ/L = 2.39 mm / 200 mm = 0.0119 mm/mm. This is a thermal strain, not load-caused. Ans: δ_B = 2.39 mm down, ε_avg = 0.0119 mm/mm.","answers":["δ_B = 2.39 mm (downward)","ε_avg = 0.0119 mm/mm"]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Rotated rigid lever arm: normal strain in a wire","q":"When force P is applied to the rigid lever arm ABC shown in the figure, the arm rotates counterclockwise about pin A through an angle of 0.05°. Determine the normal strain developed in wire BD.","figs":["ex2-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal rigid lever arm CBA pinned to a wall at A (right end). Wire BD runs vertically from B up to a ceiling support at D, length 300 mm. B is 400 mm to the left of A; C is the handle end at the far left, where a downward force P acts. Dimensions: 300 mm (wire BD) and 400 mm (AB).","sol":"Solution I (exact geometry): 1) α = tan⁻¹(400/300) = 53.1301° and L_AD = √(300² + 400²) = 500 mm. 2) After rotation, angle at A in triangle AB\u0027D is φ = 90° − α + 0.05° = 36.92°. 3) Law of cosines: L_B\u0027D = √(500² + 400² − 2(500)(400)cos 36.92°) = 300.3491 mm. 4) ε_BD = (L_B\u0027D − L_BD)/L_BD = (300.3491 − 300)/300 = 0.00116 mm/mm. Solution II (small-angle approximation): ΔL_BD ≈ θ L_AB = (0.05°·π/180)(400) = 0.3491 mm, so ε_BD = 0.3491/300 = 0.00116 mm/mm. Ans: ε_BD = 0.00116 mm/mm.","answers":["ε_BD = 0.00116 mm/mm"]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Plate shifted at one edge: diagonal strain and shear strain","q":"The plate shown in the figure is fixed connected along AB and held in the horizontal guides at its top and bottom, AD and BC. If its right side CD is given a uniform horizontal displacement of 2 mm, determine (a) the average normal strain along the diagonal AC, and (b) the shear strain at E relative to the x, y axes.","figs":["ex2-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Square plate 150 mm by 150 mm with corners A (top left), D (top right), B (bottom left), C (bottom right). Diagonals AC and BD cross at the center E. The plate is fixed along the left edge AB; top and bottom edges slide in horizontal guides. The right edge CD is displaced 2 mm to the right (dashed line). Axes x and y pass through E along the diagonals: x runs along BD toward D, y runs along CA toward A.","sol":"Part (a): 1) Original diagonal AC = √(0.150² + 0.150²) = 0.21213 m. 2) After displacement the plate is a rectangle 150 mm tall by 152 mm wide, so A\u0027C\u0027 = √(0.150² + 0.152²) = 0.21355 m. 3) ε_AC = (0.21355 − 0.21213)/0.21213 = 0.00669 mm/mm. Part (b): 4) After deformation, half the angle at E\u0027: tan(θ\u0027/2) = 76/75 → θ\u0027/2 = 45.38°, θ\u0027 = 90.759° = 1.58404 rad. 5) γ_xy = π/2 − θ\u0027 = 1.5708 − 1.58404 = −0.0132 rad. The negative sign means the angle between the x and y axes has increased above 90°. (If x, y were horizontal and vertical at E, γ_xy would be 0.) Ans: (ε_AC)avg = 0.00669 mm/mm, γ_xy = −0.0132 rad.","answers":["(ε_AC)avg = 0.00669 mm/mm","γ_xy = −0.0132 rad"]},{"id":"3.1","title":"Steel alloy tension test: modulus, yield strength, ultimate and fracture stress","q":"A tension test for a steel alloy results in the stress-strain diagram shown in the figure. Calculate the modulus of elasticity and the yield strength based on a 0.2% offset. Identify on the graph the ultimate stress and the fracture stress.","figs":["ex3-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Stress-strain diagram of a steel alloy: vertical axis σ (ksi) with ticks 10 to 120, horizontal axis ε (in./in.) with ticks every 0.02 from 0.02 to 0.24. The curve rises very steeply from O, bends over, reaches a maximum and then falls, ending at its right-hand end (near ε = 0.23). A second, magnified strain scale printed in blue below the axis (labels 0.0004, 0.0008, 0.0012, 0.0016, 0.0020, 0.0024, with a bracket marked 0.2% at 0.0020) is provided so the initial straight-line portion of the curve, drawn on that scale as the lower curve starting at O, can be read accurately. No answer values or construction lines are shown.","sol":"1) Modulus E = slope of the initial straight line OA. Using the magnified scale, A is approximately (0.0016 in./in., 50 ksi). E = 50 ksi / 0.0016 = 31.2(10³) ksi (line OA: σ = 31.2(10³)ε). 2) Yield strength by 0.2% offset: start at ε = 0.0020, draw a line parallel to OA; where it meets the curve (A\u0027) gives σ_YS ≈ 68 ksi. 3) Ultimate stress = the peak of the curve, point B: σ_u = 108 ksi. 4) Fracture stress = stress at the end of the curve, point C at ε_f = 0.23 in./in.: σ_f = 90 ksi. Ans: E = 31.2(10³) ksi, σ_YS = 68 ksi, σ_u = 108 ksi, σ_f = 90 ksi.","answers":["E = 31.2(10³) ksi","σ_YS = 68 ksi","σ_u = 108 ksi","σ_f = 90 ksi"]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Aluminum alloy unloaded from 600 MPa: permanent strain and resilience","q":"The stress-strain diagram for an aluminum alloy that is used for making aircraft parts is shown in the figure. If a specimen of this material is stressed to 600 MPa, determine the permanent strain that remains in the specimen when the load is released. Also, find the modulus of resilience both before and after the load application.","figs":["ex3-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Stress-strain diagram for the aluminum alloy: vertical axis σ (MPa) with ticks 150, 300, 450, 600, 750; horizontal axis ε (mm/mm) with ticks 0.01, 0.02, 0.03, 0.04. The curve rises steeply and almost linearly from O to point A, labelled σ_Y = 450 MPa (the corresponding yield strain ε_Y = 0.006 is dimensioned below the axis), then curves over, passing through point B at σ = 600 MPa (marked by a horizontal line from the stress axis) and continuing to the end point F near ε = 0.04. A small sketch of a cylindrical specimen under axial tension is at the top right. The strain at B, the unloading line and the permanent strain are not shown.","sol":"1) Elastic slope from OA: E = 450 MPa / 0.006 = 75.0 GPa. 2) Loading to B (σ = 600 MPa) gives ε ≈ 0.023 mm/mm. On release the stress path follows BC, parallel to OA, so the recovered elastic strain CD satisfies E = BD/CD: 75.0(10⁹) = 600(10⁶)/CD → CD = 0.008 mm/mm. 3) Permanent strain ε_OC = 0.023 − 0.008 = 0.0150 mm/mm. (A 50 mm gauge length would become 50 + 0.0150(50) = 50.75 mm.) 4) Modulus of resilience u_r = ½σ_pl ε_pl. Initial: ½(450 MPa)(0.006) = 1.35 MJ/m³. Final (after strain hardening, new proportional limit 600 MPa): ½(600 MPa)(0.008) = 2.40 MJ/m³. Resilience increased, toughness decreased. Ans: ε_OC = 0.0150 mm/mm; (u_r)initial = 1.35 MJ/m³; (u_r)final = 2.40 MJ/m³.","answers":["ε_OC = 0.0150 mm/mm","(u_r)initial = 1.35 MJ/m³","(u_r)final = 2.40 MJ/m³"]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Stepped aluminum rod beyond yield: approximate elongation","q":"An aluminum rod, shown in the figure, has a circular cross section and is subjected to an axial load of 10 kN. If a portion of the stress-strain diagram is shown in the figure, determine the approximate elongation of the rod when the load is applied. Take E_al = 70 GPa.","figs":["ex3-3.webp","ex3-3b.webp"],"figDesc":"Panel (a): a stepped circular rod ABC, loaded in tension by 10 kN at each end. Segment AB is 600 mm long with 20 mm diameter; segment BC is 400 mm long with 15 mm diameter. Panel (b) (second file): part of the aluminum stress-strain diagram, σ (MPa) with ticks 10 to 60 versus ε with ticks 0.02, 0.04, 0.06. The curve rises steeply up to the yield stress σ_Y = 40 MPa (labelled), then continues with a gentle upward slope (plastic region). No construction lines or answer values are shown.","sol":"1) Neglect localized effects at the load points and the section change. 2) Stresses: σ_AB = P/A = 10(10³)/[π(0.01 m)²] = 31.83 MPa; σ_BC = 10(10³)/[π(0.0075 m)²] = 56.59 MPa. 3) Segment AB: 31.83 MPa \u003c σ_Y = 40 MPa, so elastic: ε_AB = σ/E = 31.83(10⁶)/70(10⁹) = 0.0004547 mm/mm. 4) Segment BC: 56.59 MPa \u003e σ_Y, so plastic; read from the graph ε_BC ≈ 0.045 mm/mm. 5) δ = Σ εL = 0.0004547(600) + 0.0450(400) = 18.3 mm. Ans: δ ≈ 18.3 mm.","answers":["δ = 18.3 mm"]},{"id":"3.4","title":"Steel bar in tension: change in length and cross-section","q":"A bar made of A-36 steel has the dimensions shown in the figure. If an axial force of P = 80 kN is applied to the bar, determine the change in its length and the change in the dimensions of its cross section after applying the load. The material behaves elastically. For A-36 steel take E_st = 200 GPa and ν_st = 0.32.","figs":["ex3-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular steel bar with axes x, y, z at its right end; z runs along the bar\u0027s length. Length 1.5 m. Cross-section 100 mm (along x) by 50 mm (along y). An axial force P = 80 kN pulls the bar at each end (arrow at the left end pointing away, arrow at the right end along +z).","sol":"1) Axial stress σ_z = P/A = 80(10³)/[(0.1)(0.05)] = 16.0(10⁶) Pa. 2) Axial strain ε_z = σ_z/E = 16.0(10⁶)/200(10⁹) = 80(10⁻⁶). 3) Elongation δ_z = ε_z L = 80(10⁻⁶)(1.5 m) = 120 μm. 4) Lateral strains from Poisson\u0027s ratio: ε_x = ε_y = −ν ε_z = −0.32(80)(10⁻⁶) = −25.6 μm/m. 5) δ_x = ε_x L_x = −25.6(10⁻⁶)(0.1 m) = −2.56 μm; δ_y = ε_y L_y = −25.6(10⁻⁶)(0.05 m) = −1.28 μm. Ans: δ_z = 120 μm, δ_x = −2.56 μm, δ_y = −1.28 μm.","answers":["δ_z = 120 μm","δ_x = −2.56 μm","δ_y = −1.28 μm"]},{"id":"3.5","title":"Titanium torsion test and sheared block: G, limits, displacement, force","q":"A specimen of titanium alloy is tested in torsion and the shear stress-strain diagram is shown in the figure. Determine the shear modulus G, the proportional limit, and the ultimate shear stress. Also, determine the maximum distance d that the top of a block of this material, shown in the figure, could be displaced horizontally if the material behaves elastically when acted upon by a shear force V. What is the magnitude of V necessary to cause this displacement?","figs":["ex3-5.webp","ex3-5b.webp"],"figDesc":"Panel (a): shear stress-strain diagram, τ (ksi) with ticks 10 to 90 versus γ (rad). A steep straight portion from the origin, then the curve flattens, reaches a maximum and falls, ending at γ = 0.73 (labelled on the strain axis, with a vertical line from the curve end). Plotted data points lie on the curve. No other values are labelled. Panel (b) (second file): rectangular block of the material, 2 in. high, with a top face 4 in. by 3 in.; a shear force V acts horizontally on the top face, shearing it through a horizontal distance d, with shear strain γ shown.","sol":"1) Shear modulus = slope of the straight portion OA; point A = (0.008 rad, 52 ksi): G = 52/0.008 = 6500 ksi (Hooke\u0027s law for shear: τ = 6500γ). 2) Proportional limit = stress where the graph stops being linear (point A): τ_pl = 52 ksi. 3) Ultimate shear stress = peak of the curve (point B): τ_u = 73 ksi. 4) Maximum elastic displacement: max elastic shear strain γ = 0.008 rad (very small angle), so tan(0.008) ≈ 0.008 = d/(2 in.) → d = 0.016 in. 5) At that point the average shear stress equals τ_pl = 52 ksi on the sheared area A = (3 in.)(4 in.): 52 = V/[(3)(4)] → V = 624 kip. Ans: G = 6500 ksi, τ_pl = 52 ksi, τ_u = 73 ksi, d = 0.016 in., V = 624 kip.","answers":["G = 6500 ksi","τ_pl = 52 ksi","τ_u = 73 ksi","d = 0.016 in.","V = 624 kip"]},{"id":"3.6","title":"Aluminum specimen in tension: modulus and diameter contraction","q":"An aluminum specimen shown in the figure has a diameter of d₀ = 25 mm and a gauge length of L₀ = 250 mm. If a force of 165 kN elongates the gauge length 1.20 mm, determine the modulus of elasticity. Also, determine by how much the force causes the diameter of the specimen to contract. Take G_al = 26 GPa and σ_Y = 440 MPa.","figs":["ex3-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical tension test specimen with threaded (gripped) ends, loaded by a 165 kN force pulling up at the top and a 165 kN force pulling down at the bottom. The central reduced section has diameter d₀, and the gauge length L₀ is marked between two points on it.","sol":"1) Average stress σ = P/A = 165(10³)/[(π/4)(0.025)²] = 336.1 MPa. 2) Average strain ε = δ/L = 1.20/250 = 0.00480 mm/mm. 3) σ \u003c σ_Y = 440 MPa, so the response is elastic: E_al = σ/ε = 336.1(10⁶)/0.00480 = 70.0 GPa. 4) Poisson\u0027s ratio from G = E/[2(1 + ν)]: 26 = 70.0/[2(1 + ν)] → ν = 0.347. 5) ν = −ε_lat/ε_long → ε_lat = −0.347(0.00480) = −0.00166 mm/mm. 6) Diameter change δ\u0027 = ε_lat d₀ = (0.00166)(25 mm) = 0.0416 mm (a contraction). Ans: E_al = 70.0 GPa, δ\u0027 = 0.0416 mm.","answers":["E_al = 70.0 GPa","δ\u0027 = 0.0416 mm (diameter contraction)"]},{"id":"4.1","title":"Steel rod through aluminum tube: displacement of end C","q":"The assembly shown in the figure consists of an aluminum tube AB having a cross-sectional area of 400 mm². A steel rod having a diameter of 10 mm is attached to a rigid collar and passes through the tube. If a tensile load of 80 kN is applied to the rod, determine the displacement of the end C of the rod. Take E_st = 200 GPa, E_al = 70 GPa.","figs":["ex4-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Tube AB (aluminum) is 400 mm long, with its end A against a fixed wall/flange on the right and its free end B (rigid collar with nut) on the left. The steel rod passes through the tube, is attached at the collar at B, and sticks out through the wall at the right; its end C is 600 mm from B. A horizontal 80 kN tensile load acts at C, pointing right (away from the wall).","sol":"1) Cut the rod and tube: the rod carries tension P_BC = 80 kN, the tube carries compression P_AB = 80 kN (the collar at B transfers the rod force into the tube). 2) Displacement of C relative to B (rod, L = 0.6 m, A = π(0.005)² m²): δ_C/B = PL/AE = (80e3)(0.6)/[π(0.005)²(200e9)] = +0.003056 m, to the right. 3) Displacement of B relative to fixed end A (tube, L = 0.4 m, A = 400e-6 m²): δ_B = (−80e3)(0.4)/[(400e-6)(70e9)] = −0.001143 m; the tube shortens, so B moves 0.001143 m to the right. 4) Both are to the right, so δ_C = δ_B + δ_C/B = 0.001143 + 0.003056 = 0.00420 m. Ans: δ_C = 4.20 mm (to the right).","answers":["δ_C = 4.20 mm (to the right)"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Rigid beam on steel and aluminum posts: displacement at F","q":"Rigid beam AB rests on the two short posts shown in the figure. AC is made of steel and has a diameter of 20 mm, and BD is made of aluminum and has a diameter of 40 mm. Determine the displacement of point F on AB if a vertical load of 90 kN is applied over this point. Take E_st = 200 GPa, E_al = 70 GPa.","figs":["ex4-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal rigid beam AB supported at its ends A (left) and B (right) by two vertical posts AC (left) and BD (right) standing on the ground at C and D. Each post is 300 mm tall. A 90 kN downward load acts at point F on the beam, 200 mm from A and 400 mm from B (AB = 600 mm).","sol":"1) Equilibrium of beam AB: F is 200 mm from A and 400 mm from B, so P_AC = 90(400/600) = 60 kN and P_BD = 90(200/600) = 30 kN (both posts in compression). 2) Post AC (steel, d = 20 mm, L = 0.300 m): δ_A = PL/AE = (−60e3)(0.3)/[π(0.010)²(200e9)] = −286e-6 m = 0.286 mm downward. 3) Post BD (aluminum, d = 40 mm): δ_B = (−30e3)(0.3)/[π(0.020)²(70e9)] = −102e-6 m = 0.102 mm downward. 4) Beam is rigid, so it stays straight: displacement varies linearly from 0.286 mm at A to 0.102 mm at B. By similar triangles, δ_F = 0.102 + (0.184)(400/600) = 0.225 mm. Ans: δ_F = 0.225 mm downward.","answers":["δ_F = 0.225 mm (downward)"]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Cone hanging under its own weight: end displacement","q":"A member is made from a material that has a specific weight γ and modulus of elasticity E. If it is in the form of a cone having the dimensions shown in the figure (base radius r₀ at the top, height L), determine how far its end is displaced due to gravity when it is suspended in the vertical position.","figs":["ex4-3.webp"],"figDesc":"A solid circular cone hanging vertically with its base (radius r₀) at the top and its apex at the bottom. Height of the cone is L. A vertical y-axis passes through the centre of the base (origin at the base) and an x-axis is drawn horizontally at the apex level.","sol":"1) The axial force varies with position, so integrate: δ = ∫P(y)dy/(A(y)E). Measure y from the free (apex) end upward. 2) Radius at y by proportion: x = (r₀/L)y. Volume of cone segment of height y: V = (1/3)πx²y = πr₀²y³/(3L²). Internal force (weight of the segment below the section): P(y) = γV = γπr₀²y³/(3L²). 3) Area: A(y) = πx² = πr₀²y²/L². 4) δ = ∫₀ᴸ [γπr₀²y³/(3L²)] / [(πr₀²y²/L²)E] dy = (γ/3E)∫₀ᴸ y dy = γL²/(6E). Ans: δ = γL²/(6E), the apex moves down relative to the support. Units check: length.","answers":["δ = γL²/(6E)"]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Steel rod with 0.2 mm gap: wall reactions at A and B\u0027","q":"The steel rod shown in the figure has a diameter of 10 mm. It is fixed to the wall at A, and before it is loaded, there is a gap of 0.2 mm between the wall at B\u0027 and the rod. Determine the reactions at A and B\u0027 if the rod is subjected to an axial force of P = 20 kN as shown. Neglect the size of the collar at C. Take E_st = 200 GPa.","figs":["ex4-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal steel rod fixed to a wall at A (left). A collar at C, 400 mm from A, carries an axial force P = 20 kN pointing right (toward B). The free end B of the rod is 800 mm from C, with a 0.2 mm gap between B and the opposite wall at B\u0027 (right).","sol":"1) Assume P is large enough to close the gap so the rod touches B\u0027. Two unknown reactions, one equation: statics ΣFx = 0 (+ right): −F_A − F_B + 20(10³) = 0 (1). 2) Compatibility: end B moves only 0.0002 m relative to A: δ_B/A = 0.0002 m. 3) Load-displacement: segment AC carries F_A (tension), segment CB carries F_B (compression): 0.0002 = F_A(0.4)/(AE) − F_B(0.8)/(AE), with AE = π(0.005)²(200e9) = 15.708e6 N, giving 0.4F_A − 0.8F_B = 3141.59 N·m (2). 4) Solve (1) and (2): F_A = 16.0 kN, F_B = 4.05 kN. Since F_B \u003e 0 the contact assumption holds (if F_B had come out negative, F_B = 0 and F_A = 20 kN). Ans: F_A = 16.0 kN, F_B = 4.05 kN.","answers":["F_A = 16.0 kN","F_B = 4.05 kN"]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Aluminum post with brass core: normal stresses under load","q":"The aluminum post shown in the figure is reinforced with a brass core. If this assembly supports an axial compressive load of P = 9 kip, applied to the rigid cap, determine the average normal stress in the aluminum and the brass. Take E_al = 10(10³) ksi and E_br = 15(10³) ksi.","figs":["ex4-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical cylindrical post, 1.5 ft tall, of aluminum with a concentric brass core. The brass core has radius 1 in. and the aluminum outer radius is 2 in. (dimensions 2 in. and 1 in. are radii labelled on the top face). A rigid cap on top carries a downward axial load P = 9 kip.","sol":"1) Statically indeterminate: both materials carry the load. Equilibrium: F_al + F_br = 9 kip (1). 2) Compatibility: the rigid cap forces equal shortening, δ_al = δ_br. 3) With equal length L: F_al L/(A_al E_al) = F_br L/(A_br E_br), so F_al = F_br (A_al/A_br)(E_al/E_br). A_al = π(2² − 1²) = 3π in², A_br = π(1)² = π in². F_al = F_br (3)(10/15) = 2F_br (2). 4) Solve: F_al = 6 kip, F_br = 3 kip (both compression). 5) σ_al = 6/[π(2² − 1²)] = 0.637 ksi; σ_br = 3/[π(1²)] = 0.955 ksi. Ans: σ_al = 0.637 ksi, σ_br = 0.955 ksi (both compressive).","answers":["σ_al = 0.637 ksi (compression)","σ_br = 0.955 ksi (compression)"]},{"id":"4.6","title":"Three steel bars supporting a rigid member: bar forces","q":"The three A992 steel bars shown in the figure are pin connected to a rigid member. If the applied load on the member is 15 kN, determine the force developed in each bar. Bars AB and EF each have a cross-sectional area of 50 mm², and bar CD has a cross-sectional area of 30 mm². (Same E for all bars; bars are 0.5 m long.)","figs":["ex4-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Three vertical bars AB, CD and EF hang from the ceiling at B, D, F (top) and are pinned at A, C, E (bottom) to a horizontal rigid member. Bar length is 0.5 m. A, C, E are spaced left to right: A to C is 0.4 m, C to E is 0.4 m. A 15 kN downward load is applied through a chain hook to the rigid member 0.2 m from A and 0.2 m from C (midway between A and C).","sol":"1) Unknowns F_A, F_C, F_E (tension), two equilibrium equations, so one compatibility equation is needed. 2) ΣF_y = 0: F_A + F_C + F_E − 15 = 0 (1). ΣM_C = 0 (counterclockwise +): −F_A(0.4) + 15(0.2) + F_E(0.4) = 0 (2). 3) Compatibility: the rigid member stays straight, so by similar triangles δ_C = ½δ_A + ½δ_E. 4) Load-displacement with δ = FL/(AE), same L and E: F_C/30 = ½(F_A/50) + ½(F_E/50), so F_C = 0.3F_A + 0.3F_E (3). 5) Solving (1)-(3): F_A = 9.52 kN, F_C = 3.46 kN, F_E = 2.02 kN. Ans: F_A = 9.52 kN, F_C = 3.46 kN, F_E = 2.02 kN.","answers":["F_A = 9.52 kN","F_C = 3.46 kN","F_E = 2.02 kN"]},{"id":"4.7","title":"Bolt tightened against magnesium tube: stress in the bolt","q":"The bolt shown in the figure is made of 2014-T6 aluminum alloy and is tightened so it compresses a cylindrical tube made of Am 1004-T61 magnesium alloy. The tube has an outer radius of 1/2 in., and it is assumed that both the inner radius of the tube and the radius of the bolt are 1/4 in. The washers at the top and bottom of the tube are considered to be rigid and have a negligible thickness. Initially the nut is hand tightened snugly; then, using a wrench, the nut is further tightened one-half turn. If the bolt has 20 threads per inch, determine the stress in the bolt. Take E_al = 10.6(10³) ksi, E_mg = 6.48(10³) ksi; yield stresses (σ_Y)_al = 60 ksi and (σ_Y)_mg = 22 ksi.","figs":["ex4-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical bolt passing through a cylindrical tube, with rigid washers at the top and bottom and a nut at the bottom. The tube length between washers is 3 in. Tube outer radius is 1/2 in. and bolt radius (also tube inner radius) is 1/4 in.","sol":"1) Equilibrium of a cut section: bolt tension equals tube compression, F_b = F_t (1). 2) Compatibility: the nut advance of ½ turn × (1/20 in. per turn) = 0.025 in. is split between bolt stretch and tube shortening: δ_t = 0.025 − δ_b. 3) Load-displacement: F_t(3)/{π[(0.5)² − (0.25)²](6.48e3)} = 0.025 − F_b(3)/{π(0.25)²(10.6e3)}, giving 0.78595F_t = 25 − 1.4414F_b (2) (F in kip, displacements in units of 10⁻³ in.). 4) Solve with F_b = F_t: F_b = F_t = 11.22 kip. 5) σ_b = 11.22/[π(0.25)²] = 57.2 ksi; σ_t = 11.22/{π[(0.5)² − (0.25)²]} = 19.1 ksi. Both are below the yield stresses (60 ksi and 22 ksi), so the elastic analysis is valid. Ans: σ_b = 57.2 ksi (tube stress 19.1 ksi).","answers":["σ_b = 57.2 ksi","σ_t = 19.1 ksi"]},{"id":"4.8","title":"Steel rod with gap at wall: reactions by force method","q":"The A-36 steel rod shown in the figure has a diameter of 10 mm. It is fixed to the wall at A, and before it is loaded there is a gap between the wall at B\u0027 and the rod of 0.2 mm. Determine the reactions at A and B\u0027. Neglect the size of the collar at C. Take E_st = 200 GPa. (Solve using the force method: treat the support at B\u0027 as the redundant and use superposition.)","figs":["ex4-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal steel rod fixed to a wall at A (left). A collar at C, 400 mm from A, carries an axial force P = 20 kN pointing right. The free end of the rod is 800 mm to the right of C, with a 0.2 mm gap to the wall at B\u0027.","sol":"1) Remove the redundant support at B\u0027 and apply superposition: the load P alone moves the end to the right by δ_P; the redundant force F_B pushes it back by δ_B. Compatibility: 0.0002 = δ_P − δ_B (1). 2) δ_P = P L_AC/(AE) = 20e3(0.4)/[π(0.005)²(200e9)] = 0.5093e-3 m. 3) δ_B = F_B L_AB/(AE) = F_B(1.20)/[π(0.005)²(200e9)] = 76.3944e-9 F_B. 4) 0.0002 = 0.5093e-3 − 76.3944e-9 F_B gives F_B = 4.05e3 N = 4.05 kN. 5) Equilibrium: −F_A + 20 − 4.05 = 0, so F_A = 16.0 kN. Ans: F_B = 4.05 kN, F_A = 16.0 kN (same as the compatibility/displacement solution of the same problem).","answers":["F_B = 4.05 kN","F_A = 16.0 kN"]},{"id":"4.9","title":"Steel bar between fixed supports heated: thermal stress","q":"The A-36 steel bar shown in the figure is constrained to just fit between two fixed supports when T₁ = 60°F. If the temperature is raised to T₂ = 120°F, determine the average normal thermal stress developed in the bar. Take α = 6.60(10⁻⁶)/°F and E = 29(10³) ksi.","figs":["ex4-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical bar between two fixed supports, A at the top and B at the bottom, length 2 ft. The cross-section is a 0.5 in. by 0.5 in. square (shown as a small detail above the bar).","sol":"1) With no external load, F_A = F_B = F; the problem is indeterminate since F cannot be found from statics. 2) Compatibility: total end displacement is zero: 0 = δ_T − δ_F. 3) Load-displacement: 0 = αΔT·L − FL/(AE), so F = αΔT·A·E (L cancels). 4) F = [6.60e-6/°F](120 − 60)°F (0.5 in.)²[29e3 ksi] = 2.871 kip. 5) σ = F/A = 2.871/(0.5)² = 11.5 ksi, compressive (the heated bar tries to expand but is held). Ans: σ = 11.5 ksi (compression).","answers":["σ = 11.5 ksi (compression)"]},{"id":"4.10","title":"Rigid beam on three heated posts: force in each post","q":"The rigid beam shown in the figure is fixed to the top of the three posts made of A992 steel and 2014-T6 aluminum. The posts each have a length of 250 mm when no load is applied to the beam, and the temperature is T₁ = 20°C. Determine the force supported by each post if the bar is subjected to a uniform distributed load of 150 kN/m and the temperature is raised to T₂ = 80°C. The steel posts have diameter 40 mm and the aluminum post (middle) has diameter 60 mm. Take E_st = 200 GPa, α_st = 12(10⁻⁶)/°C, E_al = 73.1 GPa, α_al = 23(10⁻⁶)/°C.","figs":["ex4-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Rigid horizontal beam supported by three vertical posts standing on the ground: steel (left), aluminum (middle), steel (right), each 250 mm tall. A uniform downward load of 150 kN/m acts over the full beam length, shown as two 300 mm spans (600 mm total). Diameter labels: 40 mm for the steel posts and 60 mm for the aluminum post.","sol":"1) Total load = 150 kN/m × 0.6 m = 90 kN. By symmetry the two steel posts carry equal force: 2F_st + F_al − 90(10³) = 0 (1) (compression positive). 2) Compatibility: the rigid beam moves down equally at every post: δ_st = δ_al (2). 3) Each post: δ = −(δ)_T + (δ)_F (thermal expansion raises the top, force shortens). So −αΔT·L + FL/(AE) for each: −12e-6(60)(0.25) + F_st(0.25)/[π(0.020)²(200e9)] = −23e-6(60)(0.25) + F_al(0.25)/[π(0.030)²(73.1e9)]. 4) This gives F_st = 1.216F_al − 165.9(10³) N (3). 5) Solving (1) and (3): F_st = −16.4 kN, F_al = 123 kN. The negative sign means the steel posts are in tension and the aluminum post in compression. Ans: F_st = −16.4 kN (each steel post, tension), F_al = 123 kN (compression).","answers":["F_st = −16.4 kN (each steel post, in tension)","F_al = 123 kN (compression)"]},{"id":"4.11","title":"Aluminum sleeve on steel bolt heated: bolt and sleeve force","q":"A 2014-T6 aluminum tube having a cross-sectional area of 600 mm² is used as a sleeve for an A-36 steel bolt having a cross-sectional area of 400 mm². When the temperature is T₁ = 15°C, the nut holds the assembly in a snug position such that the axial force in the bolt is negligible. If the temperature increases to T₂ = 80°C, determine the force in the bolt and sleeve. The assembly length is 150 mm. Take E_st = 200 GPa, α_st = 12(10⁻⁶)/°C, E_al = 73.1 GPa, α_al = 23(10⁻⁶)/°C.","figs":["ex4-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical bolt with a hex head at the top and a nut at the bottom, passing through a tube (sleeve) with washers at both ends. The sleeve length between the washers is 150 mm.","sol":"1) Equilibrium of a cut segment: sleeve and bolt forces are equal, F_s = F_b (1). The sleeve (aluminum) has the larger α, so it tries to expand more than the bolt: the bolt is stretched and the sleeve compressed. 2) Compatibility: final end position is common: δ = (δ_b)_T + (δ_b)_F = (δ_s)_T − (δ_s)_F. 3) Thermal terms: steel 12e-6(65)(0.150); aluminum 23e-6(65)(0.150), ΔT = 65°C. 4) 12e-6(65)(0.15) + F_b(0.15)/[(400e-6)(200e9)] = 23e-6(65)(0.15) − F_s(0.15)/[(600e-6)(73.1e9)]. 5) With F_s = F_b this gives F_s = F_b = 20.3 kN. Ans: F_s = F_b = 20.3 kN (bolt in tension, sleeve in compression). The stresses should be checked against the proportional limit.","answers":["F_s = F_b = 20.3 kN"]},{"id":"4.12","title":"Elastic-plastic notched steel bar: yield and plastic loads","q":"The bar shown in the figure is made of steel that is assumed to be elastic perfectly plastic, with σ_Y = 250 MPa. Determine (a) the maximum value of the applied load P that can be applied without causing the steel to yield and (b) the maximum value of P that the bar can support. Sketch the stress distribution at the critical section for each case. (Bar is 2 mm thick and 40 mm wide at its wide ends, with 4 mm deep notches/fillets of radius 4 mm on each side, leaving a 32 mm wide minimum section. Use the stress-concentration chart for a bar with shoulder fillets: r/h = 0.125 and w/h = 1.25 give K ≈ 1.75, where h = 40 − 8 = 32 mm and w = 40 mm.)","figs":["ex4-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Flat steel bar with a reduced central section, loaded by axial force P at both ends (pulling outward). The bar is 40 mm wide at the large ends and thickness 2 mm; fillet radius labelled 4 mm at both shoulders. The narrowed section is 8 mm narrower in total (4 mm cut on each edge).","sol":"1) (a) Elastic case: use σ_max = K σ_avg with K from the chart. r/h = 4/(40−8) = 0.125, w/h = 40/(40−8) = 1.25, so K ≈ 1.75. 2) Smallest cross-section A = (0.002 m)(0.032 m). Yielding begins when σ_max = σ_Y: 250e6 = 1.75 [P_Y/((0.002)(0.032))], giving P_Y = 9.14 kN. The stress distribution is nonuniform with peak σ_Y at the fillet. 3) (b) Plastic case: the maximum load makes the whole smallest cross-section yield: σ_Y = P_p/A, so P_p = 250e6 (0.002)(0.032) = 16.0 kN. The stress distribution is uniform at σ_Y over the minimum section (P_p equals the volume of the stress distribution σ_Y·A). Ans: P_Y = 9.14 kN, P_p = 16.0 kN.","answers":["P_Y = 9.14 kN","P_p = 16.0 kN"]},{"id":"4.13","title":"Rod fixed at both ends loaded then unloaded: residual stress","q":"The rod shown in the figure has a radius of 5 mm and is made of an elastic perfectly plastic material for which σ_Y = 420 MPa and E = 70 GPa (stress-strain diagram is elastic up to σ_Y then flat). If a force of P = 60 kN is applied to the rod and then removed, determine the residual stress in the rod.","figs":["ex4-13.webp"],"figDesc":"Straight rod fixed at both ends, A at the left and B at the right. A collar at C carries an axial force P = 60 kN pointing left (toward A). Segment AC is 100 mm long; segment CB is 300 mm long (total length 400 mm).","sol":"1) Elastic analysis (indeterminate): F_A = 60(300/400) = 45 kN, F_B = 15 kN. σ_AC = 45e3/[π(0.005)²] = 573 MPa (compression) \u003e σ_Y = 420 MPa, so AC yields; σ_CB = 191 MPa (tension). Assume AC plastic, CB elastic. 2) Max force in AC: (F_A)_Y = σ_Y A = 420e6 π(0.005)² = 33.0 kN. Equilibrium: F_B = 60 − 33.0 = 27.0 kN. σ_AC = 420 MPa (compression), σ_CB = 27.0e3/[π(0.005)²] = 344 MPa (tension) \u003c 420 (OK). 3) Strains: δ_C = F_B L_CB/(AE) = 27.0(0.300)/[π(0.005)²(70e6 kN/m²)] = 0.001474 m; ε_CB = +0.004913, ε_AC = −0.01474 (yield strain 0.006). 4) Unloading is elastic with the reverse of the elastic-analysis forces (σ_AC = 573 MPa tension, σ_CB = 191 MPa compression). 5) Residual: (σ_AC)_r = −420 + 573 = 153 MPa; (σ_CB)_r = 344 − 191 = 153 MPa. Ans: residual stress 153 MPa (tension) in both segments.","answers":["(σ_AC)_r = 153 MPa","(σ_CB)_r = 153 MPa"]},{"id":"4.14","title":"Two steel wires of unequal length lifting a weight","q":"Two steel wires are used to lift the weight of 3 kip. Wire AB has an unstretched length of 20.00 ft and wire AC has an unstretched length of 20.03 ft. If each wire has a cross-sectional area of 0.05 in², and the steel can be considered elastic perfectly plastic as shown by the σ-ε graph (yield stress 50 ksi at a strain of 0.0017, then constant stress), determine the force in each wire and its elongation.","figs":["ex4-14.webp","ex4-14b.webp"],"figDesc":"Panel (a): a crane hook at A; two wires hang from it, AB (20.00 ft, straight, shorter) and AC (20.03 ft, slightly slack, longer). Both are attached at their lower ends B and C to a ring on top of a heavy cone-shaped weight resting on the ground (weight 3 kip). Panel (b): σ-ε diagram of the steel: straight elastic line from the origin to σ = 50 ksi at ε = 0.0017, then horizontal (perfectly plastic) at 50 ksi.","sol":"1) Both wires only carry load once the shorter wire AB has stretched 0.03 ft. Statically indeterminate: T_AB + T_AC − 3 kip = 0 (1). 2) Assume AB yields (plastic) and AC remains elastic. Then T_AB = σ_Y A = 50 ksi (0.05 in²) = 2.50 kip. 3) From (1): T_AC = 3 − 2.50 = 0.500 kip. 4) Check AC: σ_AC = 0.500/0.05 = 10 ksi \u003c 50 ksi, so it is elastic as assumed. 5) Strain in AC by proportion on the elastic line: ε_AC/0.0017 = 10/50, ε_AC = 0.000340. δ_AC = 0.000340(20.03 ft) = 0.00681 ft. 6) Compatibility: δ_AB = 0.03 ft + δ_AC = 0.03 + 0.00681 = 0.0368 ft. Ans: T_AB = 2.50 kip, T_AC = 0.500 kip, δ_AC = 0.00681 ft, δ_AB = 0.0368 ft.","answers":["T_AB = 2.50 kip","T_AC = 0.500 kip","δ_AC = 0.00681 ft","δ_AB = 0.0368 ft"]},{"id":"5.1","title":"Solid shaft: fraction of torque carried by outer core","q":"The solid shaft of radius c is subjected to a torque T, as shown in the figure. Determine the fraction of T that is resisted by the material contained within the outer core of the shaft, which has an inner radius of c/2 and outer radius c.","figs":["ex5-1.webp"],"figDesc":"A solid circular shaft of radius c loaded by a torque T at its end (curved arrow around the axis). An inner circle of radius c/2 is marked on the end face; the annulus between c/2 and c is the outer core.","sol":"1) Shear stress varies linearly with radius: τ = (ρ/c)τ_max. 2) Torque on a thin ring of radius ρ and width dρ: dT\u0027 = ρ(τ dA) = ρ(ρ/c)τ_max(2πρ dρ). 3) Integrate over the outer core: T\u0027 = (2πτ_max/c)∫ρ³dρ from c/2 to c = (2πτ_max/c)(1/4)[c⁴ − (c/2)⁴] = (15π/32)τ_max c³. 4) Torsion formula for the whole shaft: τ_max = Tc/J = Tc/[(π/2)c⁴] = 2T/(πc³). 5) Substitute: T\u0027 = (15π/32)(2T/(πc³))c³ = (15/16)T. Ans: T\u0027 = (15/16)T, about 94% of the torque, so the inner core (ρ \u003c c/2) carries only 1/16, which is why hollow shafts are efficient.","answers":["T\u0027 = (15/16) T (about 94% of T)"]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Shaft with three torques: shear stress at two points","q":"The shaft shown in the figure is supported by two bearings and is subjected to three torques: 42.5 kip·in. at the left wheel, 30 kip·in. at the middle wheel and 12.5 kip·in. at the right wheel (directions as shown). Determine the shear stress developed at points A and B, located at section a-a of the shaft. The shaft has a radius of 0.75 in.; point A is on the outer surface and point B is 0.15 in. from the shaft axis.","figs":["ex5-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal shaft supported by a bearing at each end, carrying three wheels with torques 42.5 kip·in. (left wheel), 30 kip·in. (middle wheel) and 12.5 kip·in. (right wheel). Section a-a is cut between the middle wheel and the right wheel. The figure itself does not show the shaft radius or the positions of A and B (given in the problem text).","sol":"1) The bearings offer no torsional resistance, so the applied torques are in equilibrium about the shaft axis. 2) Internal torque at section a-a from the left segment: ΣM_x = 0: 42.5 − 30 − T = 0, so T = 12.5 kip·in. (equals the right-hand torque). 3) J = (π/2)c⁴ = (π/2)(0.75)⁴ = 0.497 in⁴. 4) Point A is on the surface, ρ = c = 0.75 in.: τ_A = Tc/J = 12.5(0.75)/0.497 = 18.9 ksi. 5) Point B at ρ = 0.15 in.: τ_B = Tρ/J = 12.5(0.15)/0.497 = 3.77 ksi. The direction of the stresses follows the sense of the internal torque. Ans: τ_A = 18.9 ksi, τ_B = 3.77 ksi.","answers":["τ_A = 18.9 ksi","τ_B = 3.77 ksi"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Pipe tightened with torque wrench: inner and outer wall shear","q":"The pipe shown in the figure has an inner diameter of 80 mm and an outer diameter of 100 mm. If its end is tightened against the support at A using a torque wrench at B, determine the shear stress developed in the material at the inner and outer walls along the central portion of the pipe when the 80-N forces are applied to the wrench.","figs":["ex5-3.webp"],"figDesc":"A pipe fixed to a wall at A (upper right) and extending toward the viewer to B, where a wrench is fitted perpendicular to the pipe axis. Two hands apply 80 N forces on the wrench in opposite directions: one upward at 200 mm from the pipe axis on one side and one downward at 300 mm on the other side. A section plane C is indicated along the pipe.","sol":"1) Cut the pipe at an intermediate section C; the internal torque equals the moment of the wrench forces about the pipe axis. 2) ΣM_y = 0: 80(0.3) + 80(0.2) − T = 0, so T = 40 N·m. 3) J = (π/2)(c_o⁴ − c_i⁴) = (π/2)[(0.05)⁴ − (0.04)⁴] = 5.796e-6 m⁴. 4) Outer wall, ρ = 0.05 m: τ_o = Tc_o/J = 40(0.05)/5.796e-6 = 0.345 MPa. 5) Inner wall, ρ = 0.04 m: τ_i = Tc_i/J = 40(0.04)/5.796e-6 = 0.276 MPa. Ans: τ_o = 0.345 MPa, τ_i = 0.276 MPa.","answers":["τ_outer = 0.345 MPa","τ_inner = 0.276 MPa"]},{"id":"5.4","title":"Motor-driven steel shaft: required diameter for 5 hp","q":"A solid steel shaft AB, shown in the figure, is to be used to transmit 5 hp from the motor M to which it is attached. If the shaft rotates at ω = 175 rpm and the steel has an allowable shear stress of τ_allow = 14.5 ksi, determine the required diameter of the shaft to the nearest 1/8 in.","figs":["ex5-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Electric motor M at upper right, with the shaft AB leaving the motor at A and running to a pillow-block bearing at B (lower left). A belt pulley sits on the shaft near B. The rotation ω is indicated by a curved arrow around the shaft near B.","sol":"1) Power-torque relation P = Tω. Convert: P = 5 hp (550 ft·lb/s per hp) = 2750 ft·lb/s; ω = 175 rev/min (2π rad/rev)(1 min/60 s) = 18.33 rad/s. 2) T = P/ω = 2750/18.33 = 150.1 ft·lb. 3) Design for allowable stress: τ_allow = Tc/J = T/[(π/2)c³], so c = [2T/(πτ_allow)]^(1/3) = [2(150.1)(12)/(π·14 500)]^(1/3) = 0.429 in. (14.5 ksi = 14 500 psi, T in lb·in.). 4) Diameter 2c = 0.858 in., round up to the next 1/8 in.: d = 7/8 in. Ans: d = 7/8 in. = 0.875 in.","answers":["d = 7/8 in. (0.875 in.)"]},{"id":"5.5","title":"Geared shaft fixed at one end: displacement of gear tooth","q":"The gears attached to the fixed-end steel shaft are subjected to the torques shown in the figure: 150 N·m at gear A, 280 N·m at gear C and 40 N·m at gear D (directions as shown). If the shear modulus of elasticity is 80 GPa and the shaft has a diameter of 14 mm, determine the displacement of the tooth P on gear A. The shaft turns freely within the bearing at B. Segment lengths: AC = 0.4 m, CD = 0.3 m, DE = 0.5 m (E is the fixed end); gear A has a radius of 100 mm to tooth P.","figs":["ex5-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Shaft with four gears/points A, C, D along it and a fixed end E (upper right). Bearing B lies between A and C. Torques: 150 N·m at A (end gear, tooth P on its rim at radius 100 mm), 280 N·m at C, 40 N·m at D. Distances: A to C 0.4 m, C to D 0.3 m, D to E 0.5 m.","sol":"1) Internal torques by sections (positive torque pointing away from the cut end, right-hand rule): T_AC = +150 N·m; T_CD = 150 − 280 = −130 N·m; T_DE = 150 − 280 − 40 = −170 N·m. 2) J = (π/2)(0.007)⁴ = 3.771e-9 m⁴; G = 80 GPa. 3) Angle of twist of A relative to the fixed end E: φ_A = Σ TL/(JG) = [(+150)(0.4) + (−130)(0.3) + (−170)(0.5)] / [3.771e-9 × 80e9] = (60 − 39 − 85)/301.7 = −0.2121 rad. 4) The negative sign means the end A rotates opposite to the positive (right-hand-rule) sense, i.e. by the right-hand rule the thumb points toward E. 5) Tooth displacement: s_P = φ_A r = 0.2121(100 mm) = 21.2 mm. Valid only if the shear stress stays below the proportional limit. Ans: s_P = 21.2 mm (φ_A = 0.212 rad).","answers":["φ_A = 0.2121 rad (negative sign: opposite to positive sense)","s_P = 21.2 mm"]},{"id":"5.6","title":"Two meshed gear shafts: angle of twist of end A","q":"The two solid steel shafts shown in the figure are coupled together using the meshed gears. Determine the angle of twist of end A of shaft AB when the torque T = 45 N·m is applied. Take G = 80 GPa. Shaft AB is free to rotate within bearings E and F, whereas shaft DC is fixed at D. Each shaft has a diameter of 20 mm. Ignore lateral flexing of the shafts. Shaft AB is 2 m long (A to gear B, gear B radius 150 mm); shaft DC is 1.5 m long (D to gear C, gear C radius 75 mm).","figs":["ex5-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Two parallel shafts coupled by meshing gears. Lower shaft AB: torque T = 45 N·m applied at free end A (left), supported by bearings E and F, ending at gear B (radius 150 mm); length AB = 2 m. Upper shaft DC: fixed at D (left), ends at gear C (radius 75 mm) meshing with gear B; length DC = 1.5 m.","sol":"1) Tangential gear force from shaft AB equilibrium: F = T/r_B = 45/0.15 = 300 N. 2) This force on gear C gives shaft DC a torque (T_D)_x = 300(0.075) = 22.5 N·m. 3) Twist of gear C: φ_C = TL/(JG) = 22.5(1.5)/[(π/2)(0.010)⁴(80e9)] = 0.0269 rad. 4) Meshed gears: φ_B r_B = φ_C r_C so φ_B = 0.0269(0.075/0.15) = 0.0134 rad (rigid-body rotation of AB due to gear B). 5) Twist of A relative to B in shaft AB: φ_A/B = (45)(2)/[(π/2)(0.010)⁴(80e9)] = 0.0716 rad. 6) Both rotations are in the same sense, so add: φ_A = φ_B + φ_A/B = 0.0134 + 0.0716 = 0.0850 rad. Ans: φ_A = 0.0850 rad.","answers":["φ_A = 0.0850 rad"]},{"id":"5.7","title":"Post twisted by wrench with soil friction: max stress, twist","q":"The 2-in.-diameter solid cast-iron post shown in the figure is buried 24 in. in soil. If a torque is applied to its top using a rigid wrench (two 25 lb forces at 6 in. on each side of the post axis, i.e. a 12 in. arm), determine the maximum shear stress in the post and the angle of twist at its top. Assume that the torque is about to turn the post, and the soil exerts a uniform torsional resistance of t lb·in./in. along its 24-in. buried length. G = 5.5(10³) ksi. The exposed length is 36 in.","figs":["ex5-7.webp"],"figDesc":"A vertical post with a rigid wrench at its top A; the wrench carries a 25 lb force at each end, 6 in. each side of the axis, acting in opposite directions. Post diameter 2 in. The upper 36 in. (A to B) is above ground; the lower 24 in. (B to C) is buried in soil, with a distributed torque t per unit length resisting along BC.","sol":"1) Internal torque in exposed segment AB is constant: ΣM_z = 0: T_AB = 25(12) = 300 lb·in. 2) Soil resistance: equilibrium of the whole post: 25(12) − t(24) = 0, t = 12.5 lb·in./in. 3) Section in the buried part at distance x from C: T_BC = 12.5x (varies linearly from 0 at C to 300 at B). 4) Max shear stress occurs in AB (largest torque, J constant): τ_max = T_AB c/J = 300(1)/[(π/2)(1)⁴] = 191 psi. 5) Angle of twist at top relative to the bottom C: φ_A = T_AB L_AB/(JG) + ∫₀²⁴ T_BC dx/(JG) = 300(36)/(JG) + 12.5(24)²/2/(JG) = (10 800 + 3600)/(JG) = 14 400 lb·in²/[(π/2)(1)⁴(5500e3)] = 0.00167 rad. Ans: τ_max = 191 psi, φ_A = 0.00167 rad.","answers":["τ_max = 191 psi","φ_A = 0.00167 rad"]},{"id":"5.8","title":"Steel shaft fixed at both ends: end torque reactions","q":"The solid steel shaft shown in the figure has a diameter of 20 mm. If it is subjected to the two torques, determine the reactions at the fixed supports A and B. The 800 N·m torque acts at C and the 500 N·m torque acts at D.","figs":["ex5-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Straight shaft fixed at B (lower left) and at A (upper right). An 800 N·m torque acts at C, 0.2 m from B. A 500 N·m torque (opposite sense) acts at D, 0.3 m from A. The distance between C and D is 1.5 m.","sol":"1) Equilibrium about the shaft axis (one equation, two unknown reactions T_A, T_B): −T_B + 800 − 500 − T_A = 0 (1). 2) Compatibility: both ends fixed, so φ_A/B = 0. 3) Write the internal torque in each of the three regions as a function of T_B (sections taken from end B, sign convention positive away from cut): region BC (0.2 m): −T_B; region CD (1.5 m): 800 − T_B; region DA (0.3 m): 300 − T_B. 4) Load-displacement: [−T_B(0.2) + (800 − T_B)(1.5) + (300 − T_B)(0.3)]/(JG) = 0, giving 1290 = 2T_B, T_B = 645 N·m. 5) From Eq. 1: T_A = −645 + 800 − 500 = −345 N·m (negative: T_A acts opposite to the direction assumed in the free-body diagram). Ans: T_B = 645 N·m, T_A = −345 N·m.","answers":["T_B = 645 N·m","T_A = −345 N·m (opposite to assumed direction)"]},{"id":"5.9","title":"Steel tube bonded to brass core: shear stress distribution","q":"The shaft shown in the figure is made from a steel tube, which is bonded to a brass core. If a torque of T = 250 lb·ft is applied at its end, plot the shear-stress distribution along a radial line of its cross-sectional area. Take G_st = 11.4(10³) ksi, G_br = 5.20(10³) ksi. The steel tube has outer radius 1 in. and inner radius 0.5 in.; the brass core has radius 0.5 in.; the shaft is 4 ft long, fixed at B and loaded at A.","figs":["ex5-9.webp"],"figDesc":"A circular composite shaft 4 ft long, fixed to a wall at B (upper right), with a torque T = 250 lb·ft applied at the free end A (lower left). The end face shows a brass core of radius 0.5 in. inside a steel tube of outer radius 1 in. (radius dimensions 0.5 in. and 1 in. are labelled at end A).","sol":"1) Equilibrium: the wall resists the torque with steel T_st and brass T_br: −T_st − T_br + 250(12) = 0 (1) (lb·in.). 2) Compatibility: both materials twist the same angle, φ_st = φ_br. 3) Load-displacement (same L): T_st/{(π/2)[(1)⁴ − (0.5)⁴](11.4e3)} = T_br/{(π/2)(0.5)⁴(5.20e3)}, so T_st = 32.88 T_br (2). 4) Solve: T_st = 2911.5 lb·in. (242.6 lb·ft), T_br = 88.5 lb·in. (7.38 lb·ft). 5) Brass max (at r = 0.5 in.): τ_br,max = 88.5(0.5)/[(π/2)(0.5)⁴] = 451 psi (zero at center). 6) Steel: τ_st,min (at r = 0.5 in.) = 2911.5(0.5)/[(π/2)(1⁴ − 0.5⁴)] = 989 psi; τ_st,max (at r = 1 in.) = 2911.5(1)/[(π/2)(1⁴ − 0.5⁴)] = 1977 psi. Distribution is linear in each material with a jump at the interface (451 to 989 psi), since G_st \u003e G_br while shear strain is continuous. Ans: 451 psi, 989 psi, 1977 psi.","answers":["(τ_br)max = 451 psi","(τ_st)min = 989 psi","(τ_st)max = 1977 psi","T_st = 2911.5 lb·in., T_br = 88.5 lb·in."]},{"id":"5.10","title":"Triangular aluminum shaft: largest torque vs circular shaft","q":"The 6061-T6 aluminum shaft shown in the figure has a cross-sectional area in the shape of an equilateral triangle (side a = 1.5 in.). The shaft is 4 ft long. Determine the largest torque T that can be applied to the end of the shaft if the allowable shear stress is τ_allow = 8 ksi and the angle of twist at its end is restricted to φ_allow = 0.02 rad. How much torque can be applied to a shaft of circular cross section made from the same amount of material? Take G_al = 3.7(10⁶) psi. For an equilateral triangle section, use τ_max = 20T/a³ and φ = 46TL/(a⁴G).","figs":["ex5-10.webp"],"figDesc":"A prismatic bar with an equilateral-triangle cross-section (60° angles, side labelled 1.5 in.), 4 ft long, fixed to a wall at the left and loaded by a torque T at the free end (curved arrow around the axis).","sol":"1) Triangular section (Table 5-1): τ_allow = 20T/a³: 8000 = 20T/(1.5)³, T = 1350 lb·in. 2) Angle of twist limit: φ_allow = 46TL/(a⁴G): 0.02 = 46T(4)(12)/[(1.5)⁴(3.7e6)], T = 170 lb·in. The angle of twist controls: T = 170 lb·in. 3) Circular shaft with the same area: πc² = ½(1.5)(1.5 sin 60°), c = 0.557 in. 4) Stress: 8000 = T(0.557)/[(π/2)(0.557)⁴], T = 2170 lb·in. 5) Twist: 0.02 = T(4)(12)/[(π/2)(0.557)⁴(3.7e6)], T = 233 lb·in. Again twist controls. Comparison: the circular shaft supports 233/170, about 37% more torque. Ans: T = 170 lb·in. (triangle), T = 233 lb·in. (circle).","answers":["T = 170 lb·in. (triangular section)","T = 233 lb·in. (circular section, same material)"]},{"id":"5.11","title":"Thin-walled circular tube: average shear stress and twist","q":"Calculate the average shear stress in a thin-walled tube having a circular cross section of mean radius r_m and thickness t, which is subjected to a torque T. Also, what is the relative angle of twist if the tube has a length L? (Use the thin-walled-tube formulas τ_avg = T/(2tA_m) and φ = (TL/(4A_m²G))∮ds/t.)","figs":["ex5-11.webp"],"figDesc":"A circular thin-walled tube of length L loaded by equal and opposite torques T at its two ends. The wall thickness is labelled t and the mean radius r_m.","sol":"1) Mean area enclosed by the centerline: A_m = πr_m². 2) Average shear stress: τ_avg = T/(2tA_m) = T/(2πt r_m²). 3) Check with the torsion formula: J = (π/2)(r_o⁴ − r_i⁴) = (π/2)(r_o² + r_i²)(r_o + r_i)(r_o − r_i). For a thin wall r_m ≈ r_o ≈ r_i and t = r_o − r_i, so J ≈ (π/2)(2r_m²)(2r_m)t = 2πr_m³ t. Then τ_avg = Tr_m/J = T/(2πr_m² t), the same. 4) Angle of twist: φ = (TL/(4A_m²G))∮ds/t, with ∮ds = 2πr_m (length of the centerline) and constant t: φ = TL(2πr_m)/[4(πr_m²)²Gt] = TL/(2πr_m³Gt). Ans: τ_avg = T/(2πt r_m²), φ = TL/(2πr_m³Gt).","answers":["τ_avg = T/(2π t r_m²)","φ = TL/(2π r_m³ G t)"]},{"id":"5.12","title":"Rectangular bronze tube with two torques: shear and twist","q":"The tube is made of C86100 bronze and has a rectangular cross section as shown in the figure. If it is subjected to the two torques (60 N·m at the free end C and 25 N·m at D, in opposite senses), determine the average shear stress in the tube at points A and B. Also, what is the angle of twist of end C? The tube is fixed at E. Take G = 38 GPa. Cross section: 60 mm by 40 mm outside, wall thickness 5 mm on the 60 mm-tall sides (point A) and 3 mm top and bottom (point B). CD = 0.5 m, DE = 1.5 m.","figs":["ex5-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Left: rectangular hollow cross-section, 60 mm tall by 40 mm wide overall, wall thickness 3 mm at top and bottom and 5 mm at the vertical sides. Right: the tube, fixed to a wall at E (upper right), with a 60 N·m torque at the free end C, a 25 N·m torque at D (0.5 m from C), and D-E length 1.5 m. Point B is on the top face (3 mm wall) and point A on the side face (5 mm wall), between D and E.","sol":"1) Internal torque at the section through A and B (between D and E): 60 − 25 = 35 N·m. 2) Mean area (centerline rectangle): A_m = (0.035 m)(0.057 m) = 0.00200 m² (dimensions 40 − 5 = 35 mm and 60 − 3 = 57 mm). 3) τ = T/(2tA_m): point A, t = 5 mm: τ_A = 35/[2(0.005)(0.00200)] = 1.75 MPa. Point B, t = 3 mm: τ_B = 35/[2(0.003)(0.00200)] = 2.92 MPa. 4) Angle of twist: φ = Σ[TL/(4A_m²G)]∮ds/t with ∮ds/t = 2(57/5) + 2(35/3) = 46.13. Region CD: T = 60 N·m, L = 0.5 m; region DE: T = 35 N·m, L = 1.5 m (both positive). φ_C = [60(0.5) + 35(1.5)](46.13)/[4(0.00200)²(38e9)] = 6.29e-3 rad = 0.360° (using the unrounded A_m = 0.001995 m² gives the book\u0027s 6.29e-3). Ans: τ_A = 1.75 MPa, τ_B = 2.92 MPa, φ_C = 6.29e-3 rad (0.360°).","answers":["τ_A = 1.75 MPa","τ_B = 2.92 MPa","φ_C = 6.29(10⁻³) rad = 0.360°"]},{"id":"5.13","title":"Stepped shaft with fillets: maximum shear stress","q":"The stepped shaft shown in the figure is supported by bearings at A and B. Determine the maximum stress in the shaft due to the applied torques (30 N·m at each end and 60 N·m on the larger segment). The shoulder fillet at the junction of each shaft has a radius of r = 6 mm. The smaller and larger segments are labelled 20 mm and 40 mm (taken as radii in the book\u0027s solution). Use the stress-concentration-factor chart for a shaft with shoulder fillets.","figs":["ex5-13.webp"],"figDesc":"A stepped circular shaft supported by bearings at A (lower left) and B (upper right). The two end segments are labelled 20 mm and the central segment 40 mm (used as radii in the book\u0027s solution, giving D/d = 2). Torques: 30 N·m at the A end, 60 N·m on the large central segment, and 30 N·m at the B end (opposite sense to the 60 N·m). Fillets at the diameter changes have radius 6 mm.","sol":"1) The shaft is in equilibrium about its axis; the internal torque in the smaller end segments (cut near the ends) is 30 N·m, and the maximum stress occurs at the fillet where the small shaft meets the larger one. 2) Geometry ratios (book takes 20 mm and 40 mm as radii): D/d = 2(40)/2(20) = 2; r/d = 6/[2(20)] = 0.15. From the K chart for shoulder fillets in torsion: K = 1.3. 3) τ_max = K Tc/J with c = 0.020 m (small shaft radius): τ_max = 1.3 [30(0.020)]/[(π/2)(0.020)⁴] = 3.10 MPa. 4) The actual distribution is nonlinear, peaking at the fillet surface, higher than the linear torsion-formula prediction. Ans: τ_max = 3.10 MPa.","answers":["τ_max = 3.10 MPa"]},{"id":"5.14","title":"Elastic-plastic tubular shaft: yield and plastic torque","q":"The tubular shaft shown in the figure is made of an aluminum alloy that is assumed to have an elastic-plastic τ-γ diagram as shown (yield shear stress 20 MPa at a shear strain of 0.286(10⁻³) rad). The tube has an outer radius of 50 mm and an inner radius of 30 mm. Determine the maximum torque that can be applied to the shaft without causing the material to yield, and the maximum torque or plastic torque that can be applied to the shaft. Also, what should the minimum shear strain at the outer wall be in order to develop a fully plastic torque?","figs":["ex5-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Top: hollow circular tube with a torque T acting about its axis; labels 50 mm (outer radius) and 30 mm (inner radius). Bottom, panel (a): τ-γ diagram, elastic-perfectly-plastic: a straight line from the origin to τ = 20 MPa at γ = 0.286(10⁻³) rad, then constant at 20 MPa.","sol":"1) Max elastic torque: yielding starts at the outer fibre where τ = τ_Y = 20 MPa. J = (π/2)[(0.05)⁴ − (0.03)⁴] = 8.545e-6 m⁴. τ_Y = T_Y c/J: 20e6 = T_Y(0.05)/8.545e-6, T_Y = 3.42 kN·m. 2) Plastic torque (all material at τ_Y): T_p = 2π∫τ_Y ρ² dρ from 0.03 to 0.05 = (2π/3)τ_Y(c_o³ − c_i³) = (2π/3)(20e6)[(0.05)³ − (0.03)³] = 4.11 kN·m (20% more than T_Y). 3) Fully plastic requires the inner wall (ρ = 30 mm) to reach yield strain γ_Y = 0.286e-3 rad. Shear strain is linear with radius: γ_o/50 = 0.286e-3/30, so γ_o = 0.477e-3 rad. Ans: T_Y = 3.42 kN·m, T_p = 4.11 kN·m, γ_o = 0.477(10⁻³) rad.","answers":["T_Y = 3.42 kN·m","T_p = 4.11 kN·m","γ_o = 0.477(10⁻³) rad"]},{"id":"5.15","title":"Elastic-plastic solid shaft twisted 0.6 rad: required torque","q":"A solid circular shaft has a radius of 20 mm and length of 1.5 m. The material has an elastic-plastic τ-γ diagram as shown in the figure (τ_Y = 75 MPa at γ_Y = 0.0016 rad, then constant). Determine the torque needed to twist the shaft φ = 0.6 rad.","figs":["ex5-15.webp"],"figDesc":"τ-γ diagram: vertical axis τ (MPa) with a mark at 75, horizontal axis γ (rad). A straight inclined line from the origin to (γ = 0.0016, τ = 75), labelled on the γ axis, then a horizontal line at 75 MPa.","sol":"1) Shear strain at the surface from the twist: φ = γ_max L/c, so 0.6 = γ_max(1.5)/0.02, γ_max = 0.008 rad. 2) Since γ_max \u003e γ_Y = 0.0016, the outer part yields. Elastic core radius by proportion of the linear strain distribution: ρ_Y/0.0016 = 0.02/0.008, ρ_Y = 0.004 m = 4 mm. 3) Stress distribution: linear in 0 ≤ ρ ≤ 4 mm up to 75 MPa, then constant 75 MPa from 4 to 20 mm. 4) Torque: T = (πτ_Y/6)(4c³ − ρ_Y³) = [π(75e6)/6][4(0.02)³ − (0.004)³] = 1.25 kN·m. Ans: T = 1.25 kN·m.","answers":["T = 1.25 kN·m"]},{"id":"5.16","title":"Elastic-plastic tube: plastic torque and residual stress","q":"A tube has a length of 5 ft and the material has an elastic-plastic τ-γ diagram (τ_Y = 12 ksi at γ_Y = 0.002 rad, then constant), as shown in the figure. The tube has inner radius c_i = 1 in. and outer radius c_o = 2 in. Determine the plastic torque T_p. What is the residual shear-stress distribution if T_p is removed just after the tube becomes fully plastic?","figs":["ex5-16.webp"],"figDesc":"Top: annular cross-section of the tube with torque T; inner radius c_i = 1 in., outer radius c_o = 2 in. Below, panel (a): τ-γ diagram (τ in ksi vs γ in rad), elastic-perfectly-plastic: straight line from the origin to τ = 12 ksi at γ = 0.002, then horizontal at 12 ksi.","sol":"1) Plastic torque (all material at τ_Y): T_p = 2π∫τ_Y ρ² dρ from c_i to c_o = (2π/3)τ_Y(c_o³ − c_i³) = (2π/3)(12e3)[(2)³ − (1)³] = 175.9 kip·in. 2) Fully plastic occurs when the inner wall reaches γ_Y = 0.002 rad; the angle of twist is φ_p = γ_Y L/c_i = 0.002(5)(12)/1 = 0.120 rad (not needed for the stress). 3) Removing T_p is equivalent to applying T_p in the opposite direction with a fictitious linear elastic response: modulus of rupture τ_r = T_p c_o/J = 175.9(2)/[(π/2)(2⁴ − 1⁴)] = 14.93 ksi at the outer wall; at the inner wall τ_i = 14.93(1/2) = 7.47 ksi. 4) Superpose the plastic distribution (12 ksi uniform) and the reversed linear distribution: residual at the inner wall = 12 − 7.47 = 4.53 ksi; at the outer wall = 12 − 14.93 = −2.93 ksi (opposite direction). The residual distribution is linear in between. Ans: T_p = 175.9 kip·in.; τ_r = 14.93 ksi (reversal), τ_i = 7.47 ksi; residual 4.53 ksi (inner), 2.93 ksi opposite (outer).","answers":["T_p = 175.9 kip·in.","τ_r = 14.93 ksi, τ_i = 7.47 ksi (reversed linear distribution)","Residual stress: 4.53 ksi at the inner wall, −2.93 ksi at the outer wall"]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Simply supported beam, uniform load: shear and moment diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the simply supported beam shown in the figure, which carries a uniform distributed load w over its span L.","figs":["ex6-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam of span L: pin support at the left end, roller at the right end. Uniform downward distributed load of intensity w over the entire length L.","sol":"1) By symmetry each reaction is wL/2 (upward). 2) Cut at x from the left end, left segment: ΣFy = 0: wL/2 − wx − V = 0, so V = w(L/2 − x). ΣM = 0 about the cut: −(wL/2)x + (wx)(x/2) + M = 0, so M = (w/2)(Lx − x²). 3) V is linear from +wL/2 at x = 0 to −wL/2 at x = L; M is a parabola, zero at both ends. 4) Zero shear at x = L/2 (dM/dx = V = 0), where the moment is maximum: M_max = (w/2)[L(L/2) − (L/2)²] = wL²/8. Ans: V = w(L/2 − x), M = (w/2)(Lx − x²), V_max = wL/2 at the supports, M_max = wL²/8 at midspan.","answers":["V = w(L/2 − x)","M = (w/2)(Lx − x²)","M_max = wL²/8 at x = L/2"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Cantilever with triangular load: shear and moment diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the cantilever beam shown in the figure. The beam is fixed at the left end, has length L, and carries a linearly varying distributed load that is zero at the fixed support and equal to w₀ at the free (right) end.","figs":["ex6-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever beam of length L fixed to a wall at the left end, free at the right end. Downward triangular distributed load: zero intensity at the wall, increasing linearly to w₀ at the free end.","sol":"1) Replace the triangular load by its resultant ½w₀L acting 2L/3 from the wall. Support reactions at the wall: upward force w₀L/2 and a counterclockwise couple w₀L²/3. 2) Free-body of a left segment of length x: load intensity w = w₀x/L by proportion, resultant ½(w₀x/L)x acting x/3 from the cut. ΣFy = 0: w₀L/2 − ½(w₀x/L)x − V = 0, so V = (w₀/2L)(L² − x²). 3) ΣM = 0 about the cut: w₀L²/3 − (w₀L/2)x + ½(w₀x/L)x(x/3) + M = 0, so M = (w₀/6L)(−2L³ + 3L²x − x³). 4) Check: dV/dx = −w and dM/dx = V. 5) V is a parabola from w₀L/2 at the wall to 0 at the free end; M is a cubic from −w₀L²/3 at the wall to 0 at the free end. Ans: V = (w₀/2L)(L² − x²), M = (w₀/6L)(−2L³ + 3L²x − x³).","answers":["V = (w₀/2L)(L² − x²)","M = (w₀/6L)(−2L³ + 3L²x − x³)","At the wall: V = w₀L/2, M = −w₀L²/3"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Simply supported beam, trapezoidal load: shear and moment diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the beam shown in the figure. The simply supported beam is 18 ft long and carries a linearly varying (trapezoidal) distributed load that is 2 kip/ft at the left end and 6 kip/ft at the right end.","figs":["ex6-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam of span 18 ft with a roller support at the left end and a pin support at the right end. Downward trapezoidal distributed load over the full span: 2 kip/ft at the left end increasing linearly to 6 kip/ft at the right end.","sol":"1) Split the load into a rectangle (2 kip/ft, resultant 36 kip at 9 ft from left) and a triangle (rises 4 kip/ft, resultant 36 kip at 12 ft from left). Reactions: left 30 kip, right 42 kip (upward). 2) Left segment of length x: the triangular part has intensity 4(x/18) kip/ft at the cut. ΣFy = 0: 30 − 2x − ½(4x/18)x − V = 0, so V = 30 − 2x − x²/9 (kip). 3) ΣM = 0 about the cut: −30x + (2x)(x/2) + ½(4x/18)x(x/3) + M = 0, so M = 30x − x² − x³/27 (kip·ft). 4) Check: dM/dx = V and dV/dx = −2 − 2x/9 = w. 5) Maximum moment where V = 0: x² + 18x − 270 = 0, x = 9.735 ft. M_max = 30(9.735) − 9.735² − 9.735³/27 = 163 kip·ft. V goes from +30 kip to −42 kip. Ans: V = 30 − 2x − x²/9, M = 30x − x² − x³/27, M_max = 163 kip·ft at x = 9.735 ft.","answers":["V = (30 − 2x − x²/9) kip","M = (30x − x² − x³/27) kip·ft","M_max = 163 kip·ft at x = 9.735 ft"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Beam with couple, point load and partial uniform load: diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the beam shown in the figure. The beam is 10 m long with a roller support at A (left end) and a pin support at C (right end). A couple of 80 kN·m acts at A, a 15 kN downward point load acts at B (5 m from A), and a uniform load of 5 kN/m acts downward from B to C (5 m).","figs":["ex6-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam, roller at left end A (with a 80 kN·m couple applied at A, drawn counterclockwise), pin at right end C. Point B is at midspan, 5 m from A and 5 m from C. A 15 kN downward point load acts at B, and a 5 kN/m downward uniform load acts over the right half BC.","sol":"1) Reactions (found first): A_y = 5.75 kN up, C_y = 34.25 kN up (check: they sum to 15 + 5(5) = 40 kN). 2) Two regions because the load changes at B. Region 0 ≤ x₁ \u003c 5 m, left segment: V = 5.75 kN; ΣM = 0: −80 − 5.75x₁ + M = 0, so M = 5.75x₁ + 80 kN·m. 3) Region 5 m \u003c x₂ ≤ 10 m: V = 5.75 − 15 − 5(x₂ − 5) = 15.75 − 5x₂ kN; M = −2.5x₂² + 15.75x₂ + 92.5 kN·m. 4) Checks: at x₂ = 10, V = −34.25 kN and M = 0, matching the reaction at C. 5) Values for plotting: V = 5.75 kN constant up to B, drops by 15 to −9.25 kN just right of B, then falls linearly to −34.25 kN at C. M = 80 kN·m at A, rises linearly to 108.75 kN·m at B (maximum, where V changes sign), then falls parabolically to 0 at C.","answers":["0 ≤ x₁ \u003c 5 m: V = 5.75 kN, M = (5.75x₁ + 80) kN·m","5 m \u003c x₂ ≤ 10 m: V = (15.75 − 5x₂) kN, M = (−2.5x₂² + 15.75x₂ + 92.5) kN·m","M_max = 108.75 kN·m at B"]},{"id":"6.5","title":"Cantilever with two point loads: shear and moment diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the cantilever beam shown in the figure. The beam is fixed at the left end and carries a downward load P at its midpoint and a downward load P at its free end; each half of the beam has length L.","figs":["ex6-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever beam fixed at a wall on the left, total length 2L. A downward point load P acts at the midpoint (distance L from the wall) and another downward point load P acts at the free right end (distance 2L from the wall).","sol":"1) Reactions at the wall: upward force 2P and a counterclockwise couple 3PL. 2) Shear diagram: V = +2P at the wall; there is no distributed load so the slope is zero and V is constant at 2P up to the midpoint; the downward load P makes V jump down by P to +P, which stays constant to the free end, where V = P (ends at the free-end load). 3) Moment diagram: M = −3PL at the wall. Slope of M equals V, so the first half is a line of slope +2P and the second half a line of slope +P. 4) Moment at midpoint from the area of the shear diagram: M = −3PL + (2P)(L) = −PL. Then M = −PL + P(L) = 0 at the free end. Ans: V = 2P on 0 \u003c x \u003c L and P on L \u003c x \u003c 2L; M = −3PL at the wall, −PL at midspan, 0 at the free end.","answers":["V = 2P (0 \u003c x \u003c L), V = P (L \u003c x \u003c 2L)","M = −3PL at the wall, M = −PL at x = L, M = 0 at the free end"]},{"id":"6.6","title":"Simply supported beam with central couple: shear and moment diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the simply supported beam shown in the figure. The beam has span 2L, a pin at the left end, a roller at the right end, and a clockwise couple M₀ applied at midspan.","figs":["ex6-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam of total length 2L with a pin support at the left end and a roller support at the right end. A concentrated couple M₀ (curved arrow, clockwise) acts at midspan, with distance L on each side of it.","sol":"1) Reactions: ΣM about the left support gives R_right = M₀/(2L) upward and, by force balance, R_left = M₀/(2L) downward. 2) Shear diagram: no distributed load, so V is constant (zero slope). V = −M₀/(2L) along the whole beam. The couple does not affect the shear. 3) Moment diagram: M = 0 at each end. The slope of M equals V, so it has constant negative slope −M₀/(2L): M falls linearly from 0 at the left end to −M₀/2 just left of the center. 4) A clockwise couple causes a positive jump of M₀ in the moment diagram at the center: M goes from −M₀/2 to +M₀/2. 5) Then M decreases linearly with the same slope back to 0 at the right end. Ans: V = −M₀/2L throughout; M = −M₀/2 just left and +M₀/2 just right of the center.","answers":["V = −M₀/(2L) along the entire beam","M = −M₀/2 just left of the center, +M₀/2 just right of the center, 0 at both ends"]},{"id":"6.7","title":"Two cantilevers, uniform and triangular loads: diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for each of the two cantilever beams shown in the figures. Both beams have length L and are fixed at the left end. The first (first figure) carries a uniform distributed load w₀ over its full length. The second (second figure) carries a linearly varying distributed load equal to w₀ at the fixed support and zero at the free end.","figs":["ex6-7.webp","ex6-7b.webp"],"figDesc":"First figure: cantilever of length L fixed at a wall on the left, free on the right, with uniform downward load w₀ over the full length. Second figure: cantilever of length L fixed on the left, downward triangular load that is w₀ at the fixed end and decreases linearly to zero at the free end.","sol":"Beam 1 (uniform load): 1) Wall reactions: force w₀L upward and counterclockwise couple w₀L²/2. 2) Shear diagram: V = w₀L at the wall, slope = −w₀ (w negative constant), so V decreases linearly to 0 at the free end. 3) Moment diagram: M = −w₀L²/2 at the wall; slope equals V (positive, decreasing), so M is a parabola rising to 0 with zero slope at the free end. Beam 2 (triangular load, max at wall): 1) Wall reactions: force w₀L/2 upward, couple w₀L²/6. 2) V = w₀L/2 at the wall; slope of V is −w (negative, decreasing in magnitude), so V is a parabola falling to 0 with zero slope at the free end. 3) M = −w₀L²/6 at the wall, slope equals V (positive, decreasing), so M is a cubic curve rising to 0 at the free end. Note the degree rises by one each integration: w to V to M.","answers":["Beam 1: V from w₀L at the wall (linear) to 0; M from −w₀L²/2 (parabolic) to 0","Beam 2: V from w₀L/2 at the wall (parabolic) to 0; M from −w₀L²/6 (cubic) to 0"]},{"id":"6.8","title":"Cantilever with end load and partial uniform load: diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the cantilever beam shown in the figure. The beam is 4 m long, free at end A (left) and fixed at end B (right). A 2 kN downward force acts at A, and a uniform downward load of 1.5 kN/m acts over the last 2 m, adjacent to the fixed support B.","figs":["ex6-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever AB, free end A on the left, fixed support B on the right. A 2 kN downward point load at A. A 1.5 kN/m downward uniform load spans from the midpoint (2 m from A) to the fixed end B. Dimensions: 2 m from A to the start of the distributed load, 2 m for the loaded portion.","sol":"1) Reactions at B: B_y = 2 + 1.5(2) = 5 kN up and M_B = 11 kN·m (2(4) + 3(1) = 11). 2) Shear diagram: V = −2 kN at A; no load for 0 \u003c x \u003c 2 m so V stays −2 kN (zero slope). From x = 2 to 4 m the slope is −1.5 kN/m, so V falls linearly: V(4) = −2 − 1.5(2) = −5 kN (equals the area under the load). 3) Moment diagram: M = 0 at x = 0. M slope equals V. M(2) = 0 + (−2)(2) = −4 kN·m (area under shear diagram; also checks by method of sections). For 2 \u003c x \u003c 4 the slope becomes more negative (V increasing in magnitude), giving a parabola: the area of the trapezoid under V from 2 to 4 m is (−2 − 5)/2 × 2 = −7, so M(4) = −4 − 7 = −11 kN·m. Ans: V from −2 kN to −5 kN, M from 0 to −4 kN·m at x = 2 m and −11 kN·m at the wall.","answers":["V = −2 kN for 0 \u003c x \u003c 2 m, then linear to −5 kN at x = 4 m","M = 0 at A, −4 kN·m at x = 2 m, −11 kN·m at B"]},{"id":"6.9","title":"Overhang beam with uniform load on overhang: diagrams","q":"Draw the shear and moment diagrams for the overhanging beam shown in the figure. The beam has a pin at A (left end) and a roller at B, 4 m from A. It extends 2 m beyond B and carries a uniform downward load of 4 kN/m over that 2 m overhang.","figs":["ex6-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam 6 m long. Pin support at A at the left end; roller support B at 4 m from A. The beam overhangs 2 m past B, and a 4 kN/m downward uniform load acts over the overhang only.","sol":"1) Reactions: load resultant 4(2) = 8 kN acts 5 m from A. ΣM_A = 0: B_y(4) − 8(5) = 0, B_y = 10 kN up; ΣFy = 0: A_y = 2 kN downward. 2) Shear diagram: V = −2 kN at A and constant (no load) until x = 4 m, where the roller reaction causes a jump up of 10 kN to +8 kN. From x = 4 to 6 m the slope is −4 kN/m, so V decreases linearly to 0 at the free end (8 − 4(2) = 0). 3) Moment diagram: M = 0 at A; slope = −2 so M falls linearly to M(4) = −2(4) = −8 kN·m (area under shear; also by method of sections). From 4 to 6 m V is positive and decreasing, so M rises parabolically with zero slope at x = 6 m: M(6) = −8 + ½(8)(2) = 0. Ans: V = −2, +8 (jump at B), 0; M = 0, −8 kN·m at B, 0.","answers":["V = −2 kN (0 \u003c x \u003c 4 m), jump to +8 kN at B, linear to 0 at x = 6 m","M = 0 at A, M = −8 kN·m at B (x = 4 m), M = 0 at the free end"]},{"id":"6.10","title":"Shaft with triangular load: shear, moment and maximum moment","q":"The shaft shown in the figure is supported by a thrust bearing at A and a journal bearing at B. Draw the shear and moment diagrams. The shaft is 12 ft long and carries a linearly varying distributed load that is zero at A and 120 lb/ft at B.","figs":["ex6-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Shaft of length 12 ft, supported at A (left, thrust bearing) and B (right, journal bearing). Downward triangular distributed load: zero at A increasing linearly to 120 lb/ft at B.","sol":"1) Total load = ½(120)(12) = 720 lb acting 8 ft from A. Reactions: A_y = 240 lb, B_y = 480 lb (both up). 2) Shear: V(0) = +240 lb; V follows the slope −w, so it is a parabola decreasing (increasingly steeply) to V(12) = 240 − 720 = −480 lb. Locate V = 0 by sections: the load intensity at x is w = 10x lb/ft (120/12 = w/x). ΣFy = 0 on the left segment: 240 − ½(10x)x = 0, so x = 6.93 ft. 3) Moment: M = 0 at A (no moment there), then it rises with slope V and is a cubic curve. The maximum occurs at x = 6.93 ft where V = 0: ΣM = 0: M_max + ½(10)(6.93)·6.93·(6.93/3) − 240(6.93) = 0, so M_max = 1109 lb·ft. M returns to 0 at B. The linear load gives a parabolic shear and cubic moment diagram. Ans: V = 0 at x = 6.93 ft, M_max = 1109 lb·ft.","answers":["A_y = 240 lb, B_y = 480 lb","V = 0 at x = 6.93 ft","M_max = 1109 lb·ft"]},{"id":"6.11","title":"Rectangular section with given stress distribution: internal moment","q":"A beam has a rectangular cross section 6 in. wide and 12 in. deep (6 in. above and 6 in. below the neutral axis NA) and is subjected to the linear bending-stress distribution shown in the figure, with a maximum stress of 2 ksi at the top and at the bottom fibres. Determine the internal moment M at the section caused by the stress distribution (a) using the flexure formula, (b) by finding the resultant of the stress distribution using basic principles.","figs":["ex6-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular wooden beam cross-section, width 6 in., with the neutral axis NA at mid-depth; 6 in. above and 6 in. below NA. A linear stress distribution (triangular on each side of NA) is drawn: 2 ksi at the top face and 2 ksi at the bottom face (opposite senses), zero at NA.","sol":"Part (a): 1) Flexure formula σ_max = Mc/I with c = 6 in., σ_max = 2 ksi. 2) I = (1/12)bh³ = (1/12)(6)(12)³ = 864 in⁴. 3) 2 kip/in² = M(6 in.)/864 in⁴, so M = 288 kip·in = 24 kip·ft. Part (b): 1) The resultant force of each triangular stress block equals the volume of the block: F = ½(6 in.)(2 ksi)(6 in.) = 36 kip (one tension, one compression, forming a couple). 2) Each acts through the centroid of its stress volume, (2/3)(6 in.) = 4 in. from the neutral axis, so the lever arm between them is 8 in. 3) M = 36 kip(8 in.) = 288 kip·in = 24 kip·ft. Both methods agree. Ans: M = 288 kip·in = 24 kip·ft.","answers":["M = 288 kip·in = 24 kip·ft"]},{"id":"6.12","title":"Simply supported I-beam: maximum bending stress and distribution","q":"The simply supported beam in the first figure (span 6 m, uniform load 5 kN/m) has the cross-sectional area shown in the second figure (an I-section: two flanges 250 mm wide by 20 mm thick, web 20 mm thick; 150 mm from the neutral axis NA to the inside face of each flange, so total depth 340 mm). Determine the absolute maximum bending stress in the beam and draw the stress distribution over the cross section at this location.","figs":["ex6-12.webp","ex6-12b.webp"],"figDesc":"First figure: simply supported beam of span 6 m with pin at left and roller at right, carrying a uniform downward load of 5 kN/m over the whole span. Second figure: symmetric I-section with centroid C on the neutral axis N-A. Top and bottom flanges are 250 mm wide and 20 mm thick; web is 20 mm thick; the distance from NA to the inner face of each flange is 150 mm. Points B (underside of top flange at the web, 150 mm above NA), C (centroid) and D (on the bottom flange) are marked.","sol":"1) Maximum internal moment occurs at midspan: M_max = wL²/8 = 5(6)²/8 = 22.5 kN·m (the book gives this from the moment diagram). 2) By symmetry the neutral axis passes through the centroid C at mid-depth. Moment of inertia by the parallel-axis theorem in meters: I = Σ(Ī + Ad²) = 2[(1/12)(0.25)(0.020)³ + (0.25)(0.020)(0.160)²] + (1/12)(0.020)(0.300)³ = 301.3(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 3) The farthest fibre is c = 0.170 m from NA: σ_max = Mc/I = 22.5(10³)(0.170)/301.3(10⁻⁶) = 12.7 MPa (compression at top, tension at bottom). 4) The distribution is linear through the depth. At points 150 mm from NA: σ = My/I = 22.5(10³)(0.150)/301.3(10⁻⁶) = 11.2 MPa (compression at B above the NA, tension at the matching point below). Ans: σ_max = 12.7 MPa.","answers":["σ_max = 12.7 MPa","M_max = 22.5 kN·m, I = 301.3(10⁻⁶) m⁴","σ = 11.2 MPa at 150 mm from the neutral axis"]},{"id":"6.13","title":"Channel cantilever with inclined end load: maximum bending stress","q":"The beam shown in the first figure has a cross-sectional area in the shape of a channel, shown in the second figure. Determine the maximum bending stress that occurs in the beam at section a–a. The beam is fixed at the right end, is 3 m long, and carries an inclined 2.6 kN force at its free left end, acting down and to the left with a 5-12-13 slope (5 horizontal, 12 vertical). Section a–a is 2 m from the free end (1 m from the wall). The channel section: a top flange 250 mm wide by 20 mm thick, and two legs 15 mm thick extending down so that the total depth is 200 mm.","figs":["ex6-13.webp","ex6-13b.webp"],"figDesc":"First figure: horizontal cantilever fixed at a wall on the right. A 2.6 kN force at the free left end points down and to the left, with a 5-12-13 triangle marked (horizontal 5, vertical 12, hypotenuse 13). Section a–a is 2 m from the free end; the remaining 1 m reaches the wall. Second figure: inverted channel cross-section (flange on top), flange 250 mm wide by 20 mm thick, two 15 mm thick legs, total depth 200 mm. The neutral axis, centroid and ȳ label are not shown.","sol":"1) Use the segment left of a–a so reactions are not needed. Force components of the 2.6 kN load: vertical 2.4 kN, horizontal 1.0 kN (5-12-13 triangle). 2) Locate the neutral axis from the top: split into the flange and two legs. ȳ = Σ ȳA/ΣA = [2(0.100)(0.200)(0.015) + (0.010)(0.020)(0.250)]/[2(0.200)(0.015) + 0.020(0.250)] = 0.05909 m = 59.09 mm. 3) Moment about the neutral axis (the moment must be computed about the NA, and the axial force N passes through the centroid): ΣM_NA = 0: 2.4(2) + 1.0(0.05909) − M = 0, M = 4.859 kN·m. 4) I = [(1/12)(0.250)(0.020)³ + (0.250)(0.020)(0.05909 − 0.010)²] + 2[(1/12)(0.015)(0.200)³ + (0.015)(0.200)(0.100 − 0.05909)²] = 42.26(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 5) The farthest fibre is the bottom of the legs: c = 0.200 − 0.05909 = 0.1409 m. σ_max = Mc/I = 4.859(10³)(0.1409)/42.26(10⁻⁶) = 16.2 MPa. (At the top, σ = 6.79 MPa.) Axial force and shear are ignored here.","answers":["M = 4.859 kN·m","I = 42.26(10⁻⁶) m⁴","σ_max = 16.2 MPa"]},{"id":"6.14","title":"Rectangular bar with and without small ribs: maximum stress","q":"The member having a rectangular cross section, shown in the first view, is designed to resist a moment of 40 N·m. In order to increase its strength and rigidity, it is proposed that two small ribs be added at its bottom, as in the second view. Determine the maximum normal stress in the member for both cases. The rectangular section is 60 mm wide and 30 mm deep. Each rib is 10 mm wide and 5 mm deep, and the two ribs are located as shown (10 mm spacings) on the bottom face.","figs":["ex6-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Two views of a prismatic bar. (a) Rectangular cross-section 60 mm wide, 30 mm deep, subjected to a 40 N·m moment; the centroidal distance y-bar from the bottom is marked. (b) The same 60 mm by 30 mm section with two small ribs added at the bottom, each 10 mm wide and 5 mm high, with 10 mm dimensions marked along the bottom; neutral axis N-A, y-bar and 40 N·m moment shown.","sol":"Without ribs: 1) NA at mid-depth, c = 0.015 m. I = (1/12)(0.060)(0.030)³ = 0.135(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 2) σ_max = Mc/I = 40(0.015)/0.135(10⁻⁶) = 4.44 MPa. With ribs: 1) Locate the centroid from the top with three rectangles (main rectangle and two ribs): ȳ = [0.015(0.030)(0.060) + 2(0.0325)(0.005)(0.010)]/[(0.030)(0.060) + 2(0.005)(0.010)] = 0.01592 m. 2) The farthest fibre is the bottom of the ribs: c = 0.035 − 0.01592 = 0.01908 m. 3) I = [(1/12)(0.060)(0.030)³ + (0.060)(0.030)(0.01592 − 0.015)²] + 2[(1/12)(0.010)(0.005)³ + (0.010)(0.005)(0.0325 − 0.01592)²] = 0.1642(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 4) σ_max = 40(0.01908)/0.1642(10⁻⁶) = 4.65 MPa. The ribs actually increase the maximum stress, so they should be omitted.","answers":["Without ribs: σ_max = 4.44 MPa","With ribs: σ_max = 4.65 MPa"]},{"id":"6.15","title":"Rectangular section under inclined moment: corner stresses, neutral axis","q":"The rectangular cross section shown in the figure is subjected to a bending moment M = 12 kN·m. Determine the normal stress developed at each corner of the section, and specify the orientation of the neutral axis. The section is 0.4 m deep (0.2 m on each side of the z axis along y) and 0.2 m wide (0.1 m on each side of the y axis along z). The moment vector lies in the y–z plane with components in a 3-4-5 ratio (4 along the y direction, 3 along the z direction).","figs":["ex6-15.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular prism cross-section with centroidal axes y and z in the section plane and x along the member. Corners labeled B, C, D, E. Dimensions: 0.2 m + 0.2 m along the y direction (long side) and 0.1 m + 0.1 m along the z direction (short side). Moment M = 12 kN·m drawn as a vector in the y–z plane with a 3-4-5 triangle marked.","sol":"1) y and z are principal axes (axes of symmetry); take z as the axis for maximum I. Resolve M: M_y = −(4/5)(12) = −9.60 kN·m, M_z = (3/5)(12) = 7.20 kN·m. 2) I_y = (1/12)(0.4)(0.2)³ = 0.2667(10⁻³) m⁴, I_z = (1/12)(0.2)(0.4)³ = 1.067(10⁻³) m⁴. 3) σ = −M_z y/I_z + M_y z/I_y at each corner (y, z in m): B (0.2, −0.1): σ = 2.25 MPa; C (0.2, 0.1): −4.95 MPa; D (−0.2, 0.1): −2.25 MPa; E (−0.2, −0.1): 4.95 MPa. 4) The distribution is linear (superposition). 5) Neutral axis orientation: θ = −tan⁻¹(4/3) = −53.1° (angle of M measured from +z toward +y by the sign convention); tan α = (I_z/I_y) tan θ = (1.067/0.2667) tan(−53.1°) = 4.0(−1.333) = −5.33, so α = −79.4° from the z axis. (Proportion along edge BC: 2.25/z = 4.95/(0.2 − z) gives z = 0.0625 m as a check.)","answers":["σ_B = 2.25 MPa","σ_C = −4.95 MPa","σ_D = −2.25 MPa","σ_E = 4.95 MPa","Neutral axis at α = −79.4° from the z axis"]},{"id":"6.16","title":"Z-section under moment: stress at a point, neutral axis angle","q":"The Z-section shown in the figure is subjected to a bending moment of M = 20 kN·m. Using the methods of Appendix A, the principal axes y and z are oriented as shown, such that they represent the minimum and maximum principal moments of inertia, I_y = 0.960(10⁻³) m⁴ and I_z = 7.54(10⁻³) m⁴, respectively. Determine the normal stress at point P and the orientation of the neutral axis. The moment vector M lies along the horizontal axis y′ (angle θ = 57.1° from the z axis, with z at 32.9° from the vertical z′ axis).","figs":["ex6-16.webp"],"figDesc":"Z-shaped cross-section: a vertical left leg 100 mm wide (top-left, point P at its upper right corner) and 400 mm tall, a horizontal member 100 mm thick, and a right leg extending downward. A dimension of 300 mm runs from the left edge to the centroidal vertical axis z′. Centroidal axes y′ (horizontal) and z′ (vertical) and principal axes y and z (rotated; the z axis is 32.9° from z′, stated in the problem text rather than printed on the figure) are drawn through the centroid as thin lines. The moment M = 20 kN·m acts along y′ (large black arrow with a curved arrow) and the angle θ = 57.1° is marked from the z axis. The moment components and the 32.9° label were removed from the figure.","sol":"1) For Eq. 6–19 the z axis must be the principal axis of maximum moment of inertia. 2) Resolve M along the principal axes: M_y = 20 sin 57.1° = 16.79 kN·m, M_z = 20 cos 57.1° = 10.86 kN·m. 3) Coordinates of P: in the (y′, z′) frame, P = (−0.2 m, 0.35 m). Transform with the 32.9° rotation: y_P = −0.35 sin 32.9° − 0.2 cos 32.9° = −0.3580 m; z_P = 0.35 cos 32.9° − 0.2 sin 32.9° = 0.1852 m. 4) σ_P = −M_z y_P/I_z + M_y z_P/I_y = −10.86(10³)(−0.3580)/7.54(10⁻³) + 16.79(10³)(0.1852)/0.960(10⁻³) = 3.76 MPa. 5) Neutral axis: tan α = (I_z/I_y) tan θ = [7.54(10⁻³)/0.960(10⁻³)] tan 57.1° gives α = 85.3° measured from the z axis.","answers":["σ_P = 3.76 MPa","α = 85.3° (angle of the neutral axis measured from the z axis)"]},{"id":"6.17","title":"Wood beam with steel strap: stresses at two points","q":"A composite beam is made of wood and reinforced with a steel strap located on its bottom side. It has the cross-sectional area shown in the figure: the wood is 150 mm wide and 150 mm deep, and the steel strap is 150 mm wide and 20 mm thick. If the beam is subjected to a bending moment of M = 2 kN·m, determine the normal stress at points B (top of the wood) and C (bottom of the steel). Take E_w = 12 GPa and E_st = 200 GPa.","figs":["ex6-17.webp"],"figDesc":"Composite beam cross-section viewed in perspective: a 150 mm by 150 mm wood block with a 20 mm thick steel strap bonded to its bottom face (same 150 mm width). Point B is at the top right corner of the wood, point C at the bottom of the steel strap. A bending moment M = 2 kN·m acts on the section.","sol":"1) Transform the section to all steel: n = E_w/E_st = 12/200, so the wood width becomes b_st = n·b_w = (12/200)(150) = 9 mm (depth unchanged, 150 mm). 2) Neutral axis from the bottom: ȳ = [0.01(0.02)(0.150) + 0.095(0.009)(0.150)]/[0.02(0.150) + 0.009(0.150)] = 0.03638 m. 3) I_NA = [(1/12)(0.150)(0.02)³ + (0.150)(0.02)(0.03638 − 0.01)²] + [(1/12)(0.009)(0.150)³ + (0.009)(0.150)(0.095 − 0.03638)²] = 9.358(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 4) Stresses in the transformed section: at the top B′ (0.170 − 0.03638 m from NA): σ_B′ = 2(10³)(0.170 − 0.03638)/9.358(10⁻⁶) = 28.6 MPa; at C: σ_C = 2(10³)(0.03638)/9.358(10⁻⁶) = 7.78 MPa (steel, actual stress). 5) The stress in the real wood at B is the transformed stress times n: σ_B = nσ_B′ = (12/200)(28.56 MPa) = 1.71 MPa. Ans: σ_B = 1.71 MPa, σ_C = 7.78 MPa.","answers":["σ_C = 7.78 MPa (steel)","σ_B = 1.71 MPa (wood)"]},{"id":"6.18","title":"Reinforced concrete beam: steel and concrete stresses","q":"The reinforced concrete beam has the cross-sectional area shown in the figure: width 12 in., overall depth 18 in., with two 1-in.-diameter steel reinforcing rods whose centres are 2 in. above the bottom of the section. If it is subjected to a bending moment of M = 60 kip·ft, determine the normal stress in each of the steel reinforcing rods and the maximum normal stress in the concrete. Take E_st = 29(10³) ksi and E_conc = 3.6(10³) ksi.","figs":["ex6-18.webp"],"figDesc":"Perspective view of a concrete beam cross-section 12 in. wide and 18 in. deep with two 1-in.-diameter steel bars near the bottom, the bar centres 2 in. above the bottom face (the 2 in. dimension is marked at the bottom). A moment M = 60 kip·ft acts on the section.","sol":"1) Neglect the tensile strength of concrete. Total steel area A_st = 2[π(0.5 in.)²] = 1.571 in². Transform to an equivalent concrete area: n = E_st/E_conc = 29(10³)/3.6(10³), A′ = nA_st = 12.65 in², acting at the bar level, d = 18 − 2 = 16 in. below the top. 2) Neutral axis depth h′ from the top (centroid of compression concrete = transformed steel moment): 12(h′)(h′/2) − 12.65(16 − h′) = 0, so h′² + 2.11h′ − 33.7 = 0, h′ = 4.85 in. 3) I = (1/12)(12)(4.85)³ + 12(4.85)(4.85/2)² + 12.65(16 − 4.85)² = 2029 in⁴. 4) Max concrete stress at the top: σ_conc = Mc/I = 60(12)(4.85)/2029 = 1.72 ksi. 5) Stress in the transformed steel strip: σ′ = 60(12)(16 − 4.85)/2029 = 3.96 ksi; actual steel stress σ_st = nσ′ = (29/3.6)(3.96) = 31.9 ksi (each rod). Ans: (σ_conc)_max = 1.72 ksi, σ_st = 31.9 ksi.","answers":["(σ_conc)max = 1.72 ksi","σ_st = 31.9 ksi in each rod","Neutral axis depth h′ = 4.85 in., I = 2029 in⁴ (transformed section)"]},{"id":"6.19","title":"Semicircular curved bar with end moments: maximum normal stress","q":"The curved bar has the cross-sectional area shown in the figure. If it is subjected to bending moments of 4 kN·m, determine the maximum normal stress developed in the bar. The bar is a semicircular arc with its center of curvature at O′; the inner radius is 200 mm, the section is a 50 mm wide rectangle (radius 200 to 250 mm) with a triangular portion below it (250 to 280 mm, 30 mm deep, apex outward). The moments tend to decrease the radius of curvature.","figs":["ex6-19.webp"],"figDesc":"Curved bar bent in a semicircle (open on top) with center of curvature O′. A 4 kN·m couple is applied at each end. Radii from O′ are marked: 200 mm (inner face, point B), 250 mm, and 280 mm (outer extreme point A). The cross-section detail at the right shows a 50 mm wide section: a rectangle 50 mm tall (radius 200 to 250 mm) atop a triangle 30 mm tall tapering outward to a point; r-bar (centroid radius) is indicated.","sol":"1) The moment tends to decrease the radius of curvature, so M = −4 kN·m. 2) Area: ΣA = (0.05)² + ½(0.05)(0.03) = 3.250(10⁻³) m². 3) Centroid radius from O′ using a rectangle (centroid at 0.225 m) and a triangle (centroid at 0.260 m): r̄ = [0.225(0.05)(0.05) + 0.260(½)(0.05)(0.03)]/3.250(10⁻³) = 0.23308 m. 4) ∫dA/r from Table 6–1: rectangle = 0.05 ln(0.250/0.200) = 0.011157 m; triangle = [(0.05)(0.280)/(0.280 − 0.250)] ln(0.280/0.250) − 0.05 = 0.0028867 m. 5) Neutral axis radius R = ΣA/Σ∫dA/r = 3.250(10⁻³)/(0.011157 + 0.0028867) = 0.23142 m; r̄ − R = 0.00166 m. 6) Curved-beam formula σ = M(R − r)/[A r (r̄ − R)]: at B (r = 0.200 m): σ_B = −4(10³)(0.23142 − 0.200)/[3.250(10⁻³)(0.200)(0.00166)] = −116 MPa; at A (r = 0.280 m): σ_A = −4(10³)(0.23142 − 0.280)/[3.250(10⁻³)(0.280)(0.00166)] = +129 MPa. The maximum is at A. Ans: σ_max = 129 MPa.","answers":["σ_A = 129 MPa (maximum), σ_B = −116 MPa","R = 0.23142 m, r̄ = 0.23308 m"]},{"id":"6.20","title":"Stepped steel bar with fillets: maximum bending stress","q":"The transition in the cross-sectional area of the steel bar is achieved using shoulder fillets as shown in the figure. If the bar is subjected to a bending moment of 5 kN·m, determine the maximum normal stress developed in the steel. The yield stress is σ_Y = 500 MPa. The bar is 20 mm thick; the wide part is 120 mm deep, the narrow part is 80 mm deep, and the fillet radius is r = 16 mm.","figs":["ex6-20.webp"],"figDesc":"Flat steel bar, 20 mm thick, with a step in depth: 120 mm on the left, 80 mm on the right, connected by a quarter-circle shoulder fillet of radius r = 16 mm (symmetric notch on the opposite side). A 5 kN·m moment acts at each end.","sol":"1) The largest stress occurs at the base of the fillet, where the cross-sectional area is smallest (h = 80 mm). 2) Stress-concentration factor from the K chart for fillets: r/h = 16/80 = 0.2 and w/h = 120/80 = 1.5, so K = 1.45. 3) Use Eq. 6–26 with the smaller section: c = 0.04 m, I = (1/12)(0.020)(0.08)³. σ_max = K(Mc/I) = 1.45[5(10³)(0.04)]/[(1/12)(0.020)(0.08)³] = 340 MPa. 4) Since 340 MPa \u003c σ_Y = 500 MPa, the steel remains elastic and the elastic stress-concentration factor is valid. (Away from the transition, about 80 mm or more, the flexure formula gives 234 MPa.) Ans: σ_max = 340 MPa.","answers":["σ_max = 340 MPa (K = 1.45; material stays elastic)"]},{"id":"6.21","title":"Wide-flange steel beam: shape factor for elastic-plastic bending","q":"The steel wide-flange beam has the dimensions shown in the figure. If it is made of an elastic perfectly plastic material having a tensile and compressive yield stress of σ_Y = 36 ksi, determine the shape factor for the beam. The section has two flanges 8 in. wide by 0.5 in. thick, and a web 0.5 in. thick with 9 in. clear height between the flanges.","figs":["ex6-21.webp"],"figDesc":"Perspective view of a symmetric wide-flange (I) section. Flange width 8 in., flange thickness 0.5 in. (top and bottom); web thickness 0.5 in.; clear web height 9 in. (total depth 10 in.).","sol":"1) Shape factor k = M_p/M_Y. 2) Maximum elastic moment M_Y: I = 2[(1/12)(8)(0.5)³ + 8(0.5)(4.75)²] + (1/12)(0.5)(9)³ = 211.0 in⁴ (flange centroids 4.75 in. from the neutral axis). With c = 5 in.: σ_Y = M_Y c/I, so M_Y = 36(211.0)/5 = 1519.5 kip·in. 3) Plastic moment: whole section yields at 36 ksi; the neutral axis stays at the centroid by symmetry. Divide the stress distribution into four rectangular blocks: flange blocks C₂ = T₂ = 36(0.5)(8) = 144 kip at 4.75 in. from NA; web blocks C₁ = T₁ = 36(0.5)(4.5) = 81 kip at 2.25 in. from NA. M_p = 2[2.25(81) + 4.75(144)] = 1732.5 kip·in. 4) k = 1732.5/1519.5 = 1.14, i.e., only 14% more moment than the elastic limit. Ans: k = 1.14.","answers":["k = 1.14","M_Y = 1519.5 kip·in., M_p = 1732.5 kip·in."]},{"id":"6.22","title":"T-beam of elastic-plastic material: plastic moment","q":"A T-beam has the dimensions shown in the figure. If it is made of an elastic perfectly plastic material having a tensile and compressive yield stress of σ_Y = 250 MPa, determine the plastic moment that can be resisted by the beam. The flange is 100 mm wide by 15 mm thick, and the web is 15 mm thick and 120 mm tall below the flange.","figs":["ex6-22.webp"],"figDesc":"Perspective view of a T-section: horizontal flange 100 mm wide by 15 mm thick on top; vertical web 15 mm thick and 120 mm tall below the flange (120 mm measured from the underside of the flange to the bottom).","sol":"1) The section is not symmetric, so the fully plastic neutral axis does not go through the centroid; it is located by requiring zero net axial force: T − C₁ − C₂ = 0. 2) Let d be the distance of the NA above the bottom of the web (assume d ≤ 120 mm). Tension below NA: web area 0.015·d. Compression above NA: web part 0.015(0.120 − d) plus flange 0.015(0.100). So 250(0.015)(d) − 250(0.015)(0.120 − d) − 250(0.015)(0.100) = 0, giving d = 0.110 m \u003c 0.120 m, OK (NA lies in the web, 10 mm below the flange). 3) Resultant forces: T = 250(0.015)(0.110) = 412.5 kN; C₁ = 250(0.015)(0.010) = 37.5 kN (web part above NA); C₂ = 250(0.015)(0.100) = 375 kN (flange). 4) Moments about the NA: M_p = 412.5(0.110/2) + 37.5(0.01/2) + 375(0.01 + 0.015/2) = 29.4 kN·m. Ans: M_p = 29.4 kN·m.","answers":["M_p = 29.4 kN·m","Plastic neutral axis d = 0.110 m from the bottom of the web"]},{"id":"6.23","title":"Wide-flange beam after fully plastic moment: residual stress","q":"The steel wide-flange beam shown in the figure is subjected to a fully plastic moment M_p. If this moment is removed, determine the residual-stress distribution in the beam. The material is elastic perfectly plastic and has a yield stress of σ_Y = 36 ksi. The section has two flanges 8 in. wide by 0.5 in. thick and a web 0.5 in. thick with 9 in. clear height (overall depth 10 in.). For this section I = 211.0 in⁴ and M_p = 1732.5 kip·in.","figs":["ex6-23.webp"],"figDesc":"Perspective view of a symmetric wide-flange section: flanges 8 in. wide and 0.5 in. thick, web 0.5 in. thick, clear web height 9 in. (total depth 10 in.).","sol":"1) While M_p acts, the stress is ±36 ksi over the entire depth (jump at the neutral axis). 2) Removing M_p is an elastic unloading, equivalent to applying M_p in the reverse direction with a linear distribution. Compute the (fictitious) modulus of rupture: σ_r = M_p c/I = 1732.5(5)/211.0 = 41.1 ksi (\u003c 2σ_Y, as expected, so unloading is elastic). 3) Superposition of the +36 ksi plastic distribution and the reversed linear distribution (41.1 ksi at the extreme fibres, 0 at NA) gives the residual stress. 4) At the extreme fibres: residual = 36 − 41.1 ≈ −5.05 ksi (reversed sign). 5) The residual stress is zero where the reversed elastic stress equals 36 ksi, found by proportion: 41.1/5 = 36/y, so y = 4.38 in. from the neutral axis. At the neutral axis the residual stress is ±36 ksi (just either side). Ans: σ_r = 41.1 ksi; residual stress distribution: 36 ksi at NA decreasing linearly to zero at y = 4.38 in. and reaching 5.05 ksi (opposite sign) at the outer fibres.","answers":["σ_r = 41.1 ksi","Zero residual stress at y = 4.38 in. from the neutral axis","Residual stress 5.05 ksi (reversed) at extreme fibres; 36 ksi at the neutral axis"]},{"id":"6.24","title":"Titanium alloy beam, bilinear stress-strain: moment for given strain","q":"The beam is made of an alloy of titanium that has a stress–strain diagram that can in part be approximated by two straight lines, as shown in the second figure: σ = 15(10³)ε for ε ≤ 0.010 (σ rises to 150 ksi) and σ = 1000ε + 140 (ksi) for 0.010 ≤ ε ≤ 0.050 (σ reaches 190 ksi at ε = 0.050). If the material behavior is the same in both tension and compression, determine the bending moment that can be applied to the beam that will cause the material at the top and bottom of the beam to be subjected to a strain of 0.050 in./in. The beam cross section is rectangular, 2 in. wide and 3 in. deep.","figs":["ex6-24.webp","ex6-24b.webp"],"figDesc":"First figure: rectangular beam cross section, 2 in. wide and 3 in. deep, with a bending moment M acting about the horizontal centroidal axis. Second figure: stress–strain diagram, σ in ksi versus ε in in./in. Line 1: σ = 15(10³)ε from the origin to (0.010, 150 ksi). Line 2: σ = 1000ε + 140 from (0.010, 150 ksi) to (0.050, 190 ksi).","sol":"1) Section symmetric and tension/compression behavior equal, so the neutral axis is at the centroid (mid-depth, 1.5 in. each side). 2) Strain distribution is linear: ε = 0.05 at y = 1.5 in. The strain 0.010 (end of the elastic line) occurs at y = 0.3 in. (0.05/1.5 = 0.010/y). 3) Stress distribution: 150 ksi at y = 0.3 in. rising to 190 ksi at y = 1.5 in. Divide into blocks (2 in. wide) for the tension side: T₁ = C₁ = ½(1.2)(190 − 150 = 40 ksi)(2) = 48 kip at y₁ = 0.3 + (2/3)(1.2) = 1.10 in.; T₂ = C₂ = (1.2)(150)(2) = 360 kip at y₂ = 0.3 + 0.6 = 0.90 in.; T₃ = C₃ = ½(0.3)(150)(2) = 45 kip at y₃ = (2/3)(0.3) = 0.2 in. 4) M = 2[48(1.10) + 360(0.90) + 45(0.2)] = 772 kip·in. (Alternatively integrate: σ = 500y for 0 ≤ y ≤ 0.3 in., σ = 33.33y + 140 for 0.3 ≤ y ≤ 1.5 in.; M = 2[2∫500y² dy + 2∫(33.3y² + 140y) dy] = 772 kip·in.) Ans: M = 772 kip·in.","answers":["M = 772 kip·in."]},{"id":"7.1","title":"Glue shear stress joining two boards in a loaded beam","q":"The simply supported beam shown in the figure is made from two boards (a T-section: a top board and a vertical bottom board). Determine the maximum shear stress in the glue necessary to hold the boards together along the seam where they are joined.","figs":["ex7-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam, 8 m long, roller at left end and pin at right end. Uniform downward load 6.5 kN/m acting over the right half only (the last 4 m); the left 4 m is unloaded. Cross-section (a): T-shape made of a top board (flange) 150 mm wide by 30 mm thick and a vertical bottom board (web) 30 mm wide and 150 mm tall under it. Neutral axis N-A is marked and ȳ is measured from the bottom of the section to it. The glue seam is the interface between the two boards.","sol":"1) Reactions: total load = 6.5(4) = 26 kN at 6 m from the left support. ΣM about left support: R_right(8) = 26(6) so R_right = 19.5 kN; R_left = 6.5 kN. Shear diagram: V = 6.5 kN from x=0 to 4 m, then decreasing linearly to -19.5 kN at 8 m, so |V|max = 19.5 kN at the right support. 2) Centroid from the bottom: ȳ = [0.075(0.150)(0.030) + 0.165(0.030)(0.150)]/[0.150(0.030) + 0.030(0.150)] = 0.120 m. 3) I about the neutral axis = [1/12(0.030)(0.150)³ + 0.150(0.030)(0.120 - 0.075)²] + [1/12(0.150)(0.030)³ + 0.030(0.150)(0.165 - 0.120)²] = 27.0(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 4) The glue is at the underside of the top board, so A\u0027 = top board area (0.150)(0.030) and Q = ȳ\u0027A\u0027 = (0.180 - 0.015 - 0.120)(0.03)(0.150) = 0.2025(10⁻³) m³; width at the seam t = 0.030 m. 5) τmax = VQ/(It) = 19.5(10³)(0.2025e-3)/[27.0e-6(0.030)] = 4.88 MPa.","answers":["τ_max (glue) = 4.88 MPa"]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Shear stress distribution over a rectangular beam section","q":"Determine the distribution of the shear stress over the cross section of the beam shown in the figure. The beam has a rectangular cross section of width b and height h and carries an internal shear force V.","figs":["ex7-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Isometric view of a prismatic beam segment with rectangular cross section: height h (vertical), width b. A downward internal shear force V acts on the cut face. No numerical values (symbolic).","sol":"1) Pick an arbitrary height y above the neutral axis and let A\u0027 be the part of the section above y, of height (h/2 - y) and width b. Its centroid is at y\u0027 = y + ½(h/2 - y). 2) Q = y\u0027A\u0027 = [y + ½(h/2 - y)](h/2 - y)b = ½(h²/4 - y²)b. 3) With I = bh³/12 and t = b: τ = VQ/(It) = (6V/bh³)(h²/4 - y²). 4) The distribution is parabolic in y: τ = 0 at y = ±h/2 (top and bottom surfaces) and is largest at the neutral axis y = 0: τmax = 6V/(bh³)(h²/4) = 1.5 V/A, i.e. 50% larger than the average V/A. (Integrating τ dA over the section returns V.)","answers":["τ = (6V/(bh³))(h²/4 - y²), parabolic distribution","τ_max = 1.5 V/A at the neutral axis (y = 0)"]},{"id":"7.3","title":"Shear stress distribution in a steel wide-flange beam","q":"A steel wide-flange beam has the dimensions shown in the figure. If it is subjected to a shear of V = 80 kN, plot the shear-stress distribution acting over the beam\u0027s cross-sectional area (determine the shear stress at the key points: just above and below the flange-web junction, and at the neutral axis).","figs":["ex7-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Isometric view of an I-shaped (wide-flange) cross section with an 80 kN downward shear V. Flanges 300 mm wide and 20 mm thick (top and bottom). Web 15 mm thick, with 100 mm above and 100 mm below the neutral axis N-A (web depth between flanges 200 mm). The label A marks the right end of the neutral axis and N its left end.","sol":"1) Section is made of rectangles, so the stress varies parabolically in each part. Need τ at B\u0027 (in the flange, just above the web junction), B (in the web, just below the flange) and C (neutral axis). 2) I = 1/12(0.015)(0.200)³ + 2[1/12(0.300)(0.02)³ + 0.300(0.02)(0.110)²] = 155.6(10⁻⁶) m⁴ (flange centroid is 0.110 m from the neutral axis). 3) Point B\u0027 (in the flange, width t = 0.300 m): A\u0027 = flange area, Q = 0.110(0.300)(0.02) = 0.660(10⁻³) m³; τ_B\u0027 = VQ/(It) = 80(10³)(0.660e-3)/[155.6e-6(0.300)] = 1.13 MPa. 4) Point B (in the web at the same level, t = 0.015 m): same Q, τ_B = 80(10³)(0.660e-3)/[155.6e-6(0.015)] = 22.6 MPa. 5) Point C (neutral axis, t = 0.015): A\u0027 = flange plus upper half web; Q = 0.110(0.300)(0.02) + 0.05(0.015)(0.100) = 0.735(10⁻³) m³; τ_C = τmax = 80(10³)(0.735e-3)/[155.6e-6(0.015)] = 25.2 MPa. Distribution: nearly uniform in the web (22.6 to 25.2 MPa), small in the flanges. Average web shear V/A_w = 80(10³)/(0.015)(0.2) = 26.7 MPa. The formula is inaccurate near the flange-web junction.","answers":["τ_B\u0027 = 1.13 MPa","τ_B = 22.6 MPa","τ_C = τ_max = 25.2 MPa"]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Glue shear flow in a built-up three-board beam","q":"The beam is constructed from three boards glued together as shown in the figure. If it is subjected to a shear of V = 850 kN, determine the shear flow at B and B\u0027 that must be resisted by the glue.","figs":["ex7-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Built-up section: a horizontal top board 250 mm wide and 10 mm thick sits on two vertical boards, each 10 mm wide and 300 mm tall (total height 300 mm), separated by a 125 mm clear gap. Glue seams at B and B\u0027 (where the vertical boards meet the underside of the top board). Neutral axis N-A drawn through the centroid; V = 850 kN acts downward. The A\u0027_B, ȳ\u0027_B and ȳ labels were removed.","sol":"1) Centroid from the bottom of the beam: ȳ = [2(0.15)(0.3)(0.01) + 0.305(0.250)(0.01)]/[2(0.3)(0.01) + 0.250(0.01)] = 0.1956 m. 2) I about the neutral axis = 2[1/12(0.01)(0.3)³ + (0.01)(0.3)(0.1956 - 0.150)²] + [1/12(0.250)(0.01)³ + 0.250(0.01)(0.305 - 0.1956)²] = 87.42(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 3) The glue holds the top board, so Q_B = ȳ\u0027_B A\u0027_B = (0.305 - 0.1956)(0.250)(0.01) = 0.2735(10⁻³) m³. 4) Total shear flow along both seams: q\u0027 = VQ/I = 850(10³)(0.2735e-3)/87.42e-6 = 2.66 MN/m. 5) Two seams share it equally: q_B = q_B\u0027 = q\u0027/2 = 1.33 MN/m.","answers":["q_B = q_B\u0027 = 1.33 MN/m (total q\u0027 = 2.66 MN/m over the two seams)"]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Nail spacing in a box beam made of four boards","q":"A box beam is constructed from four boards nailed together as shown in the figure. If each nail can support a shear force of 30 lb, determine the maximum spacing s of the nails at B and at C so that the beam will support the force of 80 lb.","figs":["ex7-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever box beam, fixed at the left wall, free at the right end where a downward 80 lb force acts. Nails at spacing s along the length (on the top and on the side). Cross-section (a): square box formed by four boards each 1.5 in. thick; the 6 in. dimension is drawn between board mid-thickness lines (outer size 7.5 in. by 7.5 in., inner void 4.5 in. by 4.5 in.). Nails at B (through the top board into a side board) and at C (through a side board into the inner top board) are marked.","sol":"1) Internal shear is constant along the beam: V = 80 lb. 2) I = 1/12(7.5)(7.5)³ - 1/12(4.5)(4.5)³ = 229.5 in⁴ (outer 7.5 in. square minus 4.5 in. square void). 3) Nails at B hold the top board (7.5 in. by 1.5 in., centroid 3 in. above the neutral axis): Q_B = 3(7.5)(1.5) = 33.75 in³; q_B = VQ_B/I = 80(33.75)/229.5 = 11.76 lb/in. 4) Nails at C hold the inner portion: Q_C = 3(4.5)(1.5) = 20.25 in³; q_C = 80(20.25)/229.5 = 7.059 lb/in. 5) Each shear flow is resisted at two surfaces (nail row on one side, fibers on the other), so each nail carries half: s_B = 30/(11.76/2) = 5.10 in., use 5 in.; s_C = 30/(7.059/2) = 8.50 in., use 8.5 in.","answers":["s_B = 5 in. (5.10 in. computed)","s_C = 8.5 in. (8.50 in. computed)"]},{"id":"7.6","title":"Largest shear for two nailed I-beam constructions","q":"Nails having a total shear strength of 40 lb are used in a beam that can be constructed either as in Case I or as in Case II, as shown in the figure. If the nails are spaced at 9 in., determine the largest vertical shear that can be supported in each case so that the fasteners will not fail.","figs":["ex7-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Two I-shaped timber beams with the same overall 5 in. depth. Case I: one 3 in. wide, 0.5 in. thick top flange board and one bottom flange board, nailed to a central web board 1 in. thick and 4 in. tall; nails at s = 9 in. go through the flange into the web. Case II: web 1 in. thick, with each flange made of three 1 in. wide by 0.5 in. thick boards side by side (1 in. + 1 in. + 1 in. = 3 in.); nails at s = 9 in. fasten the side flange boards to the web. Neutral axis N-A at mid-depth.","sol":"1) The section is the same in both cases, a 3 in. x 5 in. rectangle minus two 1 in. x 4 in. voids: I = 1/12(3)(5)³ - 2[1/12(1)(4)³] = 20.58 in⁴. 2) Case I: one row of nails holds a whole 3 in. x 0.5 in. flange: Q = ȳ\u0027A\u0027 = 2.25(3)(0.5) = 3.375 in³ (flange centroid 2.25 in. from the neutral axis). Nail shear flow q = 40 lb / 9 in. = VQ/I, so V = (40/9)(20.58)/3.375 = 27.1 lb. 3) Case II: one row of nails holds one 1 in. x 0.5 in. side board onto the web: Q = 2.25(1)(0.5) = 1.125 in³. V = (40/9)(20.58)/1.125 = 81.3 lb.","answers":["Case I: V = 27.1 lb","Case II: V = 81.3 lb"]},{"id":"7.7","title":"Shear flow variation in a thin-walled box beam","q":"The thin-walled box beam shown in the figure is subjected to a shear of 10 kip. Determine the variation of the shear flow throughout the cross section (at points B, C and D).","figs":["ex7-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Hollow rectangular tube (box) with wall thickness 1 in. Outside dimensions: height 8 in. (1 + 3 + 3 + 1 in.) and width 6 in. (1 + 2 + 2 + 1 in.). Vertical 10 kip shear acts downward through the section. Neutral axis N-A horizontal through the center. Points: B at the top center of the top wall, C at the top-left corner, D at the left wall on the neutral axis.","sol":"1) Use centerline dimensions for thin walls: the box is 5 in. wide by 7 in. tall (centerline), wall t = 1 in. By symmetry the neutral axis is at mid-height. 2) I = 1/12(2)(7)³ + 2[(5)(1)(3.5)²] = 179.7 in⁴ (the two 1 in. x 7 in. side walls give the first term; the two 5 in. x 1 in. horizontal walls sit 3.5 in. from the axis). 3) B (middle of the top wall, on the axis of symmetry): the area cut off is effectively zero, so Q_B = 0 and q_B = 0. 4) C (top corner): A\u0027 = the whole top wall, Q_C = (3.5 in.)(5 in.)(1 in.) = 17.5 in³; two walls carry it: q_C = ½(VQ_C/I) = ½(10)(17.5)/179.7 = 0.487 kip/in. 5) D (neutral axis, in a side wall): A\u0027 = the top wall plus the upper 3.5 in. of each of the two side walls (three rectangles): Q_D = 2[(3.5/2)(1)(3.5)] + 3.5(5)(1) = 29.75 in³; q_D = ½(10)(29.75)/179.7 = 0.828 kip/in. Shear flow is linear along the horizontal walls (0 at B to 0.487 at C) and parabolic along the vertical walls (0.487 at C to 0.828 max at D).","answers":["q_B = 0","q_C = 0.487 kip/in","q_D = 0.828 kip/in (maximum)"]},{"id":"7.8","title":"Shear center of a thin-walled channel section","q":"Determine the location e of the shear center, measured from the web, for the thin-walled channel section having the dimensions shown in the figure (flange width b, web height h, uniform wall thickness t).","figs":["ex7-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Thin-walled C-shaped channel opening to the right. Flange length b (horizontal), web height h (vertical, between flange centerlines), constant wall thickness t. Symbolic; no numeric values.","sol":"1) Apply a vertical shear V; the shear flow produces a horizontal force F_f in each flange and a force V in the web. Moments are taken about the web (point A on the web centerline) so only the flange forces matter. 2) I about the neutral axis (thin walls) = (1/12)t h³ + 2[b t (h/2)²] = (t h²/2)(h/6 + b). 3) Shear flow at distance x along the (lower) flange: q = VQ/I with Q = (h/2)(b - x)t, giving q = V(b - x)/(h[(h/6) + b]). 4) Flange force F_f = ∫₀ᵇ q dx = V b²/(2h[(h/6) + b]). 5) Moment equilibrium about A: V e = F_f h, so e = F_f h/V = b²/[2((h/6) + b)] = b²/[(h/3) + 2b]. The result depends only on the geometry of the section.","answers":["e = b²/[(h/3) + 2b]"]},{"id":"7.9","title":"Shear center and leg forces of an equal-leg angle","q":"Determine the location of the shear center for the angle having equal legs shown in the figure. Also, find the internal shear force resultant in each leg. (Each leg has length b and thickness t; the legs are at 45° above and below the horizontal axis; the vertical shear V is applied.)","figs":["ex7-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Thin-walled angle section with two equal legs of length b and thickness t, meeting at a corner on the left and opening to the right, symmetric about a horizontal axis; each leg is inclined 45° from the horizontal line.","sol":"1) Under a vertical shear V the shear flow in each leg runs along that leg, so each leg\u0027s resultant force F acts along the centerline of the leg. 2) Horizontal equilibrium requires the two leg forces to be equal in magnitude; their lines of action both pass through the corner O of the angle, so O is the shear center (moment of F, F and V about O is zero). 3) Find F: shear flow at distance s along the top leg: Q = ȳ\u0027A\u0027 = (1/√2)(b - s/2)(s t) (the part of the leg from the free end to s; its centroid is (1/√2)(b - s + s/2)). 4) Moment of inertia from first principles: I = 2∫₀ᵇ [(1/√2)(b - s)]² t ds = t b³/3. 5) q = VQ/I = [3V/(√2 b³)] s(b - s/2), parabolic, max at s = b. 6) F = ∫₀ᵇ q ds = [3V/(√2 b³)](b·b²/2 - b³/6) = V/√2. Check: the vertical components of the two leg forces sum to 2(V/√2)(1/√2) = V and the horizontal components cancel.","answers":["Shear center is at the corner (vertex) O of the angle","F = V/√2 = 0.707 V in each leg"]},{"id":"8.1","title":"Maximum pressure for cylindrical versus spherical vessel","q":"A cylindrical pressure vessel has an inner diameter of 4 ft and a thickness of ½ in. Determine the maximum internal pressure it can sustain so that neither its circumferential nor its longitudinal stress component exceeds 20 ksi. Under the same conditions, what is the maximum internal pressure that a similar-size spherical vessel can sustain?","figs":[],"figDesc":"No problem figure (the example shows only photographs of a cylindrical tank and a spherical tank).","sol":"1) Inner radius r = 24 in., t = 0.5 in.; r/t = 48 \u003e 10 so thin-wall formulas apply. 2) Cylinder: the circumferential (hoop) stress governs, σ1 = pr/t (longitudinal σ2 = pr/2t is half as large). 20 = p(24)/0.5, so p = 20(0.5)/24 = 0.4167 ksi = 417 psi. (Then σ2 = 10 ksi; radial stress at inner wall = p = 417 psi, about 48 times smaller than the hoop stress, so it is neglected.) 3) Sphere: stress is the same in all in-plane directions, σ2 = pr/(2t). 20 = p(24)/[2(0.5)], so p = 20(1)/24 = 0.833 ksi = 833 psi. The sphere carries twice the pressure of the cylinder.","answers":["Cylinder: p = 417 psi","Sphere: p = 833 psi"]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Stresses at two points of a block under an edge load","q":"A force of 150 lb is applied to the edge of the member shown in the figure. Neglect the weight of the member and determine the state of stress at points B and C. (The cross section is 10 in. by 4 in.; the section through B and C is taken as shown.)","figs":["ex8-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical rectangular block (cross-section 10 in. wide by 4 in. deep: dimensions 5 in. + 5 in. across the width and 2 in. + 2 in. across the depth). A 150 lb vertical downward force acts on the top face at the right edge (5 in. from the centroid in the width direction, on the centerline in the depth direction). Points B (left edge) and C (right edge) are marked on the side faces at a section through mid-height of the block, with a horizontal section line through them.","sol":"1) Cut the member through B and C and take the upper part. Equilibrium requires an axial force P = 150 lb through the centroid plus a bending moment M = 150(5) = 750 lb·in. about the centroidal axis. 2) A = 10(4) = 40 in², I = (1/12)(4)(10)³ = 333.3 in⁴, c = 5 in. 3) Normal force: σ = P/A = 150/40 = 3.75 psi (compressive). 4) Bending: σmax = Mc/I = 750(5)/333.3 = 11.25 psi (tension on the side away from the load, compression on the loaded side). 5) Superpose, compression negative: B (far side from the load): σ_B = -3.75 + 11.25 = 7.5 psi (tension). C (near the load): σ_C = -3.75 - 11.25 = -15 psi (compression). Only uniaxial normal stress acts at B and C (no shear). The line of zero stress is at x = 3.33 in. from B (by proportional triangles 7.5/x = 15/(10 - x)).","answers":["σ_B = 7.5 psi (tension)","σ_C = 15 psi (compression)"]},{"id":"8.3","title":"State of stress in an open steel tank filled with water","q":"The tank shown in the figure has an inner radius of 24 in. and a thickness of 0.5 in. It is filled to the top with water having a specific weight of γ_w = 62.4 lb/ft³. If it is made of steel having a specific weight of γ_st = 490 lb/ft³, determine the state of stress at point A. The tank is open at the top.","figs":["ex8-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Open-top cylindrical tank filled with water to the top. Inner radius r = 24 in., wall thickness t = 0.5 in. Point A is on the outside wall at a depth of 3 ft below the top rim.","sol":"1) Weight of the steel wall above A: W_st = γ_st V_st = 490[π(24.5/12)² - π(24/12)²](3) = 777.7 lb. The water\u0027s weight is carried by the water below, not by the walls, because the tank is open (no longitudinal pressure force on the walls). 2) Water (gauge) pressure at depth 3 ft: p = γ_w z = 62.4(3) = 187.2 lb/ft² = 1.30 psi. 3) r/t = 24/0.5 = 48 \u003e 10, so thin-wall theory applies. Circumferential: σ1 = pr/t = 1.30(24)/0.5 = 62.4 psi. 4) Longitudinal: the walls just carry the weight above A: σ2 = W_st/A_st = 777.7/{π[(24.5)² - (24)²]} = 10.2 psi. (Do not use σ2 = pr/2t; the open top means no pressure force acts on the walls longitudinally.) Point A is in biaxial stress, no shear.","answers":["σ1 (circumferential) = 62.4 psi","σ2 (longitudinal) = 10.2 psi"]},{"id":"8.4","title":"Stress at a point of a pinned and propped bent beam","q":"The member shown in the figure has a rectangular cross section (50 mm wide by 250 mm deep). Determine the state of stress that the loading produces at point C. (A is a pin support and B is a roller on a surface perpendicular to the inclined segment; the inclined segment carries a distributed load of 50 kN/m perpendicular to it.)","figs":["ex8-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Bent beam: horizontal segment from pin support A at the left wall for 4 m, then an inclined segment dropping 1.5 m vertically over 2 m horizontally (length 2.5 m), ending at roller B on an inclined surface. Distributed load 50 kN/m acts over the 2.5 m inclined length, perpendicular to it. Point C is on the top edge of the horizontal segment 1.5 m from the left. The cross section (shown at left) is 50 mm thick by 250 mm tall with C at the top; the pin is at the mid-depth (125 mm from top).","sol":"1) Support reactions: resultant of the distributed load = 50(2.5) = 125 kN, perpendicular to the incline (3-4-5 slope), acting at its midpoint, 1.25 m along the incline. Moments about A and equilibrium give B = 97.59 kN (perpendicular to incline), A_x = 16.45 kN, A_y = 21.93 kN. 2) Cut at C (1.5 m from A) and use segment AC: N = 16.45 kN (compression), V = 21.93 kN, M = 21.93(1.5) = 32.89 kN·m. 3) Normal force: σ = P/A = 16.45(10³)/[(0.050)(0.250)] = 1.32 MPa (compression). 4) Shear: C is on the top surface so Q = 0 and τ_C = 0. 5) Bending: C is at y = c = 0.125 m: σ = Mc/I = 32.89(10³)(0.125)/[(1/12)(0.050)(0.250)³] = 63.16 MPa (compression at the top). 6) Superposition: σ_C = -1.32 - 63.16 = -64.5 MPa. C is in uniaxial compression.","answers":["σ_C = 64.5 MPa (compression)","τ_C = 0"]},{"id":"8.5","title":"Stress at a point of a bent rod loaded axially","q":"The solid rod shown in the figure has a radius of 0.75 in. If it is subjected to the force of 500 lb, determine the state of stress at point A.","figs":["ex8-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Solid circular rod CB bent at a right angle, fixed to a wall at C. From C a straight segment runs along the y direction, with point A on it 8 in. from C and 10 in. before the bend; after the bend a 14 in. segment runs parallel to the x direction to the free end B. A 500 lb force acts at B parallel to the y axis (along the first segment\u0027s direction). Axes x, y, z (z vertical) are drawn. Rod radius 0.75 in.","sol":"1) Cut the rod at A and use segment AB. Equilibrium: normal force P = 500 lb along the rod axis (y direction) at A, and a bending moment M = 500(14) = 7000 lb·in. about the z axis (arm 14 in.); no shear, torque or other loads. 2) Section properties: A = π(0.75)² = 1.767 in², I = (π/4)(0.75)⁴ = 0.2485 in⁴, c = 0.75 in. 3) Normal force: σ = P/A = 500/1.767 = 283 psi = 0.283 ksi. 4) Bending: A lies on the outer fiber of the bending axis (c = 0.75 in.): σ = Mc/I = 7000(0.75)/0.2485 = 21,126 psi = 21.13 ksi. 5) Both act in the same sense at A, so they add: (σ_A)_y = 0.283 + 21.13 = 21.4 ksi. There is no shear stress at A (uniaxial stress).","answers":["(σ_A)_y = 21.4 ksi (from 0.283 ksi axial + 21.13 ksi bending)"]},{"id":"8.6","title":"Stress at a point of a bent rod under a vertical load","q":"The solid rod shown in the figure has a radius of 0.75 in. If it is subjected to the force of 800 lb, determine the state of stress at point A.","figs":["ex8-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Same geometry as a bent solid rod CB fixed at C (wall at upper left, partly cropped): straight segment from C along y, point A on it 8 in. from C and 10 in. before the bend, then a 14 in. segment parallel to x to the free end B. A 800 lb force acts at B vertically downward (negative z). Axes x, y, z are drawn. Rod radius 0.75 in. (The crop cuts off the wall and the label C on the left edge.)","sol":"1) Section at A, use segment AB. Six equilibrium equations give: shear V = 800 lb (vertical), torque T = 800(14) = 11,200 lb·in. (arm 14 in. along x), bending moment M = 800(10) = 8000 lb·in. (arm 10 in. from A to the bend line). 2) Section properties: I = (π/4)(0.75)⁴ = 0.2485 in⁴, J = (π/2)(0.75)⁴ = 0.4970 in⁴. 3) Transverse shear: A lies on the neutral axis of bending, where Q = ȳ\u0027A\u0027 = [4(0.75)/(3π)][½π(0.75)²] = 0.2813 in³; τ = VQ/(It) = 800(0.2813)/[0.2485(2×0.75)] = 604 psi = 0.604 ksi. 4) Bending: A is on the neutral axis so σ_A = 0. 5) Torsion: ρ = c = 0.75 in.: τ = Tc/J = 11,200(0.75)/0.4970 = 16,901 psi = 16.90 ksi. 6) The shear stresses from V and T act in the same direction at A and add: τ_A = 0.604 + 16.90 = 17.5 ksi. Pure shear state (σ = 0).","answers":["τ_A = 17.5 ksi (0.604 ksi from shear force + 16.90 ksi from torque)","σ_A = 0"]},{"id":"8.7","title":"Middle-third rule for an eccentric load on a block","q":"A rectangular block has a negligible weight and is subjected to a vertical force P, as shown in the figure. (a) Determine the range of values for the eccentricity e_y of the load along the y axis so that it does not cause any tensile stress in the block. (b) Specify the region on the cross section where P may be applied without causing a tensile stress in the block.","figs":["ex8-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular block, top face dimensions: h along the y direction and b along the x direction. Axes x, y, z (z vertical, origin at the centroid of the top face). Vertical downward load P applied on the top face at a distance e_y from the centroid along the y axis. A generic point at coordinate y on the section is marked. Symbolic.","sol":"1) Move P to the centroid and add the couple M_x = P e_y. Normal stress at coordinate y (compression negative): σ = -P/A - (P e_y) y/I_x = -(P/A)(1 + A e_y y/I_x). 2) The smallest compressive stress is on the edge y = -h/2 (the edge where the moment produces tension): σ_min = -(P/A)(1 - A e_y h/(2I_x)). 3) No tension requires the bracket to be positive: 1 \u003e A e_y h/(2I_x). With A = bh and I_x = bh³/12 this gives 1 \u003e 6e_y/h, i.e. e_y \u003c h/6. 4) By symmetry the range is -h/6 ≤ e_y ≤ h/6 (the \u0027middle third\u0027). 5) Part (b): repeating the argument for P placed along the x axis gives |e_x| ≤ b/6. Combining, the allowable region (the core or kern of the section) is the shaded parallelogram (rhombus) with vertices at distance b/6 from the centroid along x and h/6 along y.","answers":["(a) -h/6 ≤ e_y ≤ h/6 (middle-third rule)","(b) P must lie in the rhombus (kern) with vertices at x = ±b/6 and y = ±h/6"]},{"id":"9.1","title":"Plane stress on an element rotated 30° clockwise","q":"The state of plane stress at a point on the surface of the airplane fuselage is represented on the element shown in the figure (σx = -80 MPa, σy = 50 MPa, shear 25 MPa). Represent the state of stress at the point on an element that is oriented 30° clockwise from the position shown. Solve using the method of sectioning the element and equilibrium of the wedge (force equilibrium), not the transformation equations.","figs":["ex9-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element at a point on a fuselage (the fuselage is cropped out). Right face (+x): 80 MPa compressive (arrow pointing into the element) and shear 25 MPa acting downward. Left face: 80 MPa compressive (arrow into the element) and shear 25 MPa acting upward. Top face: 50 MPa tensile (arrow outward, up) with shear 25 MPa acting to the left. Bottom face: 50 MPa tensile (arrow down) with shear 25 MPa acting to the right. Two thin lines through the lower-left corner of the element, a-a (30° below horizontal, rising to the left) and b-b (perpendicular to a-a), indicate the planes of the rotated element; the 30° angle is marked.","sol":"1) Data: σx = -80 MPa, σy = +50 MPa, τxy = -25 MPa (shear on the +x face points down = negative). 2) Cut the element along plane a-a (30° clockwise orientation). Take the bottom wedge: inclined area ΔA, horizontal area ΔA cos30°, vertical area ΔA sin30°. Define x\u0027 outward normal to the cut face and y\u0027 along it. 3) Force balance in x\u0027 and y\u0027 with the forces 50ΔA cos30° (vertical), 25ΔA cos30° (horizontal shear on the bottom), 80ΔA sin30° and 25ΔA sin30° (on the vertical face): σx\u0027ΔA - (50ΔA cos30°)cos30° + (25ΔA cos30°)sin30° + (80ΔA sin30°)sin30° + (25ΔA sin30°)cos30° = 0 gives σx\u0027 = -4.15 MPa. ΣF_y\u0027 = 0 gives τx\u0027y\u0027 = 68.8 MPa. 4) Repeat on the perpendicular plane b-b: σx\u0027 = -25.8 MPa, τx\u0027y\u0027 = 68.8 MPa (positive on that face). 5) Rotated element: -4.15 MPa normal stress on one pair of faces, -25.8 MPa on the other, with 68.8 MPa shear on all four faces (directions as in the book\u0027s Fig. 9-4d). Check: σx + σy is invariant: -4.15 - 25.8 = -30 = -80 + 50.","answers":["σ_x\u0027 = -4.15 MPa (face on plane a-a)","σ_x\u0027 = -25.8 MPa (face on plane b-b)","τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 68.8 MPa"]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Rotated plane-stress element using transformation equations","q":"The state of plane stress at a point is represented by the element shown in the figure (σx = -80 MPa, σy = 50 MPa, 25 MPa shear as drawn). Determine the state of stress at the point on another element oriented 30° clockwise from the position shown, using the stress-transformation equations.","figs":["ex9-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Right face: 80 MPa compression (arrow toward element) and 25 MPa shear pointing down; left face: 80 MPa compression and shear pointing up. Top face: 50 MPa tension (arrow up) with shear pointing left; bottom face: 50 MPa tension (arrow down) with shear pointing right. Same state as Example 9.1.","sol":"1) From the sign convention (tension +, shear positive when on the +x face it acts upward): σx = -80 MPa, σy = 50 MPa, τxy = -25 MPa. 2) Plane CD (30° clockwise orientation): θ = -30° measured from x to the outward normal x\u0027. σx\u0027 = (σx+σy)/2 + (σx-σy)/2 cos2θ + τxy sin2θ = -15 + (-65)cos(-60°) + (-25)sin(-60°) = -15 - 32.5 + 21.65 = -25.8 MPa. τx\u0027y\u0027 = -(σx-σy)/2 sin2θ + τxy cos2θ = 65 sin(-60°) + (-25)cos(-60°) = -56.3 - 12.5 = -68.8 MPa. Negative signs mean they act in the negative x\u0027, y\u0027 directions. 3) Plane BC (perpendicular): θ = +60° (ccw). σx\u0027 = -15 + (-65)cos120° + (-25)sin120° = -15 + 32.5 - 21.65 = -4.15 MPa; τx\u0027y\u0027 = 65 sin120° + (-25)cos120° = 56.3 + 12.5 = 68.8 MPa (check, same magnitude as before). Result: normal stresses -25.8 MPa and -4.15 MPa with shear of magnitude 68.8 MPa on the rotated element.","answers":["Plane CD: σ_x\u0027 = -25.8 MPa, τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = -68.8 MPa","Plane BC: σ_x\u0027 = -4.15 MPa, τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 68.8 MPa"]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Principal stresses at a shaft failure point","q":"The state of plane stress at a failure point on a shaft is shown on the element in the figure (σx = -20 MPa, σy = 90 MPa, τxy = 60 MPa). Represent this stress state in terms of the principal stresses.","figs":["ex9-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Top and bottom faces: 90 MPa tension (arrows outward, up and down). Left and right faces: 20 MPa compression (arrows pointing into the element). 60 MPa shear: right face upward, left face downward, top face pointing right, bottom face pointing left.","sol":"1) σx = -20 MPa, σy = 90 MPa, τxy = 60 MPa (right-face shear points up = positive). 2) Orientation: tan2θp = τxy/[(σx-σy)/2] = 60/[(-20-90)/2] = 60/(-55). Solving: 2θp2 = -47.49°, θp2 = -23.7°; the other root is 180° apart: 2θp1 = 132.51°, θp1 = 66.3°. 3) Principal stresses: σ1,2 = (σx+σy)/2 ± √[((σx-σy)/2)² + τxy²] = 35.0 ± √(55² + 60²) = 35.0 ± 81.4, so σ1 = 116 MPa and σ2 = -46.4 MPa. 4) Which plane has which: substituting θ = -23.7° in σx\u0027 = (σx+σy)/2 + (σx-σy)/2 cos2θ + τxy sin2θ gives -46.4 MPa, so σ2 = -46.4 MPa acts on the plane at θp2 = -23.7° (clockwise) and σ1 = 116 MPa acts on the plane at θp1 = 66.3° (counterclockwise from x). No shear stress acts on these planes.","answers":["σ1 = 116 MPa at θp1 = 66.3° (counterclockwise)","σ2 = -46.4 MPa at θp2 = -23.7° (clockwise)"]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Maximum in-plane shear stress for a plane-stress element","q":"The state of plane stress at a point on a body is represented on the element shown in the figure (σx = -20 MPa, σy = 90 MPa, τxy = 60 MPa). Represent this stress state in terms of the maximum in-plane shear stress and the associated average normal stress.","figs":["ex9-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Top and bottom faces: 90 MPa tension (arrows outward). Left and right faces: 20 MPa compression (arrows pointing into the element). 60 MPa shear: right face upward, left face downward, top pointing right, bottom pointing left.","sol":"1) σx = -20, σy = 90, τxy = 60 (MPa). 2) Orientation: tan2θs = -[(σx-σy)/2]/τxy = -(-55)/60, so 2θs2 = 42.5°, θs2 = 21.3°; the second root 2θs1 = 180° + 42.5°, θs1 = 111.3°. (These planes are 45° from the principal planes of Example 9.3.) 3) Maximum in-plane shear: τmax = √[((σx-σy)/2)² + τxy²] = √(55² + 60²) = 81.4 MPa. 4) Direction: τx\u0027y\u0027 at θ = 21.3° = -(σx-σy)/2 sin2θ + τxy cos2θ = 55 sin42.5° + 60 cos42.5° = 81.4 MPa (positive), so it acts in the +y\u0027 direction on that face; the other three faces follow. 5) Average normal stress on those planes: σavg = (σx+σy)/2 = (-20+90)/2 = 35 MPa (tension).","answers":["τ_max in-plane = 81.4 MPa","σ_avg = 35 MPa (tension)","θ_s = 21.3° (and 111.3°)"]},{"id":"9.5","title":"Pure shear in a twisted bar: maximum shear and principal stresses","q":"When the torsional loading T is applied to the bar in the figure, it produces a state of pure shear stress in the material. Determine (a) the maximum in-plane shear stress and the associated average normal stress, and (b) the principal stress.","figs":["ex9-5.webp"],"figDesc":"A circular bar twisted by equal and opposite torques T at its two ends, with a small square element taken from its surface. The element is drawn enlarged: shear stress τ on all four faces (top face pointing left, right face pointing down, left face pointing up, bottom face pointing right), no normal stresses.","sol":"1) State from the sign convention: σx = 0, σy = 0, τxy = -τ (shear on the +x face points down). 2) Maximum in-plane shear: τmax = √[((σx-σy)/2)² + τxy²] = √(0 + τ²) = ±τ; σavg = (σx+σy)/2 = 0. So the original element already is the max in-plane shear state. (Ductile materials such as mild steel fail in shear along a transverse plane.) 3) Principal stresses: tan2θp = τxy/[(σx-σy)/2] = -τ/0, so θp2 = 45°, θp1 = -45°. σ1,2 = 0 ± √(0 + τ²) = ±τ. 4) Check with σx\u0027 at θ = 45°: σx\u0027 = 0 + 0 + (-τ)sin90° = -τ. So σ2 = -τ acts at θp2 = 45° and σ1 = +τ at θp1 = -45° (an element rotated 45° has pure tension and compression; brittle materials such as cast iron fail in tension along a 45° helix).","answers":["τ_max in-plane = ±τ","σ_avg = 0","σ1 = τ (at θ = -45°), σ2 = -τ (at θ = 45°)"]},{"id":"9.6","title":"Axially loaded bar: principal and maximum shear stresses","q":"When the axial loading P is applied to the bar in the figure, it produces a tensile stress in the material. Determine (a) the principal stress and (b) the maximum in-plane shear stress and associated average normal stress.","figs":["ex9-6.webp"],"figDesc":"A circular bar in axial tension with forces P at both ends, with a small square element on its surface shown enlarged: tensile stress σ acts on the left and right faces (arrows outward, horizontally), nothing on the top and bottom faces.","sol":"1) State: σx = σ, σy = 0, τxy = 0. 2) The element has no shear stress, so by inspection it is already a principal-stress element: σ1 = σ, σ2 = 0 (also from Eq. 9-4, 9-5). Brittle materials fail due to normal stress (cast iron breaks straight across). 3) Maximum in-plane shear: tan2θs = -[(σx-σy)/2]/τxy = -(σ/2)/0, so θs1 = 45°, θs2 = -45°. τmax = √[(σ/2)² + 0] = ±σ/2. σavg = (σx+σy)/2 = σ/2. 4) Direction: τx\u0027y\u0027 at θ = 45° = -(σ/2) sin90° = -σ/2; this negative stress acts on the x\u0027 face in the negative y\u0027 direction. Ductile materials (mild steel) fail in shear on 45° planes (cup-and-cone necking).","answers":["σ1 = σ, σ2 = 0","τ_max in-plane = ±σ/2 (at θ = ±45°)","σ_avg = σ/2"]},{"id":"9.7","title":"Principal stresses at a point on a shaft using Mohr\u0027s circle","q":"Due to the applied loading, the element at point A on the solid shaft in the figure is subjected to the state of stress shown (12 ksi compression on the left and right faces, 6 ksi shear). Determine the principal stresses acting at this point using Mohr\u0027s circle.","figs":["ex9-7.webp"],"figDesc":"A solid shaft with loads P (axial, pointing into the end), torque T and bending moment M at its end; the point A is on the shaft\u0027s surface and a stress element is shown enlarged. Left and right faces: 12 ksi compression (arrows pointing into the element). Shear 6 ksi: right face pointing down, left face pointing up, top face pointing left, bottom face pointing right. Top and bottom faces have no normal stress.","sol":"1) Read the element: σx = -12 ksi, σy = 0, τxy = -6 ksi (right-face shear downward). 2) Mohr\u0027s circle: center on the σ axis at σavg = (-12 + 0)/2 = -6 ksi. Reference point A(-12, -6) for the x face (θ = 0); the y face plots as A\u0027(0, +6). Center C(-6, 0). 3) Radius R = √[(12-6)² + 6²] = √72 = 8.49 ksi. 4) Principal stresses are where the circle meets the σ axis: σ1 = -6 + 8.49 = 2.49 ksi, σ2 = -6 - 8.49 = -14.5 ksi. 5) Orientation: the counterclockwise angle on the circle from CA to the σ2 point D is 2θp2 = tan⁻¹[6/(12-6)] = 45.0°, so θp2 = 22.5° counterclockwise from the x axis: the x\u0027 axis (the σ2 = -14.5 ksi direction) is rotated 22.5° ccw from x; σ1 = 2.49 ksi acts on the perpendicular face.","answers":["σ1 = 2.49 ksi","σ2 = -14.5 ksi","θ_p2 = 22.5° counterclockwise from x"]},{"id":"9.8","title":"Maximum in-plane shear stress by Mohr\u0027s circle","q":"The state of plane stress at a point is shown on the element in the figure (σx = -20 MPa, σy = 90 MPa, τxy = 60 MPa). Determine the maximum in-plane shear stress at this point using Mohr\u0027s circle.","figs":["ex9-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Top and bottom faces: 90 MPa tension (arrows outward). Left and right faces: 20 MPa compression (arrows pointing in). 60 MPa shear: right face upward, left face downward, top pointing right, bottom pointing left. Same element as Example 9.4.","sol":"1) σx = -20, σy = 90, τxy = 60 MPa. 2) Circle center C on the σ axis at σavg = (-20 + 90)/2 = 35 MPa. Reference point A(-20, 60). 3) Radius: R = √(60² + 55²) = 81.4 MPa (shaded right triangle with legs 60 and 35 - (-20) = 55). 4) Maximum in-plane shear stress is the radius, read at the top/bottom points E, F of the circle with coordinates (35, ±81.4): τmax in-plane = 81.4 MPa, with σavg = 35 MPa on the same planes. 5) Orientation: counterclockwise angle from CA to CE: 2θs1 = tan⁻¹[(20 + 35)/60] = 42.5°, so θs1 = 21.3° counterclockwise from the x axis. Point E has positive coordinates, so the average normal stress and the maximum shear both act in the positive x\u0027 and y\u0027 directions respectively.","answers":["τ_max in-plane = 81.4 MPa","σ_avg = 35 MPa","θ_s1 = 21.3° counterclockwise"]},{"id":"9.9","title":"Stresses on an element rotated 30° counterclockwise via Mohr\u0027s circle","q":"The state of plane stress at a point is shown on the element in the figure (σx = -8 ksi, σy = 12 ksi, 6 ksi shear as drawn). Represent this state of stress on an element oriented 30° counterclockwise from the position shown, using Mohr\u0027s circle.","figs":["ex9-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Top and bottom faces: 12 ksi tension (arrows outward). Left and right faces: 8 ksi compression (arrows pointing in). 6 ksi shear: right face pointing down, left face pointing up, top pointing left, bottom pointing right.","sol":"1) Read the element: σx = -8 ksi, σy = 12 ksi, τxy = -6 ksi (right-face shear downward). 2) Circle: center at σavg = (-8 + 12)/2 = 2 ksi. Reference point A(-8, -6) for θ = 0. R = √(10² + 6²) = 11.66 ksi. 3) A 30° physical rotation counterclockwise is 2(30°) = 60° counterclockwise on the circle from CA to a radial line CP. The angle of CA below the σ axis is φ = tan⁻¹(6/10) = 30.96°, so CP makes ψ = 60° - 30.96° = 29.04° with the σ axis (P is below the axis). 4) Coordinates of P (the x\u0027 face): σx\u0027 = 2 - 11.66 cos29.04° = -8.20 ksi; τx\u0027y\u0027 = 11.66 sin29.04° = 5.66 ksi. 5) The adjacent y\u0027 face is at Q, diametrically opposite P: σy\u0027 = 2 + 11.66 cos29.04° = 12.2 ksi; τ = -5.66 ksi (acts in the -y\u0027 direction). Check: -8.20 + 12.2 = 4 = σx + σy.","answers":["σ_x\u0027 = -8.20 ksi, τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 5.66 ksi (face at 30° ccw)","σ_y\u0027 = 12.2 ksi, τ_x\u0027y\u0027 = -5.66 ksi (adjacent face)"]},{"id":"9.10","title":"Absolute maximum shear stress in a pressure vessel wall","q":"The point on the surface of the cylindrical pressure vessel shown in the figure is subjected to the state of plane stress shown on the stress element (32 MPa in the vertical/hoop direction and 16 MPa in the horizontal/axial direction, both tension). Determine the absolute maximum shear stress at this point.","figs":["ex9-10.webp"],"figDesc":"A horizontal cylindrical pressure vessel on two saddle supports with a small cubic element at a point on its upper surface. The element\u0027s vertical arrows carry 32 MPa tension (hoop direction, up and down) and the horizontal arrows carry 16 MPa tension (axial direction, left and right). The stress in the radial direction through the thickness is zero (plane stress).","sol":"1) Principal in-plane stresses: σ1 = 32 MPa, σ2 = 16 MPa, and the out-of-plane (radial) principal stress is σ3 = 0 (surface, plane stress). 2) Plot the three Mohr\u0027s circles on the σ axis between 0, 16 and 32 MPa. The largest circle spans σ = 0 to 32 MPa (the plane containing σ1 and the zero stress), radius 16 MPa. 3) Both principal stresses are positive (same sign), so the absolute maximum shear is NOT the in-plane value: τabs max = σmax/2 = 32/2 = 16 MPa, with average normal stress σavg = (32 + 0)/2 = 16 MPa, on an element rotated 45° about the axis of σ2. 4) For comparison the maximum in-plane shear is (32 - 16)/2 = 8 MPa, with σavg = 24 MPa (smaller).","answers":["τ_abs max = 16 MPa","σ_avg = 16 MPa"]},{"id":"9.11","title":"Principal stresses and absolute maximum shear stress at a machine shaft point","q":"Due to an applied loading, an element at the point on a machine shaft is subjected to the state of stress shown in the figure (20 psi compression on the left and right faces, 40 psi shear). Determine the principal stresses and the absolute maximum shear stress at the point.","figs":["ex9-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Square stress element. Left and right faces: 20 psi compression (arrows pointing into the element). 40 psi shear: right face pointing down, left face pointing up, top face pointing left, bottom face pointing right. No normal stress on the top and bottom faces.","sol":"1) Read the element: σx = -20 psi, σy = 0, τxy = -40 psi. 2) Mohr\u0027s circle: center at σavg = (-20 + 0)/2 = -10 psi. Reference point A(-20, -40). Radius R = √[(20-10)² + 40²] = 41.2 psi. 3) In-plane principal stresses: σ1 = -10 + 41.2 = 31.2 psi, σ2 = -10 - 41.2 = -51.2 psi. 4) Orientation: counterclockwise angle on the circle from CA to the -σ axis, 2θ = tan⁻¹[40/(20-10)] = 76.0°, so θ = 38.0° counterclockwise from x to the x\u0027 axis, which is the direction of σ2 = -51.2 psi (σ1 = 31.2 psi acts on the perpendicular face). 5) Absolute maximum shear: the in-plane principal stresses have opposite signs (and the third principal stress is 0, between them), so τabs max = (σ1 - σ2)/2 = [31.2 - (-51.2)]/2 = 41.2 psi, with σavg = (31.2 - 51.2)/2 = -10 psi. (Equal to the in-plane maximum shear.)","answers":["σ1 = 31.2 psi","σ2 = -51.2 psi (at θ = 38.0° ccw from x)","τ_abs max = 41.2 psi","σ_avg = -10 psi"]},{"id":"10.1","title":"Plane-strain element rotated 30° clockwise: equivalent strains","q":"A differential element of material at a point is subjected to a state of plane strain ε_x = 500(10⁻⁶), ε_y = −300(10⁻⁶), γ_xy = 200(10⁻⁶), which tends to distort the element as shown in the figure. Determine the equivalent strains (ε_x\u0027, γ_x\u0027y\u0027, ε_y\u0027) acting on an element of the material oriented at the point, clockwise 30° from the original position.","figs":["ex10-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Square differential element with sides dx (horizontal) and dy (vertical) in x-y axes. Dashed outline shows the distorted shape: right face moved out by ε_x dx, top moved up by ε_y dy, and both the bottom edge and left edge rotated by γ_xy/2 (positive shear strain, right angle at the origin corner decreased).","sol":"1) Use the plane-strain transformation equations; θ is positive counterclockwise, so clockwise 30° gives θ = −30°, 2θ = −60°. 2) ε_x\u0027 = (ε_x+ε_y)/2 + (ε_x−ε_y)/2·cos2θ + (γ_xy/2)·sin2θ = 100 + 400(0.5) + 100(−0.866) = 213(10⁻⁶). 3) γ_x\u0027y\u0027/2 = −(ε_x−ε_y)/2·sin2θ + (γ_xy/2)·cos2θ = −400(−0.866) + 100(0.5) = 396.4(10⁻⁶), so γ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 793(10⁻⁶). 4) For ε_y\u0027 use the same normal-strain equation with θ = −30° + 90° = 60° (2θ = 120°): ε_y\u0027 = 100 + 400cos120° + 100 sin120° = 100 − 200 + 86.6 = −13.4(10⁻⁶). Check: ε_x\u0027+ε_y\u0027 = ε_x+ε_y = 200(10⁻⁶).","answers":["ε_x\u0027 = 213(10⁻⁶)","γ_x\u0027y\u0027 = 793(10⁻⁶)","ε_y\u0027 = −13.4(10⁻⁶)"]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Principal strains and orientation for a plane-strain state","q":"A differential element of material at a point is subjected to a state of plane strain defined by ε_x = −350(10⁻⁶), ε_y = 200(10⁻⁶), γ_xy = 80(10⁻⁶), which tends to distort the element as shown in the figure. Determine the principal strains at the point and the associated orientation of the element.","figs":["ex10-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Square differential element with sides dx and dy in x-y axes. Dashed outline shows distortion: ε_x dx change in the horizontal side, ε_y dy change in the vertical side, and shear distortion with angles γ_xy/2 at the bottom edge and left edge (positive shear strain).","sol":"1) Orientation: tan2θ_p = γ_xy/(ε_x−ε_y) = 80/(−350−200) = −0.1455, so 2θ_p = −8.28° and 171.72°, giving θ_p = −4.14° and 85.9° (counterclockwise positive from x). 2) Principal strains: ε_avg = (−350+200)/2 = −75; R = √[((−350−200)/2)² + (80/2)²] = √(275² + 40²) = 277.9. ε_1 = −75 + 277.9 = 203(10⁻⁶); ε_2 = −75 − 277.9 = −353(10⁻⁶). 3) Identify which angle goes with which strain by plugging θ = −4.14° into the ε_x\u0027 equation: ε_x\u0027 = −75 + (−275)cos(−8.28°) + 40 sin(−8.28°) = −353(10⁻⁶) = ε_2. So θ_p = −4.14° is the direction of ε_2 and 85.9° is the direction of ε_1.","answers":["θ_p = −4.14° and 85.9°","ε_1 = 203(10⁻⁶)","ε_2 = −353(10⁻⁶)"]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Maximum in-plane shear strain for a plane-strain state","q":"A differential element of material at a point is subjected to a state of plane strain defined by ε_x = −350(10⁻⁶), ε_y = 200(10⁻⁶), γ_xy = 80(10⁻⁶), which tends to distort the element as shown in the figure. Determine the maximum in-plane shear strain at the point and the associated orientation of the element.","figs":["ex10-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Square differential element OAB with O at the origin, A on the y axis and B on the x axis, sides dx and dy. Dashed outline shows the distorted shape: ε_x dx extension of the horizontal side, ε_y dy extension of the vertical side, and shear angles γ_xy/2 at the two edges (positive shear strain).","sol":"1) Orientation: tan2θ_s = −(ε_x−ε_y)/γ_xy = −(−550)/80 = 6.875, so 2θ_s = 81.72° and 261.72°, θ_s = 40.9° and 131° (45° from the principal directions of Example 10.2). 2) Max in-plane shear: γ_max/2 = √[((ε_x−ε_y)/2)² + (γ_xy/2)²] = √(275² + 40²) = 277.9, so γ_max(in-plane) = 556(10⁻⁶). 3) Sign: evaluate γ_x\u0027y\u0027/2 = −(ε_x−ε_y)/2·sin2θ + (γ_xy/2)cos2θ at θ = 40.9°: +278, positive, so γ_x\u0027y\u0027 = +556(10⁻⁶) (right angle between dx\u0027 and dy\u0027 decreases). 4) Associated average normal strain: ε_avg = (−350+200)/2 = −75(10⁻⁶) (element contracts).","answers":["θ_s = 40.9° and 131°","γ_max(in-plane) = 556(10⁻⁶)","ε_avg = −75(10⁻⁶)"]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Mohr\u0027s circle: principal strains for plane strain","q":"The state of plane strain at a point is represented by the components ε_x = 250(10⁻⁶), ε_y = −150(10⁻⁶), and γ_xy = 120(10⁻⁶). Determine the principal strains and the orientation of the element.","figs":[],"figDesc":"No problem figure; the strain components are given in the statement. (The book\u0027s Mohr\u0027s circle is part of the solution.)","sol":"1) Use Mohr\u0027s circle for strain with ε on the horizontal axis (positive right) and γ/2 on the vertical axis (positive downward). 2) Center C: ε_avg = (250 + (−150))/2 = 50(10⁻⁶). Reference point A (θ = 0°) = (ε_x, γ_xy/2) = (250, 60). 3) Radius R = √((250−50)² + 60²) = 208.8(10⁻⁶). 4) Principal strains are the ε-coordinates where the circle crosses the ε axis: ε_1 = 50 + 208.8 = 259(10⁻⁶); ε_2 = 50 − 208.8 = −159(10⁻⁶). 5) Orientation: the counterclockwise angle from CA to the ε_1 point is 2θ_p1 with tan2θ_p1 = 60/(250−50) = 0.3, so 2θ_p1 = 16.7°, θ_p1 = 8.35° counterclockwise (the x\u0027 side of the element, which carries ε_1, is rotated 8.35° ccw from x).","answers":["ε_1 = 259(10⁻⁶)","ε_2 = −159(10⁻⁶)","θ_p1 = 8.35° counterclockwise"]},{"id":"10.5","title":"Mohr\u0027s circle: maximum in-plane shear strain","q":"The state of plane strain at a point is represented by the components ε_x = 250(10⁻⁶), ε_y = −150(10⁻⁶), and γ_xy = 120(10⁻⁶). Determine the maximum in-plane shear strain and the orientation of an element.","figs":[],"figDesc":"No problem figure; the strain components are given in the statement. (The book\u0027s Mohr\u0027s circle and element sketches are part of the solution.)","sol":"1) Draw Mohr\u0027s circle (ε horizontal, γ/2 vertical downward): center ε_avg = 50(10⁻⁶); reference point A = (250, 60); R = √(200² + 60²) = 208.8(10⁻⁶). 2) Maximum in-plane shear is the top/bottom point of the circle (point E or F): γ_max/2 = R = 208.8, so γ_max(in-plane) = 418(10⁻⁶), accompanied by ε_avg = 50(10⁻⁶). 3) Orientation: from CA (θ = 0) to CE is clockwise 90° − 2θ_p1 where 2θ_p1 = 16.7° (tan = 60/200), so 2θ_s1 = 90° − 2(8.35°) = 73.3° clockwise, θ_s1 = 36.7° clockwise. 4) Point E has positive shear and positive average normal strain, so the element expands on average and the right angle between the x\u0027 and y\u0027 faces decreases.","answers":["(γ_x\u0027y\u0027)max,in-plane = 418(10⁻⁶)","ε_avg = 50(10⁻⁶)","θ_s1 = 36.7° clockwise"]},{"id":"10.6","title":"Mohr\u0027s circle: strain on element rotated 20° clockwise","q":"The state of plane strain at a point is represented on an element having components ε_x = −300(10⁻⁶), ε_y = −100(10⁻⁶), and γ_xy = 100(10⁻⁶). Determine the state of strain on an element oriented 20° clockwise from this reported position.","figs":[],"figDesc":"No problem figure; the strain components are given in the statement. (The book\u0027s Mohr\u0027s circle is part of the solution.)","sol":"1) Mohr\u0027s circle: ε_avg = (−300−100)/2 = −200(10⁻⁶). Reference point A = (−300, 50). R = √((300−200)² + 50²) = 111.8(10⁻⁶). 2) Rotating the element 20° clockwise means rotating the radial line 2(20°) = 40° clockwise from CA to CP. 3) Angle between CA and the ε axis: φ = tan⁻¹(50/100) = 26.57°; the radial line CP makes ψ = 40° − 26.57° = 13.43° with the ε axis. 4) ε_x\u0027 = −(200 + 111.8cos13.43°) = −309(10⁻⁶). γ_x\u0027y\u0027/2 = −111.8 sin13.43° = −26.0, so γ_x\u0027y\u0027 = −52.0(10⁻⁶). 5) ε_y\u0027 from the opposite point Q (since ε_x\u0027 + ε_y\u0027 = −400): ε_y\u0027 = −(200 − 111.8cos13.43°) = −91.3(10⁻⁶).","answers":["ε_x\u0027 = −309(10⁻⁶)","γ_x\u0027y\u0027 = −52.0(10⁻⁶)","ε_y\u0027 = −91.3(10⁻⁶)"]},{"id":"10.7","title":"Maximum in-plane and absolute maximum shear strain","q":"The state of plane strain at a point is represented by the strain components ε_x = −400(10⁻⁶), ε_y = 200(10⁻⁶), γ_xy = 150(10⁻⁶). Determine the maximum in-plane shear strain and the absolute maximum shear strain.","figs":[],"figDesc":"No problem figure; the strain components are given in the statement. (The book\u0027s three Mohr\u0027s circles are part of the solution.)","sol":"1) Plane strain: ε_z = 0 is the third principal strain. 2) Mohr\u0027s circle for the x-y plane: ε_avg = (−400+200)/2 = −100(10⁻⁶); reference point A = (−400, 75); R = √(300² + 75²) = 309(10⁻⁶). 3) In-plane principal strains: ε_1 = −100 + 309 = 209(10⁻⁶), ε_2 = −100 − 309 = −409(10⁻⁶). 4) Max in-plane shear = ε_1 − ε_2 = 618(10⁻⁶). 5) Absolute maximum shear: the three principal strains are 209, 0, −409; since the in-plane principal strains have opposite signs (the third, 0, lies between them), the largest circle is the in-plane one, so γ_abs max = 618(10⁻⁶) as well.","answers":["γ_max(in-plane) = 618(10⁻⁶)","γ_abs max = 618(10⁻⁶)"]},{"id":"10.8","title":"Strain rosette on bracket: in-plane principal strains","q":"The state of strain at point A on the bracket shown in the figure is measured using the strain rosette. Gauges a, b, c are oriented at θ_a = 0°, θ_b = 60°, θ_c = 120°, measured counterclockwise from the +x axis (a 60° rosette). Due to the loadings, the gauge readings give ε_a = 60(10⁻⁶), ε_b = 135(10⁻⁶), and ε_c = 264(10⁻⁶). Determine the in-plane principal strains at the point and the directions in which they act.","figs":["ex10-8.webp"],"figDesc":"(a) Bracket (a post fixed to a wall with a lever arm carrying two chain loads, one pulling up-right and one pulling down) with point A on the post surface where a three-gauge rosette a, b, c is mounted. (b) Rosette layout in the x-y plane: gauge a lies along the +x axis, gauge b is at 60° counterclockwise from x, gauge c at 120° counterclockwise from x.","sol":"1) Write ε = ε_x cos²θ + ε_y sin²θ + γ_xy sinθ cosθ for each gauge. a (0°): ε_x = 60(10⁻⁶). b (60°): 135 = 0.25ε_x + 0.75ε_y + 0.433γ_xy. c (120°): 264 = 0.25ε_x + 0.75ε_y − 0.433γ_xy. 2) Solving: ε_x = 60, ε_y = 246, γ_xy = −149 (all ×10⁻⁶) (matches Eq. 10–17). 3) Mohr\u0027s circle: ε_avg = (60+246)/2 = 153; reference point A = (60, γ_xy/2 = −74.5); R = √(93² + 74.5²) = 119.1. 4) ε_1 = 153 + 119.1 = 272(10⁻⁶); ε_2 = 153 − 119.1 = 33.9(10⁻⁶). 5) 2θ_p2 = tan⁻¹(74.5/(153−60)) = 38.7°, so θ_p2 = 19.3° (direction of ε_2, measured counterclockwise from x per the book\u0027s figure; ε_1 acts 90° from it). Note an out-of-plane strain also exists (Poisson) but does not affect the result.","answers":["ε_1 = 272(10⁻⁶)","ε_2 = 33.9(10⁻⁶)","θ_p2 = 19.3° (direction of ε_2; ε_1 is perpendicular)"]},{"id":"10.9","title":"Principal stresses at bracket surface from measured strains","q":"The bracket (shown in the figure) is made of steel for which E_st = 200 GPa and ν_st = 0.3. Point A is on the free surface of the bracket, where strain gauges gave the in-plane principal strains ε_1 = 272(10⁻⁶) and ε_2 = 33.9(10⁻⁶) (equivalently ε_x = 60(10⁻⁶), ε_y = 246(10⁻⁶), γ_xy = −149(10⁻⁶) in the x-y axes of the rosette). Determine the principal stresses at point A.","figs":["ex10-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Bracket: a post fixed to a wall with a lever arm carrying two chain loads (one pulling up-right, one pulling down). Point A is on the surface of the post where gauges a, b, c of a strain rosette are attached.","sol":"1) Point A is on a free (unloaded) surface, so it is in plane stress: σ_3 = 0. 2) Hooke\u0027s law with principal strains: ε_1 = σ_1/E − νσ_2/E and ε_2 = σ_2/E − νσ_1/E. With E = 200(10⁹) Pa: 54.4(10⁶) = σ_1 − 0.3σ_2 and 6.78(10⁶) = σ_2 − 0.3σ_1. 3) Solve simultaneously: σ_1 = 62.0 MPa, σ_2 = 25.4 MPa. Alternative check: from ε_x, ε_y get σ_x = 29.4 MPa, σ_y = 58.0 MPa; G = E/[2(1+ν)] = 76.9 GPa, τ_xy = Gγ_xy = −11.46 MPa; Mohr\u0027s circle center 43.7 MPa, R = √(14.3² + 11.46²) = 18.3 MPa, giving the same σ_1 = 62.0 and σ_2 = 25.4 MPa. Valid because material is linear elastic and isotropic.","answers":["σ_1 = 62.0 MPa","σ_2 = 25.4 MPa"]},{"id":"10.10","title":"Copper bar under biaxial edge loading: new dimensions","q":"The copper bar shown in the figure is subjected to a uniform loading along its edges: 800 MPa tension on the two end faces (along length a) and 500 MPa compression on the two side faces (along width b). The bar has length a = 300 mm, width b = 50 mm, and thickness t = 20 mm before the load is applied. Determine its new length, width, and thickness after application of the load. Take E_cu = 120 GPa, ν_cu = 0.34.","figs":["ex10-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular copper bar in 3D with length a (horizontal), width b (vertical in the front face), and thickness t (into the page). Uniform 800 MPa arrows pull outward on the left and right end faces; uniform 500 MPa arrows push inward on the top and bottom long faces. Front and back faces are unloaded.","sol":"1) Faces perpendicular to thickness are free, so plane stress: σ_x = +800 MPa (along a), σ_y = −500 MPa (along b), τ_xy = 0, σ_z = 0. 2) Generalized Hooke\u0027s law with E = 120(10³) MPa, ν = 0.34: ε_x = σ_x/E − (ν/E)(σ_y+σ_z) = 800/120000 − 0.34(−500)/120000 = 0.00808. ε_y = −500/120000 − 0.34(800)/120000 = −0.00643. ε_z = 0 − 0.34(800 − 500)/120000 = −0.000850. 3) New dimensions: a\u0027 = 300 + 0.00808(300) = 302.4 mm; b\u0027 = 50 + (−0.00643)(50) = 49.68 mm; t\u0027 = 20 + (−0.000850)(20) = 19.98 mm.","answers":["a\u0027 = 302.4 mm","b\u0027 = 49.68 mm","t\u0027 = 19.98 mm"]},{"id":"10.11","title":"Rubber block under uniform pressure: dilatation and side changes","q":"The rectangular block shown in the figure, with sides a = 4 in., b = 2 in., c = 3 in., is subjected to a uniform pressure p = 20 psi on all faces. Determine the dilatation and the change in length of each side. Take E = 600 psi, ν = 0.45.","figs":["ex10-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular block in 3D pictorial view with dimensions a = 4 in. (along the front-bottom edge), b = 2 in. (receding bottom-right edge), and c = 3 in. (vertical edge).","sol":"1) Uniform pressure on all faces: σ_x = σ_y = σ_z = −20 psi. 2) Dilatation: e = [(1−2ν)/E](σ_x+σ_y+σ_z) = [(1−0.9)/600](−60) = −0.01 in³/in³. 3) Normal strain on each side (Hooke\u0027s law): ε = (1/E)[σ_x − ν(σ_y+σ_z)] = (1/600)[−20 − 0.45(−40)] = −0.00333 in./in. 4) Changes in length: δa = −0.00333(4) = −0.0133 in.; δb = −0.00333(2) = −0.00667 in.; δc = −0.00333(3) = −0.0100 in. Negative signs mean each dimension decreases.","answers":["e = −0.01 in³/in³","δa = −0.0133 in.","δb = −0.00667 in.","δc = −0.0100 in."]},{"id":"10.12","title":"Cast-iron shaft under torque: smallest radius, max-normal-stress theory","q":"The solid cast-iron shaft shown in the figure is subjected to a torque of T = 400 lb·ft. Determine its smallest radius so that it does not fail according to the maximum-normal-stress theory. A specimen of cast iron, tested in tension, has an ultimate stress of (σ_ult)_t = 20 ksi.","figs":["ex10-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Solid circular shaft of radius r (unknown) with equal and opposite torques T = 400 lb·ft applied at its two ends.","sol":"1) Critical stress is at the surface: τ_max = Tc/J = T r/[(π/2)r⁴] = 2T/(πr³) = (400 lb·ft)(12 in./ft)/[(π/2)r³] = 3055.8 lb·in./r³ (r in inches). 2) Pure shear: Mohr\u0027s circle is centered at the origin with R = τ_max, so σ_1 = −σ_2 = τ_max. 3) Maximum-normal-stress theory: |σ_1| ≤ σ_ult, so 3055.8/r³ ≤ 20 000 psi. 4) Smallest radius: r³ = 3055.8/20 000 = 0.1528, r = 0.535 in.","answers":["r = 0.535 in."]},{"id":"10.13","title":"Shaft under axial load and torque: failure by two yield theories","q":"The solid shaft shown in the figure has a radius of 0.5 in. and is made of steel having a yield stress of σ_Y = 36 ksi. It carries an axial compressive force of 15 kip and a torque of 3.25 kip·in. Determine if the loadings cause the shaft to fail according to the maximum-shear-stress theory and the maximum-distortion-energy theory.","figs":["ex10-13.webp"],"figDesc":"Solid cylindrical shaft fixed to a wall at the far end. At the free end: an axial force of 15 kip acting along the shaft axis pushing toward the shaft (compression), a torque of 3.25 kip·in. (curved arrow), and the radius labeled 0.5 in. A point A on the outer surface is marked with a small square.","sol":"1) Stresses at surface point A: σ_x = P/A = −15/[π(0.5)²] = −19.10 ksi (compression, σ_y = 0); τ_xy = Tc/J = 3.25(0.5)/[(π/2)(0.5)⁴] = 16.55 ksi. 2) Principal stresses: σ_1,2 = (−19.10)/2 ± √[(19.10/2)² + 16.55²] = −9.55 ± 19.11, so σ_1 = 9.56 ksi, σ_2 = −28.66 ksi (opposite signs). 3) Max-shear-stress theory (opposite signs): |σ_1 − σ_2| ≤ σ_Y? 9.56 − (−28.66) = 38.2 ksi \u003e 36 ksi, so failure (yielding) occurs. 4) Max-distortion-energy theory: σ_1² − σ_1σ_2 + σ_2² ≤ σ_Y²? 9.56² + 9.56(28.66) + 28.66² = 1187 \u003c 36² = 1296, so no failure by this theory.","answers":["Max-shear-stress theory: 38.2 ksi \u003e 36 ksi, shaft fails","Max-distortion-energy theory: 1187 \u003c 1296, shaft does not fail"]},{"id":"11.1","title":"Overhanging steel beam: select a W shape for bending and shear","q":"A beam is to be made of steel that has an allowable bending stress of σ_allow = 24 ksi and an allowable shear stress of τ_allow = 14.5 ksi. Select an appropriate W shape (wide-flange section, using the table in Appendix B) that will carry the loading shown in the figure.","figs":["ex11-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam 18 ft long with a pin support at the left end and a roller support 12 ft from the left end. A 40 kip downward point load acts 6 ft from the left end (midway between the supports), and a 20 kip downward point load acts at the free right end (6 ft beyond the roller). Three 6 ft dimensions are marked along the beam.","sol":"1) Reactions: ΣM about the pin: R_roller(12) = 40(6) + 20(18) = 600, so roller reaction = 50 kip up and pin reaction = 10 kip up. 2) Shear and moment diagrams: V = +10 kip for 0–6 ft, −30 kip for 6–12 ft, jump of +50 to +20 kip at the roller, then 20 kip to the end. Moment: 60 kip·ft at the 40 kip load, −120 kip·ft at the roller. So V_max = 30 kip and M_max = 120 kip·ft. 3) Required section modulus: S_req\u0027d = M_max/σ_allow = 120(12)/24 = 60 in³. 4) From the Appendix B table, adequate shapes (S ≥ 60 in³) include W18×40 (68.4), W16×45 (72.7), W14×43 (62.7), W12×50 (64.7), W10×54 (60.0), W8×67 (60.4). Choose the lightest per foot: W18×40. 5) Beam weight (0.040 kip/ft)(18 ft) = 0.72 kip is slight and S_req\u0027d = 60 \u003c 68.4 in³ stays OK. 6) Shear check with the web area taken over the full depth: d = 17.90 in., t_w = 0.315 in.; τ_avg = V_max/A_w = 30/[(17.90)(0.315)] = 5.32 ksi \u003c 14.5 ksi OK. Ans: use W18×40.","answers":["W18×40","S_req\u0027d = 60 in³, M_max = 120 kip·ft, V_max = 30 kip","τ_avg = 5.32 ksi \u003c 14.5 ksi"]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Laminated wood beam: smallest width for bending and shear","q":"The laminated wooden beam shown in the figure supports a uniform distributed loading of 12 kN/m. If the beam is to have a height-to-width ratio of 1.5, determine its smallest width. The allowable bending stress is σ_allow = 9 MPa and the allowable shear stress is τ_allow = 0.6 MPa. Neglect the weight of the beam.","figs":["ex11-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Rectangular laminated wooden beam of width a and height 1.5a (shown by a cross-section detail at the left end). The beam is 4 m long, resting on two supports: support A is 1 m from the left end and support B is at the right end, 3 m from A. A uniform downward load of 12 kN/m acts over the full 4 m length.","sol":"1) Reactions: total load 12(4) = 48 kN; ΣM_B = 0 gives A_y = 32 kN and B_y = 16 kN. From the diagrams V_max = 20 kN (just right of A) and M_max = 10.67 kN·m (at 1.33 m from B). 2) Bending: S_req\u0027d = M_max/σ_allow = 10.67(10³)/9(10⁶) = 0.00119 m³. With width a and height 1.5a: S = I/c = [(1/12)(a)(1.5a)³]/(0.75a) = 0.375a³, so a³ = 0.003160 m³ and a = 0.147 m. 3) Shear check (rectangular section): τ_max = 1.5V_max/A = 1.5(20 × 10³)/[(0.147)(1.5)(0.147)] = 0.929 MPa \u003e 0.6 MPa, so shear governs. 4) Redesign on shear: τ_allow = 1.5V_max/A: 600 kN/m² = 1.5(20 × 10³)/[(a)(1.5a)], so a = 0.183 m. This larger section also satisfies bending. Ans: smallest width a = 183 mm (height 1.5a = 274 mm).","answers":["a = 183 mm (shear governs)","Bending alone would need a = 0.147 m, giving τ_max = 0.929 MPa \u003e 0.6 MPa"]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Nailed wooden T-beam: safety check and maximum nail spacing","q":"The wooden T-beam shown in the figure is made from two 200 mm × 30 mm boards. If the allowable bending stress is σ_allow = 12 MPa and the allowable shear stress is τ_allow = 0.8 MPa, determine if the beam can safely support the loading shown. Also, specify the maximum spacing of nails needed to hold the two boards together if each nail can safely resist 1.50 kN in shear. The beam is simply supported (support B at the left, support D at the right) over 4 m; it carries a uniform load of 0.5 kN/m over the first 2 m (B to C) and a 1.5 kN downward point load at C, 2 m from B. The T-section has a 200 mm wide, 30 mm thick flange board nailed on top of a 30 mm wide, 200 mm deep web board.","figs":["ex11-3.webp","ex11-3b.webp"],"figDesc":"First figure: simply supported beam, 4 m long, roller at B (left) and pin at D (right); point C at midspan (2 m from each support). A uniform downward load of 0.5 kN/m acts from B to C (2 m) and a 1.5 kN downward point load acts at C. Second figure: wooden T cross-section; flange board 200 mm wide by 30 mm thick on top, nailed (nail drawn vertically) to a web board 30 mm wide and 200 mm tall below it; y-bar is measured from the bottom of the web to the centroid.","sol":"1) Reactions: B_y = 1.5 kN, D_y = 1 kN (ΣM_D: 4B_y = 0.5(2)(3) + 1.5(2) = 6 kN·m); the diagrams give V_max = 1.5 kN (in BC, starting at 1.5 kN) and M_max = 2 kN·m (at C); V = −1 kN in CD. 2) Centroid from the bottom: ȳ = [0.1(0.03)(0.2) + 0.215(0.03)(0.2)]/[0.03(0.2) + 0.03(0.2)] = 0.1575 m. 3) I = [(1/12)(0.03)(0.2)³ + (0.03)(0.2)(0.1575 − 0.1)²] + [(1/12)(0.2)(0.03)³ + (0.2)(0.03)(0.215 − 0.1575)²] = 60.125(10⁻⁶) m⁴. 4) Bending: c = 0.1575 m (bottom, farther than 0.0725 m at top): σ = M_max c/I = 2(10³)(0.1575)/60.125(10⁻⁶) = 5.24 MPa \u003c 12 MPa OK. 5) Shear: max at the NA, in the web (t = 0.03 m); use the area below the NA: Q = (0.1575/2)(0.1575)(0.03) = 0.372(10⁻³) m³; τ = V_max Q/(It) = 1.5(10³)(0.372 × 10⁻³)/[60.125(10⁻⁶)(0.03)] = 309 kPa \u003c 800 kPa OK. 6) Nail spacing from the shear flow at the flange-web joint: Q = ȳ′A′ = (0.0725 − 0.015)(0.2)(0.03) = 0.345(10⁻³) m³. q_BC = 1.5(10³)(0.345 × 10⁻³)/60.125(10⁻⁶) = 8.61 kN/m; q_CD = 1(10³)(0.345 × 10⁻³)/60.125(10⁻⁶) = 5.74 kN/m. s = (1.50 kN)/q: s_BC = 0.174 m, s_CD = 0.261 m. Round down to convenient values: s_BC = 150 mm, s_CD = 250 mm.","answers":["Beam is safe: σ = 5.24 MPa \u003c 12 MPa and τ = 309 kPa \u003c 800 kPa","s_BC = 150 mm","s_CD = 250 mm"]},{"id":"11.4","title":"Fully stressed simply supported beam: depth profile for central load","q":"Determine the shape of a fully stressed, simply supported beam that supports a concentrated force P at its center. The beam has a rectangular cross section of constant width b, and the allowable stress is σ_allow. The span is L, with the force P acting downward at midspan (L/2 from each support), and x is measured from the left support. Let h be the depth of the beam at distance x and h_0 the depth at the center. Express h as a function of x (0 ≤ x ≤ L/2) in terms of h_0 and L.","figs":[],"figDesc":"No figure is shown (the book figure draws the beam profile, which is the answer). Set-up: simply supported beam of span L, supported by a pin at the right end and a roller at the left end, with a concentrated downward force P at midspan (L/2 on each side). The cross section is rectangular with constant width b and a depth h that varies along the length (h at distance x from the left support, h_0 at the center); the beam is symmetric about midspan.","sol":"1) Left half free-body: reaction P/2 at the support, so the internal moment is M = (P/2)x for 0 ≤ x \u003c L/2. 2) Fully stressed means σ = σ_allow at every section, so the required section modulus is S = M/σ_allow = Px/(2σ_allow). 3) For a rectangle b × h: S = I/c = [(1/12)bh³]/(h/2) = bh²/6. Equate: bh²/6 = Px/(2σ_allow), so h² = 3Px/(σ_allow b). 4) At x = L/2, h = h₀: h₀² = 3PL/(2σ_allow b). Dividing, h² = (2h₀²/L)x. 5) The depth varies parabolically with x (h = 0 at the supports in theory; in practice h \u003e 0 is needed there to resist shear). This is the basis of leaf springs. Ans: h² = (2h₀²/L)x.","answers":["h² = (2h₀²/L) x for 0 ≤ x ≤ L/2 (parabolic variation)"]},{"id":"11.5","title":"Tapered cantilever with end load: absolute maximum bending stress","q":"The cantilevered beam shown in the figure is formed into a trapezoidal shape having a depth h₀ at A and a depth 3h₀ at B. If it supports a load P at its end, determine the absolute maximum normal stress in the beam. The beam has a rectangular cross section of constant width b and length L.","figs":["ex11-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever of length L fixed to a wall at the right end B, free at the left end A where a downward force P acts. Constant width b (rectangular section shown at the right). The top edge is straight; the bottom edge slopes down linearly so that the depth is h₀ at A and 3h₀ at B. At a distance x from A the depth is h.","sol":"1) At a section x from A the internal moment magnitude is M = Px. 2) Depth varies linearly: h = h₀ + (2h₀/L)x = (h₀/L)(2x + L). 3) Maximum stress at a section is at the top/bottom face: σ = Mc/I = Px(h/2)/[(1/12)bh³] = 6Px/(bh²) = 6PL²x/[bh₀²(2x + L)²]. 4) The absolute maximum is not necessarily at the wall: set dσ/dx = 0: (2x + L)² − x(2)(2x + L)(2) = 0, which simplifies to L² − 4x² = 0, so x = L/2. 5) Substitute x = L/2: σ_abs max = 6PL²(L/2)/[bh₀²(2L)²] = (3/4)PL/(bh₀²). 6) For comparison, at the wall B: σ = PL(1.5h₀)/[(1/12)b(3h₀)³] = (2/3)PL/(bh₀²), which is 11.1% smaller. Ans: σ_abs max = (3/4)PL/(bh₀²), at x = L/2 from the free end.","answers":["σ_abs max = (3/4) PL/(b h₀²) at x = L/2","At the wall B: σ = (2/3) PL/(b h₀²) (11.1% smaller)"]},{"id":"11.6","title":"Pulley shaft with belt tensions: smallest diameter, max-shear theory","q":"The shaft shown in the figure is supported by smooth journal bearings at A and B. Due to the transmission of power to and from the shaft, the belts on the pulleys are subjected to the tensions shown. Determine the smallest diameter of the shaft using the maximum-shear-stress theory, with τ_allow = 50 MPa.","figs":["ex11-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Shaft along the y axis (z is vertical) with journal bearings at A and B and two pulleys: pulley C (radius 0.050 m) between the bearings and pulley D (radius 0.075 m) overhanging beyond B. Distances along the shaft: A to C 0.250 m, C to B 0.250 m, B to D 0.150 m. At C the belt strands pull vertically downward with 550 N and 400 N. At D the belt strands pull in the horizontal x direction (upward-right in the picture) with 300 N and 200 N.","sol":"1) Reactions and free-body diagram of the shaft (from the book, found by statics): vertical forces: A 475 N up, C 950 N down (550 + 400), B 475 N up. Horizontal (x) forces: A 150 N, B 650 N, D 500 N (300 + 200). Torques: at C (550 − 400)(0.05) = 7.5 N·m, at D (300 − 200)(0.075) = 7.5 N·m, balanced. 2) Bending-moment diagrams: M_x (vertical-plane) peaks at C: 475(0.250) = 118.75 N·m; M_z (horizontal-plane) is 150(0.250) = 37.5 N·m at C and 75 N·m at B. Torque diagram: T = 7.5 N·m between C and D. 3) Critical sections are just right of C or at B. At C: M_C = √(118.75² + 37.5²) = 124.5 N·m; at B: M_B = 75 N·m (smaller). Torque 7.5 N·m applies at C (right of C). 4) Maximum-shear-stress theory (Eq. 11–2): c = [(2/(πτ_allow))√(M² + T²)]^(1/3) = [2/(π(50 × 10⁶)) · √(124.5² + 7.5²)]^(1/3) = 0.0117 m. 5) d = 2c = 23.3 mm. Ans: d = 23.3 mm.","answers":["M_C = 124.5 N·m (critical), T = 7.5 N·m","c = 0.0117 m","d = 23.3 mm"]},{"id":"12.1","title":"Cantilever with end load: equation of elastic curve","q":"The cantilevered beam shown in the figure is subjected to a vertical load P at its end. Determine the equation of the elastic curve. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever beam AB of length L, fixed (wall) at the right end B, free at the left end A. Downward vertical load P at the free end A. Axes: x measured from A to the right, v positive upward. (The figure also draws the deflected shape with v_A, θ_A and the label \u0027Elastic curve\u0027.)","sol":"1) Take x from the free end A; a single x coordinate covers the whole beam. 2) Cut at x: M = −Px. 3) EI v\u0027\u0027 = −Px. Integrate: EI v\u0027 = −Px²/2 + C1; EI v = −Px³/6 + C1 x + C2. 4) Boundary conditions at the fixed end x = L: v\u0027 = 0 and v = 0. From the first: C1 = PL²/2. From the second: C2 = −PL³/3. 5) θ = dv/dx = P/(2EI)(L² − x²); v = P/(6EI)(−x³ + 3L²x − 2L³). 6) Max slope and deflection at A (x = 0): θ_A = PL²/(2EI) (counterclockwise), v_A = −PL³/(3EI) (downward).","answers":["θ = P(L² − x²)/(2EI)","v = P(−x³ + 3L²x − 2L³)/(6EI)","θ_A = PL²/(2EI)","v_A = −PL³/(3EI)"]},{"id":"12.2","title":"Simply supported beam, triangular load: maximum deflection","q":"The simply supported beam shown in the figure supports the triangular distributed loading. Determine its maximum deflection. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam of span L (roller at left end, pin at right end). Symmetric triangular distributed load acting downward, zero at both ends and peak intensity w0 at midspan. Span is marked as two halves, L/2 and L/2; x is measured from the left end. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) By symmetry the maximum deflection is at midspan and only 0 ≤ x ≤ L/2 is needed. Each reaction = w0L/4. 2) Load on left half: w = 2w0x/L. Cut at x: resultant of triangle = w0x²/L acting x/3 from the cut. ΣM = 0: M = −w0x³/(3L) + w0Lx/4. 3) EI v\u0027\u0027 = M; integrate: EI v\u0027 = −w0x⁴/(12L) + w0Lx²/8 + C1; EI v = −w0x⁵/(60L) + w0Lx³/24 + C1x + C2. 4) Conditions: v = 0 at x = 0 gives C2 = 0; v\u0027 = 0 at x = L/2 (symmetry) gives C1 = −5w0L³/192. 5) Evaluate at x = L/2: v_max = −w0L⁴/(120EI) (downward).","answers":["v_max = −w0L⁴/(120EI) (downward, at midspan)"]},{"id":"12.3","title":"Simply supported beam with off-center point load: maximum deflection","q":"The simply supported beam shown in the figure is subjected to the concentrated force. Determine the maximum deflection of the beam. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam ABC: pin support at A (left), roller at C (right). Downward 6 kN force at B, which is 2 m from A; BC = 1 m (total span 3 m). Point D marked on the beam between A and B (near its middle, about 1.5 m from A). Coordinates x1 and x2 are both measured from A.","sol":"1) Reactions: A = 2 kN up, C = 4 kN up. 2) Two coordinates from A: for 0 ≤ x1 \u003c 2 m, M1 = 2x1; for 2 m \u003c x2 ≤ 3 m, M2 = 2x2 − 6(x2 − 2) = 4(3 − x2). 3) Integrate EI v\u0027\u0027 = M twice in each region: EI v1\u0027 = x1² + C1, EI v1 = x1³/3 + C1x1 + C2; EI v2\u0027 = 4(3x2 − x2²/2) + C3, EI v2 = 4(3x2²/2 − x2³/6) + C3x2 + C4. 4) Four conditions: v1 = 0 at x1 = 0; v2 = 0 at x2 = 3; slopes equal and deflections equal at x = 2 m. Solve: C1 = −8/3, C2 = 0, C3 = −44/3, C4 = 8. 5) Max deflection occurs where slope = 0 in AB: x1² − 8/3 = 0, x1 = 1.633 m (point D). 6) EI v1 = x1³/3 − (8/3)x1 gives v_max = −2.90 kN·m³/EI (downward).","answers":["v_max = −2.90 kN·m³/EI (downward, at x = 1.633 m from A)"]},{"id":"12.4","title":"Overhanging beam with end load: deflection at the free end","q":"The beam in the figure is subjected to a load at its end. Determine the displacement at C. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam ABC: pin support at A (left), roller support at B, 2 m from A; overhang BC = 1 m to the free end C. Downward 4 kN load at C. Coordinate x1 is measured from A to the right (0 ≤ x1 \u003c 2 m); x2 is measured from C to the left (0 ≤ x2 \u003c 1 m). v_C is the vertical displacement at C. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) Reactions: ΣM_A: B_y(2) = 4(3) so B_y = 6 kN up, A_y = 2 kN down. 2) Two coordinates: M1 = −2x1 (0 ≤ x1 ≤ 2); M2 = −4x2 (0 ≤ x2 ≤ 1, x2 from C). 3) Integrate: EI v1\u0027 = −x1² + C1, EI v1 = −x1³/3 + C1x1 + C2; EI v2\u0027 = −2x2² + C3, EI v2 = −2x2³/3 + C3x2 + C4. 4) Conditions: v1 = 0 at x1 = 0; v1 = 0 at x1 = 2; v2 = 0 at x2 = 1 (roller B); slope continuity at B with opposite x directions: dv1/dx1 = −dv2/dx2 at x1 = 2, x2 = 1. Solve: C1 = 4/3, C2 = 0, C3 = 14/3, C4 = −4. 5) At C (x2 = 0): EI v_C = C4 = −4, so v_C = −4 kN·m³/EI (downward).","answers":["v_C = −4 kN·m³/EI (downward)"]},{"id":"12.5","title":"Overhanging beam with end load and couple: maximum deflection","q":"Determine the maximum deflection of the beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use discontinuity functions. The reactions are A_y = 6 kip up and B_y = 2 kip up.)","figs":["ex12-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam CAB, total length 30 ft: free end C at the left with a downward 8 kip load; pin support at A, 10 ft from C; roller support at B at the right end, 20 ft from A. Clockwise couple 120 kip·ft applied at B. Point D (between A and B) marks where the slope is zero. v_C and v_D are displacements. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) x from C. Loading function (reactions included; the couple and force at B are omitted since x ≤ 30 ft): w = −8⟨x−0⟩⁻¹ + 6⟨x−10⟩⁻¹ kip/ft. 2) Integrate: V = −8 + 6⟨x−10⟩⁰; M = −8x + 6⟨x−10⟩¹ kip·ft. 3) EI v\u0027\u0027 = M; EI v\u0027 = −4x² + 3⟨x−10⟩² + C1; EI v = −(4/3)x³ + ⟨x−10⟩³ + C1x + C2. 4) v = 0 at x = 10 and at x = 30: gives C1 = 1333, C2 = −12000. 5) At C (x = 0): EI v_C = −12000. 6) Check interior point D where slope = 0 (x \u003e 10): −4x² + 3(x−10)² + 1333 = 0, x² + 60x − 1633 = 0, x_D = 20.3 ft; v_D = +5006 kip·ft³/EI (upward, smaller). 7) So v_max = v_C.","answers":["v_C = v_max = −12 000 kip·ft³/EI (downward)","x_D = 20.3 ft, v_D = 5006 kip·ft³/EI (smaller)"]},{"id":"12.6","title":"Cantilever with partial load, couple and end load: elastic curve","q":"Determine the equation of the elastic curve for the cantilevered beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use discontinuity functions. The reactions at A are 52 kN up and 258 kN·m.)","figs":["ex12-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever ABC fixed (wall) at A on the left. Uniformly distributed downward load 8 kN/m over AB, AB = 5 m. A clockwise couple of 50 kN·m at B. Downward 12 kN point load at the free end C; BC = 4 m (total 9 m). (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) Reactions at A (computed in the book): A_y = 52 kN, M_A = 258 kN·m (opposing the loads). 2) The 8 kN/m load stops at B, so use superposition: extend it to the end and add an equal upward 8 kN/m from x = 5 m. Loading function (12 kN omitted since x ≤ 9 m): w = 52⟨x⟩⁻¹ − 258⟨x⟩⁻² − 8⟨x⟩⁰ + 50⟨x−5⟩⁻² + 8⟨x−5⟩⁰ kN/m. 3) Integrate to M = −258 + 52x − 4x² + 50⟨x−5⟩⁰ + 4⟨x−5⟩² kN·m. 4) EI v\u0027\u0027 = M; EI v\u0027 = −258x + 26x² − (4/3)x³ + 50⟨x−5⟩¹ + (4/3)⟨x−5⟩³ + C1; EI v = −129x² + (26/3)x³ − (1/3)x⁴ + 25⟨x−5⟩² + (1/3)⟨x−5⟩⁴ + C1x + C2. 5) Fixed end: v\u0027 = 0 and v = 0 at x = 0, so C1 = C2 = 0.","answers":["v = (1/EI)[−129x² + (26/3)x³ − (1/3)x⁴ + 25⟨x−5⟩² + (1/3)⟨x−5⟩⁴] m (x in m, EI in kN·m²)"]},{"id":"12.7","title":"Cantilever with end load: slope at the free end by moment-area","q":"Determine the slope of the beam shown in the figure at point B. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever beam AB of length L, fixed (wall) at the left end A, free at the right end B. Downward vertical force P at B.","sol":"1) Moment diagram: M = −Px, so M/EI is zero at B and −PL/(EI) at A, a triangle. 2) Elastic curve is concave downward. The tangent at fixed support A is horizontal (slope zero), so θ_B = θ_B/A. 3) Theorem 1: θ_B/A = area of the M/EI diagram between A and B = ½(−PL/EI)(L). 4) θ_B = −PL²/(2EI); the negative sign means the tangent at B rotates clockwise from the tangent at A, i.e. the beam slopes downward at B.","answers":["θ_B = −PL²/(2EI) (clockwise)"]},{"id":"12.8","title":"Cantilever with end couple: displacements at B and C","q":"Determine the displacement of points B and C of the beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever ABC fixed (wall) at the left end A, free end C. B is at midspan: AB = L/2, BC = L/2. A clockwise couple M0 is applied at the free end C. No other loads.","sol":"1) Internal moment is constant M = −M0 along AB-C, so M/EI = −M0/(EI), a rectangle of height M0/EI. 2) The tangent at the fixed support A is horizontal, so the displacements equal the tangential deviations: Δ_B = t_B/A and Δ_C = t_C/A. 3) Theorem 2: t_B/A = moment about B of the M/EI area between A and B = (L/4)[(−M0/EI)(L/2)] = −M0L²/(8EI). 4) t_C/A = moment about C of the whole area = (L/2)[(−M0/EI)(L)] = −M0L²/(2EI). 5) Negative signs: B and C lie below the tangent at A (deflect downward).","answers":["Δ_B = −M0L²/(8EI) (downward)","Δ_C = −M0L²/(2EI) (downward)"]},{"id":"12.9","title":"Simply supported shaft with central load: slope at a point","q":"Determine the slope at point C of the shaft shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Shaft AB of length L on bearing supports at A (left) and B (right). Downward load P at D at midspan (L/2 from A). Point C is L/4 to the right of D, and L/4 from B.","sol":"1) Reactions P/2 each; M/EI diagram is a triangle rising from 0 at A to PL/(4EI) at D, down to 0 at B. At C (L/4 from B) M/EI = PL/(8EI). 2) By symmetry the tangent at D is horizontal; the angle between tangents at D and C equals θ_C, so θ_C = θ_C/D. 3) Theorem 1: θ_C/D = area of M/EI between D and C (trapezoid) = (PL/8EI)(L/4) + ½(PL/4EI − PL/8EI)(L/4). 4) = PL²/(32EI) + PL²/(64EI) = 3PL²/(64EI). Positive means counterclockwise rotation from the tangent at D to C.","answers":["θ_C = 3PL²/(64EI)"]},{"id":"12.10","title":"Simply supported steel beam: slope at a point, numeric","q":"Determine the slope at point C for the steel beam shown in the figure. Take E_st = 200 GPa, I = 17(10⁶) mm⁴. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam AB (pin at A, roller at B), total span 8 m. Point C is 2 m from A. Downward 16 kN load located 2 m from B, i.e. 4 m to the right of C (6 m from A).","sol":"1) Reactions: A_y = 4 kN, B_y = 12 kN. M/EI diagram: 0 at A, 8/EI at C (kN·m units), 24/EI under the load at 6 m, 0 at B. 2) θ_C/A = area of M/EI between A and C = ½(2)(8/EI) = 8 kN·m²/EI. 3) t_B/A = moment about B of the M/EI area between A and B = [2 + (1/3)(6)](½)(6)(24/EI) + (2/3)(2)(½)(2)(24/EI) = 320 kN·m³/EI. 4) Slope at A: |θ_A| = t_B/A / L_AB = (320/EI)/8 = 40/EI. 5) |θ_C| = |θ_A| − |θ_C/A| = 40/EI − 8/EI = 32 kN·m²/EI (clockwise). 6) EI = 200(10⁶) kN/m² × 17(10⁻⁶) m⁴ = 3400 kN·m². θ_C = 32/3400 = 0.00941 rad.","answers":["θ_C = 32 kN·m²/EI = 0.00941 rad (clockwise)"]},{"id":"12.11","title":"Simply supported beam with end couple: midspan displacement","q":"Determine the displacement at C for the beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam AB of span L (pin at A, roller at B). Counterclockwise couple M0 applied at A. Point C is at midspan: AC = L/2, CB = L/2.","sol":"1) Moment varies linearly from M0 at A to 0 at B (M/EI: M0/EI at A, M0/(2EI) at C, 0 at B). 2) Draw tangents at A, B and C. From geometry Δ_C = Δ\u0027 − t_C/B with Δ\u0027 = t_A/B / 2 (proportional triangles, C at midspan). 3) Theorem 2: t_A/B = (L/3)[½(L)(M0/EI)] = M0L²/(6EI). 4) t_C/B (triangle of base L/2 and height M0/(2EI) next to B) = (1/3)(L/2)[½(L/2)(M0/2EI)] = M0L²/(48EI). 5) Δ_C = ½(M0L²/6EI) − M0L²/(48EI) = M0L²/(16EI), downward.","answers":["Δ_C = M0L²/(16EI) (downward)"]},{"id":"12.12","title":"Overhanging steel beam: displacement at the free end","q":"Determine the displacement at point C for the steel overhanging beam shown in the figure. Take E_st = 29(10³) ksi, I = 125 in⁴. (Use the moment-area method. The forces acting on the beam are: 5 kip down at A, 10 kip up at B, 5 kip down at C.)","figs":["ex12-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam ABC, A at the left (pin), B (roller) 12 ft from A, C at the free right end, 12 ft beyond B (total 24 ft). Forces shown: 5 kip downward at A (reaction), 10 kip upward at B (reaction), 5 kip downward at the end C.","sol":"1) Moment diagram: M = 0 at A, M_B = −5(12) = −60 kip·ft at B, back to 0 at C (two triangles, M/EI = −60/EI at B). 2) Draw tangents at A, B, C. Geometry with proportional triangles: Δ_C = |t_C/A| − Δ\u0027 where Δ\u0027/24 = |t_B/A|/12, so Δ\u0027 = 2|t_B/A|. 3) Theorem 2: t_C/A = moment about C of M/EI area A–C = (12 ft)[½(24 ft)(−60/EI)] = −8640 kip·ft³/EI. 4) t_B/A = moment about B of area A–B = (1/3)(12)[½(12)(−60/EI)] = −1440 kip·ft³/EI. 5) Δ_C = 8640/EI − 2(1440/EI) = 5760 kip·ft³/EI. 6) Convert: 5760(1728)/[29(10³)(125)] = 2.75 in., downward.","answers":["Δ_C = 5760 kip·ft³/EI = 2.75 in. (downward)"]},{"id":"12.13","title":"Simply supported beam, partial load and center force: slope and deflection","q":"Determine the displacement at point C and the slope at the support A of the beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use superposition and the table of beam deflections.)","figs":["ex12-13.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported beam AB of span 8 m (pin at A, roller at B). A uniformly distributed downward load of 2 kN/m acts on the left half AC (4 m). A downward 8 kN point load acts at midspan C. CB = 4 m. θ_A and v_C are the requested slope at A and displacement at C. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) Split into two cases on the simply supported span L = 8 m: (b) 2 kN/m on the left half; (c) 8 kN at midspan. 2) Table values for the half-span uniform load: (θ_A)1 = 3wL³/(128EI) = 3(2)(8)³/(128EI) = 24/EI (clockwise); (v_C)1 = 5wL⁴/(768EI) = 5(2)(8)⁴/(768EI) = 53.33/EI (down). 3) Central point load: (θ_A)2 = PL²/(16EI) = 8(8)²/(16EI) = 32/EI (clockwise); (v_C)2 = PL³/(48EI) = 8(8)³/(48EI) = 85.33/EI (down). 4) Add algebraically: θ_A = 24 + 32 = 56 kN·m²/EI; v_C = 53.33 + 85.33 ≈ 139 kN·m³/EI.","answers":["θ_A = 56 kN·m²/EI (clockwise)","v_C = 139 kN·m³/EI (downward)"]},{"id":"12.14","title":"Overhanging beam with distributed load and end force: end deflection","q":"Determine the displacement at the end C of the overhanging beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. (Use superposition.)","figs":["ex12-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam ABC: pin support at A (left), roller support at B, 4 m from A; overhang BC = 2 m with free end C. Uniform downward load 5 kN/m acts on AB (4 m). Downward 10 kN point load at C.","sol":"1) Split into: (b) simply supported span AB with 5 kN/m; (c) the 10 kN overhang load replaced by a 10 kN force at B (no effect) plus a 20 kN·m couple at B on the simply supported span; (d) cantilever BC with 10 kN at C. 2) (b): (θ_B)1 = wL³/(24EI) = 5(4)³/(24EI) = 13.33/EI (clockwise); small angle so (v_C)1 = 2(13.33/EI) = 26.67/EI upward. 3) (c): (θ_B)2 = M0L/(3EI) = 20(4)/(3EI) = 26.67/EI; (v_C)2 = 2(26.67/EI) = 53.33/EI downward. 4) (d): (v_C)3 = PL³/(3EI) = 10(2)³/(3EI) = 26.67/EI downward. 5) Sum (+ downward): v_C = −26.7 + 53.3 + 26.7 = 53.3 kN·m³/EI downward.","answers":["v_C = 53.3 kN·m³/EI (downward)"]},{"id":"12.15","title":"Cantilever with triangular load over part: end deflection","q":"Determine the displacement at the end C of the cantilever beam shown in the figure. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-15.webp"],"figDesc":"Cantilever ABC fixed (wall) at A on the left. A downward triangular distributed load acts on AB (6 m), 4 kN/m at A decreasing to 0 at B. The part BC (2 m) is unloaded and ends at the free end C. Labels v_B, θ_B, v_C show displacements at B and C. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) Treat AB as a cantilever with a triangular load (w0 = 4 kN/m at the wall, L = 6 m); use the table: θ_B = w0L³/(24EI) = 4(6)³/(24EI) = 36/EI; v_B = w0L⁴/(30EI) = 4(6)⁴/(30EI) = 172.8/EI. 2) The unloaded part BC stays straight, so v_C = v_B + θ_B(L_BC) (small angles) = 172.8/EI + (36/EI)(2) = 244.8/EI downward.","answers":["v_C = 244.8 kN·m³/EI (downward)"]},{"id":"12.16","title":"Steel bar on two springs: displacement under a load","q":"The steel bar shown in the figure is supported by two springs at its ends A and B. Each spring has a stiffness of k = 15 kip/ft and is originally unstretched. If the bar is loaded with a force of 3 kip at point C, determine the vertical displacement of the force. Neglect the weight of the bar and take E_st = 29(10³) ksi, I = 12 in⁴.","figs":["ex12-16.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal steel bar AB of length 9 ft supported at each end by a vertical spring (k = 15 kip/ft each). Downward 3 kip load at C, which is 3 ft from A and 6 ft from B.","sol":"1) Spring reactions: A = 2 kip, B = 1 kip (moments). 2) Spring compressions: (v_A)1 = 2/15 = 0.1333 ft; (v_B)1 = 1/15 = 0.0667 ft. 3) Rigid-body part at C by proportion: (v_C)1 = (v_B)1 + (6/9)[(v_A)1 − (v_B)1] = 0.0667 + (2/3)(0.0667) = 0.1111 ft down. 4) Bar bending (simply supported, load at a = 3 ft, b = 6 ft, L = 9 ft): (v_C)2 = Pab(L² − b² − a²)/(6EIL) = 3(3)(6)(81 − 36 − 9)/[6EI(9)] with EI = 29(10³)(144)(12/20736) ≈ 2417 kip·ft² gives 0.0149 ft down. 5) v_C = 0.1111 + 0.0149 = 0.126 ft = 1.51 in. downward.","answers":["v_C = 0.126 ft = 1.51 in. (downward)"]},{"id":"12.17","title":"Propped cantilever with triangular load: reaction at the roller","q":"The beam is subjected to the distributed loading shown in the figure. Determine the reaction at A. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-17.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam AB of length L. Left end A is on a roller support; right end B is fixed (wall). Downward triangular distributed load increasing linearly from zero at A to w0 at B. x is measured from A to the right. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) One degree indeterminate; take A_y (roller reaction, upward) as the redundant, x from A. 2) Moment at x: M = A_y x − (1/6)w0 x³/L. 3) EI v\u0027\u0027 = A_y x − w0x³/(6L); EI v\u0027 = A_y x²/2 − w0x⁴/(24L) + C1; EI v = A_y x³/6 − w0x⁵/(120L) + C1x + C2. 4) Conditions: v = 0 at x = 0 gives C2 = 0; v\u0027 = 0 at x = L: A_yL²/2 − w0L³/24 + C1 = 0; v = 0 at x = L: A_yL³/6 − w0L⁴/120 + C1L = 0. 5) Solve: A_y = w0L/10 (upward), C1 = −w0L³/120. (Then B_y = 2w0L/5, M_B = w0L²/15, B_x = 0.)","answers":["A_y = w0L/10 (upward)"]},{"id":"12.18","title":"Fixed-fixed beam, uniform load: support reactions","q":"The beam in the figure is fixed supported at both ends and is subjected to the uniform loading shown. Determine the reactions at the supports. Neglect the effect of axial load. EI is constant.","figs":["ex12-18.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AB of length L, fixed (walls) at both ends A and B. Uniform downward distributed load w over the entire span. x is measured from A. (The figure also sketches the elastic curve.)","sol":"1) Symmetry: V_A = V_B = wL/2 and the end moments are equal, M_A = M_B = M\u0027 (the redundant). Beam is indeterminate to the second degree but symmetry reduces it to one unknown. 2) Moment at x: M = (wL/2)x − (w/2)x² − M\u0027. 3) EI v\u0027\u0027 = (wL/2)x − (w/2)x² − M\u0027; EI v\u0027 = (wL/4)x² − (w/6)x³ − M\u0027x + C1; EI v = (wL/12)x³ − (w/24)x⁴ − M\u0027x²/2 + C1x + C2. 4) At x = 0: v = 0 gives C2 = 0 and v\u0027 = 0 gives C1 = 0. At x = L, v = 0: wL⁴/12 − wL⁴/24 − M\u0027L²/2 = 0, so M\u0027 = wL²/12. (Slope condition at x = L is satisfied automatically by symmetry.)","answers":["V_A = V_B = wL/2","M_A = M_B = M\u0027 = wL²/12"]},{"id":"12.19","title":"Fixed beam with intermediate roller and end load: reactions","q":"The beam is subjected to the concentrated force shown in the figure. Determine the reactions at the supports. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-19.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam fixed (wall) at the left end A, resting on a roller support at B, a distance L from A, and extending a further L to its free right end where a downward force P acts. Total length 2L.","sol":"1) Indeterminate to the first degree; take B_y as the redundant. 2) Superpose M/EI diagrams on a cantilever fixed at A: B_y (upward at B) gives a triangle of height B_yL/EI at A and 0 at B (base L); P gives a trapezoid-shaped diagram, −2PL/EI at A to 0 at the tip, with −PL/EI at B. 3) Since A is fixed and B is on a support, Δ_B = 0, so the tangent deviation t_B/A = 0. 4) Moment of M/EI areas about B: (2/3)L[½(B_yL/EI)(L)] + (L/2)[(−PL/EI)(L)] + (2/3)L[½(−PL/EI)(L)] = 0 (the P diagram over AB split into a rectangle of height PL/EI and a triangle). 5) Solve: B_y = 2.5P (upward). 6) Equilibrium: A_x = 0; −A_y + 2.5P − P = 0 gives A_y = 1.5P; ΣM_A: −M_A + 2.5P(L) − P(2L) = 0 gives M_A = 0.5PL.","answers":["B_y = 2.5P","A_x = 0","A_y = 1.5P","M_A = 0.5PL"]},{"id":"12.20","title":"Two-span beam with end couple: reaction at middle support","q":"The beam is subjected to the couple moment at its end C as shown in the figure. Determine the reaction at B. EI is constant. (Use the moment-area method.)","figs":["ex12-20.webp"],"figDesc":"Beam ABC with total length 2L: pin support at A (left end), roller at B in the middle (L from A, shown touching the top of the beam), roller at C (right end, L from B). A clockwise couple M0 is applied at C.","sol":"1) Indeterminate to the first degree; take B_y as the redundant (it acts downward as drawn). Treat the beam as simply supported at A and C and superpose M/EI diagrams for B_y and M0. 2) The tangent deviations of the supports A, B, C from the tangent at C must be proportional: t_B/C = ½ t_A/C. 3) t_B/C = (L/3)[½(B_yL/2EI)(L)] + (2L/3)[½(−M0/2EI)(L)] + (L/2)[(−M0/2EI)(L)]; t_A/C = L[½(B_yL/2EI)(2L)] + (2L/3)[½(−M0/EI)(2L)]. 4) Substituting into t_B/C = ½ t_A/C and simplifying gives B_y = 3M0/(2L). 5) Equilibrium then gives A_x = 0, C_y = 5M0/(4L), A_y = M0/(4L).","answers":["B_y = 3M0/(2L)","(then A_x = 0, C_y = 5M0/(4L), A_y = M0/(4L))"]},{"id":"12.21","title":"Propped cantilever with uniform load and point load: roller reaction","q":"Determine the reactions at the roller support B of the beam shown in the figure, then draw the shear and moment diagrams. EI is constant. (Use superposition.)","figs":["ex12-21.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AB, 10 ft long, fixed (wall) at the left end A and on a roller support at the right end B. Uniform downward load 2 kip/ft over the whole length. Downward 8 kip point load 5 ft from A (at midspan).","sol":"1) Indeterminate to the first degree; take B_y (upward) as the redundant. Remove the roller and apply B_y separately. 2) Compatibility (+ down): 0 = v_B − v_B\u0027. 3) Table values: v_B = wL⁴/(8EI) + 5PL³/(48EI) = 2(10)⁴/(8EI) + 5(8)(10)³/(48EI) = 3333 kip·ft³/EI (down); v_B\u0027 = B_yL³/(3EI) = 333.3B_y/EI (up). 4) 0 = 3333 − 333.3B_y gives B_y = 10 kip (upward). 5) Equilibrium: A_y = 18 kip up, M_A = 40 kip·ft. 6) Shear: V = 18 kip at A, drops linearly to 8 kip just left of the 8 kip load (x = 5 ft), 0 just right, then −10 kip at B. Moment: M_A = −40 kip·ft, rising to a maximum +25 kip·ft at x = 5 ft, falling to 0 at B.","answers":["B_y = 10 kip (upward)","A_y = 18 kip, M_A = 40 kip·ft","V: 18, 8/0 (at 5 ft), −10 kip; M: −40, +25 (at 5 ft), 0 kip·ft"]},{"id":"12.22","title":"Cantilever beam supported by an elastic rod: force in rod","q":"The beam in the figure is fixed supported to the wall at A and pin connected to a ½-in.-diameter rod BC. If E = 29(10³) ksi for both members, determine the force developed in the rod due to the loading. The moment of inertia of the beam about its neutral axis is I = 475 in⁴.","figs":["ex12-22.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AB, 10 ft long, fixed (wall) at the left end A. A downward 8 kip load acts 5 ft from A (midspan). At the right end B a vertical rod BC, 8 ft long, is pin-connected to the beam and pinned to a fixed support above at C. Rod diameter 1/2 in.","sol":"1) Indeterminate to the first degree; take the rod force F_BC as the redundant. Remove it from the beam and apply it separately at B. 2) Compatibility (+ down): v_B\u0027\u0027 = v_B − v_B\u0027 where v_B\u0027\u0027 is the rod\u0027s elongation (the beam end moves down as the rod stretches). 3) Rod: v_B\u0027\u0027 = PL/(AE) = F_BC(8)(12)/[(π/4)(½)²(29)(10³)] = 0.01686F_BC. 4) Beam with 8 kip at midspan: v_B = 5PL³/(48EI) = 5(8)(10)³(12)³/[48(29)(10³)(475)] = 0.1045 in. down. 5) Beam with F_BC at the end: v_B\u0027 = F_BC L³/(3EI) = F_BC(10)³(12)³/[3(29)(10³)(475)] = 0.04181F_BC up. 6) 0.01686F_BC = 0.1045 − 0.04181F_BC gives F_BC = 1.78 kip.","answers":["F_BC = 1.78 kip"]},{"id":"12.23","title":"Fixed-fixed beam, partial uniform load: moment at B","q":"Determine the moment at B for the beam shown in the figure. EI is constant. Neglect the effects of axial load.","figs":["ex12-23.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam AB, 12 ft long, fixed (walls) at both ends A (left) and B (right). Uniform downward load 3 kip/ft acts on the left half only (6 ft from A to midspan); the right 6 ft is unloaded.","sol":"1) Indeterminate to the second degree; take B_y and M_B at B as redundants, so the beam is a cantilever fixed at A (L = 12 ft). 2) Compatibility at B: 0 = θ_B + θ_B\u0027 + θ_B\u0027\u0027; 0 = v_B + v_B\u0027 + v_B\u0027\u0027. 3) Table: load w on half span: θ_B = wL³/(48EI) = 108/EI; v_B = 7wL⁴/(384EI) = 1134/EI (down). B_y only: θ_B\u0027 = PL²/(2EI) = 72B_y/EI; v_B\u0027 = PL³/(3EI) = 576B_y/EI. M_B only: θ_B\u0027\u0027 = ML/EI = 12M_B/EI; v_B\u0027\u0027 = ML²/(2EI) = 72M_B/EI. 4) 0 = 108 + 72B_y + 12M_B; 0 = 1134 + 576B_y + 72M_B. 5) Solve: B_y = −3.375 kip (i.e. opposite to the assumed sense), M_B = 11.25 kip·ft.","answers":["M_B = 11.25 kip·ft","B_y = −3.375 kip"]},{"id":"13.1","title":"Pin-ended W8×31 steel column: largest axial load","q":"The A992 steel W8 × 31 member shown in the figure is to be used as a pin-connected column. Determine the largest axial load it can support before it either begins to buckle or the steel yields. Take E = 29(10³) ksi and σ_Y = 50 ksi. Section properties: A = 9.13 in², I_x = 110 in⁴, I_y = 37.1 in⁴.","figs":["ex13-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical W8×31 steel column on a base plate at the bottom, with a horizontal beam (capping member) at the top; length 12 ft. An inset shows the cross-section orientation: an H shape with y–y the horizontal axis and x–x the vertical axis (flanges are the vertical strokes of the H). Pin-connected ends.","sol":"1) Buckling occurs about the weak axis, y–y, because I_y = 37.1 in⁴ \u003c I_x = 110 in⁴. 2) Euler load for pin ends (K = 1): P_cr = π²EI_y/L² = π²(29×10³)(37.1)/(12×12)² = 512 kip. 3) Check the stress at that load: σ_cr = P_cr/A = 512/9.13 = 56.1 ksi, which exceeds σ_Y = 50 ksi, so Euler\u0027s formula is not valid; yielding occurs first. 4) Load from simple compression at yield: P = σ_Y A = 50(9.13) = 456 kip (a factor of safety would be applied in practice).","answers":["P = 456 kip (governed by yielding; Euler P_cr = 512 kip is not valid since σ_cr = 56.1 ksi \u003e σ_Y)"]},{"id":"13.2","title":"Fixed-end W6×15 column braced about weak axis: critical load","q":"A W6 × 15 steel column is 24 ft long and is fixed at its ends as shown in the figure. Its load-carrying capacity is increased by bracing it about the y–y (weak) axis using struts that are assumed to be pin connected to its midheight. Determine the load it can support so that the column does not buckle nor the material exceed the yield stress. Take E_st = 29(10³) ksi and σ_Y = 60 ksi. Section properties: A = 4.43 in², I_x = 29.1 in⁴, I_y = 9.32 in⁴, r_x = 2.56 in., r_y = 1.46 in.","figs":["ex13-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical W6×15 column, 24 ft tall, fixed at the base and at the top, with a downward axial load P at the top. Axes x–x and y–y are drawn at the top. A strut (beam) crosses the column at midheight (12 ft from each end), bracing it against movement about the y–y axis; each half is marked 12 ft.","sol":"1) Behavior differs by axis. About x–x (no bracing) the column acts as fixed–fixed over the full 24 ft: K = 0.5, (KL)_x = 0.5(24 ft) = 12 ft = 144 in. About y–y the struts pin the midheight, so each 12 ft segment acts as fixed–pinned: K = 0.7, (KL)_y = 0.7(12 ft) = 8.40 ft = 100.8 in. 2) (P_cr)_x = π²EI_x/(KL)_x² = π²(29×10³)(29.1)/(144)² = 401.7 kip. 3) (P_cr)_y = π²EI_y/(KL)_y² = π²(29×10³)(9.32)/(100.8)² = 262.5 kip. 4) Smaller governs: buckling about y–y. (Check: KL/r = 56.2 about x, 69.0 about y; larger slenderness governs.) 5) σ_cr = 262.5/4.43 = 59.3 ksi \u003c σ_Y = 60 ksi, so Euler is valid and buckling occurs before yield.","answers":["P_cr = 263 kip (buckling about the y–y axis)"]},{"id":"13.3","title":"Braced aluminum column with factor of safety: allowable load","q":"The aluminum column is braced at its top by cables so as to prevent movement at the top along the x axis. If it is assumed to be fixed at its base, determine the largest allowable load P that can be applied. Use a factor of safety for buckling of F.S. = 3.0. Take E_al = 70 GPa, σ_Y = 215 MPa, A = 7.5(10⁻³) m², I_x = 61.3(10⁻⁶) m⁴, I_y = 23.2(10⁻⁶) m⁴. The column is 5 m tall.","figs":["ex13-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical aluminum column (wide-flange/I-type section) 5 m tall, fixed to a base plate at the bottom. Axial downward load P applied at the top (z axis up). Two cables are attached at the top and run along the x axis in opposite directions to wall anchors, preventing top movement in the x direction. Axes x, y, z indicated at the top.","sol":"1) Buckling about the x–x axis involves sideways movement of the top in the y direction, which is not restrained: fixed–free, K = 2, (KL)_x = 2(5 m) = 10 m. 2) Buckling about y–y involves movement in the x direction, which the cables prevent (top pinned, base fixed): K = 0.7, (KL)_y = 0.7(5) = 3.5 m. 3) (P_cr)_x = π²EI_x/(KL)_x² = π²(70×10⁹)(61.3×10⁻⁶)/(10)² = 424 kN. 4) (P_cr)_y = π²EI_y/(KL)_y² = π²(70×10⁹)(23.2×10⁻⁶)/(3.5)² = 1.31 MN. 5) x–x buckling governs: P_cr = 424 kN. 6) P_allow = P_cr/F.S. = 424/3.0 = 141 kN. 7) Check Euler validity: σ_cr = 424 kN/7.5×10⁻³ m² = 56.5 MPa \u003c σ_Y = 215 MPa, OK.","answers":["P_allow = 141 kN (x–x axis buckling governs, P_cr = 424 kN)"]},{"id":"13.4","title":"Braced W8×40 column with eccentric load: maximum load","q":"The W8 × 40 A992 steel column shown in the figure is fixed at its base and braced at the top so that it is fixed from displacement, yet free to rotate about the y–y axis. Also, it can sway to the side in the y–z plane. Determine the maximum eccentric load the column can support before it either begins to buckle or the steel yields. The load P acts 9 in. from the column axis. Take E = 29(10³) ksi, σ_Y = 50 ksi. Section properties: A = 11.7 in², depth d = 8.25 in. (so c = d/2 = 4.125 in.), r_x = 3.53 in., I_y = 49.1 in⁴. Column length 12 ft.","figs":["ex13-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical W8×40 column 12 ft tall, fixed at the base, with a horizontal beam at the top bracing it. Load P is applied on a bracket 9 in. off the column axis in the y–z plane (labeled 9 in.). Axes x, y, z drawn at the top.","sol":"1) y–y axis (buckling): top is pinned against displacement and base fixed, K_y = 0.7, (KL)_y = 0.7(12) = 8.40 ft = 100.8 in. (P_cr)_y = π²EI_y/(KL)_y² = π²(29×10³)(49.1)/(100.8)² = 1383 kip. 2) x–x axis (yielding by bending): column is free at the top and fixed at the bottom (K_x = 2), carrying P plus moment M = P(9 in.): (KL)_x = 2(12) = 24 ft = 288 in. Use the secant formula: σ_max = σ_Y = (P/A)[1 + (ec/r_x²) sec((KL)_x/(2r_x) · √(P/(EA)))], with e = 9 in., c = 4.125 in., r_x = 3.53 in. 3) Numerically: ec/r² = 2.979, (KL)/(2r)·1/√(EA) = 0.0700, and σ_Y A = 585 kip, so 585 = P[1 + 2.979 sec(0.0700√P)]. 4) Solve by trial and error (argument in radians): P_x = 115 kip. 5) 115 kip \u003c 1383 kip, so failure (yielding) occurs about the x–x axis.","answers":["P = 115 kip (x–x axis yielding governs; (P_cr)_y = 1383 kip)"]},{"id":"13.5","title":"Inelastic buckling of a short solid rod: critical load","q":"A solid rod has a diameter of 30 mm and is 600 mm long. It is made of a material that can be modeled by the stress–strain diagram shown in the figure. If it is used as a pin-supported column, determine the critical load.","figs":["ex13-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Stress–strain diagram, σ (MPa) vertical and ε horizontal. Bilinear curve from the origin: first straight segment rises to σ_pl = 150 MPa at ε = 0.001; second, flatter segment continues through σ = 270 MPa at ε = 0.002 and beyond.","sol":"1) Radius of gyration of a solid circle: r = √(I/A) = √[(π/4)(15)⁴/(π(15)²)] = 7.5 mm. 2) Slenderness ratio for pin ends (K = 1): KL/r = 600/7.5 = 80. 3) Engesser relation: σ_cr = π²E_t/(KL/r)² = π²E_t/80² = 1.542(10⁻³)E_t. 4) First assume elastic: E = 150 MPa/0.001 = 150 GPa, giving σ_cr = 1.542(10⁻³)(150×10³) = 231.3 MPa \u003e σ_pl = 150 MPa, so inelastic buckling occurs. 5) Use the tangent modulus from the second segment: E_t = Δσ/Δε = (270 − 150)/(0.002 − 0.001) = 120 GPa. 6) σ_cr = 1.542(10⁻³)(120×10³) = 185.1 MPa, which lies between 150 and 270 MPa, so valid. 7) P_cr = σ_cr A = 185.1(10⁶) Pa × π(0.015 m)² = 131 kN.","answers":["P_cr = 131 kN (σ_cr = 185.1 MPa, E_t = 120 GPa)"]},{"id":"13.6","title":"Pin-supported W10×100 steel column: AISC allowable load","q":"An A992 steel W10 × 100 member is used as a pin-supported column, 16 ft long. Using the AISC column design formulas, determine the largest load that it can safely support. Take E = 29(10³) ksi, σ_Y = 50 ksi. Section properties from Appendix B: A = 29.4 in², r_x = 4.60 in., r_y = 2.65 in.","figs":["ex13-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical W10×100 wide-flange column, 16 ft long, loaded by an axial compressive force P at the top and equal and opposite P at the bottom. Axes x–x and y–y are marked at the top.","sol":"1) Pin ends: K = 1 for both axes, so the weaker axis (smaller radius, r_y = 2.65 in.) gives the larger slenderness: KL/r = 1(16)(12)/2.65 = 72.45. 2) Transition slenderness: (KL/r)_c = √(2π²E/σ_Y) = √(2π²(29×10³)/50) = 107. 3) Since 0 \u003c 72.45 \u003c 107, use the inelastic AISC formula: σ_allow = [1 − (KL/r)²/(2(KL/r)_c²)]σ_Y / {5/3 + (3/8)(KL/r)/(KL/r)_c − (KL/r)³/(8(KL/r)_c³)}. 4) Evaluate with KL/r = 72.45 and (KL/r)_c = 107, σ_Y = 50 ksi. The book prints σ_allow = 23.93 ksi and P = σ_allow A = 23.93(29.4) = 704 kip. NOTE FOR EXAMINER: direct evaluation of the stated formula gives numerator ≈ 38.5 ksi, denominator ≈ 1.882, so σ_allow ≈ 20.5 ksi and P ≈ 602 kip; the book\u0027s printed value appears inconsistent with its own formula. Accept the method and either value, but 704 kip is the book\u0027s answer.","answers":["P = 704 kip (σ_allow = 23.93 ksi, as printed in the book; direct formula evaluation gives about 20.5 ksi, P ≈ 602 kip)"]},{"id":"13.7","title":"Fixed-fixed steel rod: smallest diameter by AISC","q":"The steel rod shown in the figure is to be used to support an axial load of 18 kip. If E_st = 29(10³) ksi and σ_Y = 50 ksi, determine the smallest diameter of the rod as allowed by the AISC specification. The rod is 15 ft long and is fixed at both ends.","figs":["ex13-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal circular rod of diameter d and length 15 ft, shown fixed (wall-like supports) at both ends, loaded by an axial compressive force of 18 kip pushing in from each end.","sol":"1) For a solid circle r = √(I/A) = d/4. 2) (KL/r)_c = √(2π²E/σ_Y) = √(2π²(29×10³)/50) = 107.0. 3) Since r is unknown, assume the elastic (long-column) AISC formula, Eq. 13–21: σ_allow = 12π²E/[23(KL/r)²] with K = 0.5 (fixed–fixed). 4) 18/[(π/4)d²] = 12π²(29×10³)/{23[0.5(15)(12)/(d/4)]²}, so 22.92/d² = 1.152d². 5) d⁴ = 19.9, d = 2.11 in. Use d = 2.25 in. (2¼ in.). 6) Check: KL/r = 0.5(15)(12)/(2.25/4) = 160, and since 107.0 \u003c 160 \u003c 200 the long-column formula is appropriate and the slenderness limit is satisfied.","answers":["d = 2.25 in. (2¼ in.; calculated 2.11 in.)"]},{"id":"13.8","title":"Pin-ended aluminum bar: cross-section for a 12 kip load","q":"A bar having a length of 30 in. is used to support an axial compressive load of 12 kip. It is pin supported at its ends and made of a 2014-T6 aluminum alloy. Determine the dimensions of its cross-sectional area if its width is to be twice its thickness. (Use the AISC aluminum column formulas: σ_allow = 28 ksi for KL/r ≤ 12; 30.7 − 0.23(KL/r) ksi for 12 \u003c KL/r \u003c 55; 54 000 ksi/(KL/r)² for KL/r ≥ 55.)","figs":["ex13-8.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical rectangular bar 30 in. long, axial compressive force of 12 kip applied at each end. Cross-section thickness b and width 2b; x and y axes drawn at the top.","sol":"1) Pin ends, K = 1; use I_min = I_y (the thickness direction): KL/r_y = 30/√[(1/12)(2b)(b³)/(2b·b)] = 103.9/b. 2) Try the short-column formula first: P/A = 28 ksi, 12/(2b²) = 28, b = 0.463 in. 3) Check: KL/r = 103.9/0.463 = 224.5, which is \u003e 12, so that formula is invalid. 4) Try the long-column formula (KL/r ≥ 55): P/A = 54 000/(KL/r)²: 12/(2b²) = 54 000/(103.9/b)², giving b = 1.05 in. 5) Check: KL/r = 103.9/1.05 = 99.3 \u003e 55, OK. 6) The cross-section is b = 1.05 in. by 2b = 2.10 in.; a 1 in. by 2 in. section would be satisfactory.","answers":["b = 1.05 in. (cross-section about 1.05 in. × 2.10 in.)"]},{"id":"13.9","title":"Pin-supported wood board: greatest allowable length by NFPA","q":"A board having cross-sectional dimensions of 5.5 in. by 1.5 in. is used to support an axial load of 5 kip. If the board is assumed to be pin supported at its top and bottom, determine its greatest allowable length L as specified by the NFPA. (For 26 \u003c KL/d ≤ 50 the NFPA allowable stress is σ_allow = 540 ksi/(KL/d)², where d is the smaller dimension.)","figs":["ex13-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical rectangular wooden board of length L with cross-section 1.5 in. by 5.5 in. (dimensions marked at the top), axial load 5 kip applied at the top and bottom. x and y axes drawn at the top.","sol":"1) The board buckles about its weak (y) axis, so in the NFPA equations d = 1.5 in. 2) Assume the 26 \u003c KL/d ≤ 50 equation applies: P/A = 540/(KL/d)² with K = 1. 3) 5/[(5.5)(1.5)] = 540/(L/1.5)², giving L = 44.8 in. 4) Check: KL/d = 44.8/1.5 = 29.8, which lies between 26 and 50, so the assumed equation is valid.","answers":["L = 44.8 in."]},{"id":"13.10","title":"Eccentrically loaded aluminum column: maximum load P","q":"The column shown in the figure is made of aluminum alloy 2014-T6 and is used to support an eccentric load P. Determine the maximum magnitude of P that can be supported if the column is fixed at its base and free at its top. Use the formula σ_allow = P/A + Pec/I (the allowable stress equals the maximum stress, assumed uniform over the cross section). Aluminum allowable stress for KL/r ≥ 55: σ_allow = 54 000 ksi/(KL/r)². Column is 80 in. long, with a 2 in. by 4 in. cross section; P acts at an eccentricity of 1 in. from the centroid.","figs":["ex13-10.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical rectangular aluminum column, 80 in. long, fixed at the base; cross-section 2 in. by 4 in. (labeled 2 in., 2 in., 2 in. along the top edges). Load P acts downward at the top, offset 1 in. from the centroid.","sol":"1) Fixed–free: K = 2. The largest slenderness uses the smaller radius of gyration: KL/r = 2(80)/√[(1/12)(4)(2)³/(2·4)] = 277.1. 2) Since 277.1 \u003e 55 use σ_allow = 54 000/(KL/r)² = 54 000/(277.1)² = 0.7031 ksi. 3) Maximum compressive stress from axial load plus bending: σ_max = P/A + Pec/I = P/[(2)(4)] + P(1)(2)/[(1/12)(2)(4)³] = 0.3125P. 4) Set σ_allow = σ_max: 0.7031 = 0.3125P, so P = 2.25 kip.","answers":["P = 2.25 kip"]},{"id":"13.11","title":"Eccentric load on pin-ended W6×20 column: interaction method","q":"The A-36 steel W6 × 20 column shown in the figure is pin connected at its ends and is subjected to the eccentric load P (applied 30 in. from the axis). Determine the allowable value of P using the interaction method if the allowable bending stress is (σ_b)_allow = 22 ksi. Column length 15 ft; E = 29(10³) ksi, σ_Y = 36 ksi. Section properties: A = 5.87 in², I_x = 41.4 in⁴, r_y = 1.50 in., d = 6.20 in.","figs":["ex13-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical W6×20 column, 15 ft tall, pin connected at the ends. Axial load P applied at the top on a bracket offset 30 in. from the column axis (dimension 30 in.); equal reaction force P at the base, drawn with an equivalent couple M = P(30 in.). Axes x and y shown at the top.","sol":"1) Pin ends K = 1. Largest slenderness uses r_y: KL/r = 1(15)(12)/1.50 = 120. 2) (KL/r)_c = √(2π²E/σ_Y) = √(2π²(29×10³)/36) = 126.1; since 120 \u003c 126.1 use the inelastic AISC formula. 3) σ_allow = [1 − (120)²/(2(126.1)²)](36) / {5/3 + (3/8)(120/126.1) − (120)³/(8(126.1)³)} = 10.28 ksi (allowable axial stress). 4) Bending about the x axis: M = P(30 in.), c = d/2 = 3.10 in. 5) Interaction formula: σ_a/(σ_a)_allow + σ_b/(σ_b)_allow ≤ 1: (P/5.87)/10.28 + [P(30)(3.10)/41.4]/22 = 1. 6) Solve: P = 8.43 kip. 7) Check applicability: σ_a/(σ_a)_allow = (8.43/5.87)/10.28 = 0.140 \u003c 0.15, OK.","answers":["P = 8.43 kip","(σ_a)_allow = 10.28 ksi"]},{"id":"13.12","title":"Timber column of two nailed boards: eccentric load","q":"The timber column shown in the figure is made from two boards nailed together so that the cross section has the dimensions shown (3 in. by 6 in.). If the column is fixed at its base and free at its top, use σ_allow = P/A + Mc/I to determine the eccentric load P that can be supported. The column is 60 in. long, and P acts 4 in. from the centroid (3 in. to the edge of the 6 in. side plus 1 in. beyond). For 26 \u003c KL/d ≤ 50 the NFPA allowable stress is σ_allow = 540 ksi/(KL/d)².","figs":["ex13-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Vertical timber column 60 in. long, built of two boards nailed together (visible nail heads), fixed at its base. Cross-section 3 in. thick by 6 in. wide (dimensions 3 in., 3 in., 3 in. and a 1 in. offset marked at the top). The load P acts at the top on a small projection (bracket) offset from the axis. Axes x and y drawn at the top.","sol":"1) Fixed–free: K = 2. Use d = 3 in. (smaller dimension) so KL/d is as large as possible: KL/d = 2(60)/3 = 40. 2) Since 26 \u003c 40 \u003c 50, σ_allow = 540/(KL/d)² = 540/40² = 0.3375 ksi. 3) With σ_allow = σ_max: 0.3375 = P/A + Mc/I = P/[(3)(6)] + P(4)(3)/[(1/12)(3)(6)³] (e = 4 in., c = 3 in., I = 54 in⁴, A = 18 in²). 4) 0.3375 = 0.0556P + 0.2222P = 0.2778P, so P = 1.22 kip.","answers":["P = 1.22 kip"]},{"id":"14.1","title":"Two bolts: maximum elastic strain energy each can absorb","q":"One of the two high-strength steel bolts A and B shown in the figure is to be chosen to support a sudden tensile loading. For the choice it is necessary to determine the greatest amount of elastic strain energy that each bolt can absorb. Bolt A has a diameter of 0.875 in. for 2 in. of its length and a root (or smallest) diameter of 0.731 in. within the 0.25-in. threaded region. Bolt B has \"upset\" threads, such that the diameter throughout its 2.25-in. length can be taken as 0.731 in. In both cases, neglect the extra material that makes up the threads. Take E_st = 29(10³) ksi, σ_Y = 44 ksi.","figs":["ex14-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Two bolts side by side, each with a hex head at the top and a nut below. Bolt A: shank diameter 0.875 in. over a 2 in. length, then a threaded region 0.25 in. long, root diameter 0.731 in. Bolt B: uniform shank diameter 0.731 in. over a 2.25 in. length.","sol":"1) The load is limited by yielding at the smallest section, the 0.731-in. root of the threaded region of bolt A (and the whole of B). P_max = σ_Y·A_min = 44·π(0.731/2)² = 44(0.4197) = 18.47 kip. The same P_max applies to both bolts. 2) Axial strain energy U_i = Σ N²L/(2AE) with N = P_max in every segment. 3) Bolt A: U = (18.47)²(2)/[2·π(0.875/2)²·29000] + (18.47)²(0.25)/[2·π(0.731/2)²·29000] = 0.0231 in.·kip. 4) Bolt B: U = (18.47)²(2.25)/[2·π(0.731/2)²·29000] = 0.0315 in.·kip. 5) Bolt B absorbs about 36% more energy because its cross section is smaller (more uniform stress) along the shank.","answers":["P_max = 18.47 kip","U_A = 0.0231 in.·kip","U_B = 0.0315 in.·kip"]},{"id":"14.2","title":"Bending strain energy of cantilever with uniform load","q":"Determine the elastic strain energy due to bending of the cantilevered beam shown in the figure. The beam has length L and carries a uniform distributed load w along its entire length. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-2.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever beam of length L fixed to a wall at its right end, free at the left end, loaded by a uniform downward distributed load w over the whole length.","sol":"1) Choose x with origin at the free (left) end. Cut at x and take the left segment: resultant wx at x/2, so ΣM = 0 gives M + wx(x/2) = 0, M = −wx²/2. 2) Bending strain energy U_i = ∫₀ᴸ M² dx/(2EI) = ∫₀ᴸ [−w x²/2]² dx/(2EI) = (w²/8EI)∫₀ᴸ x⁴ dx. 3) Integrate: ∫₀ᴸ x⁴ dx = L⁵/5, so U_i = w²L⁵/(40EI). (Using x measured from the wall gives the same result but with more algebra.)","answers":["U_i = w²L⁵/(40EI)"]},{"id":"14.3","title":"Bending strain energy in span AB of overhanging beam","q":"Determine the bending strain energy in region AB of the beam shown in the figure. The beam is pinned at A and supported by a roller at B, with AB = L, and overhangs to the free end C with BC = L. A downward point load P acts at C. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-3.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal beam ABC. Pin support at A (left end), roller support at B at distance L from A, free end C at distance L beyond B. Downward force P applied at C. Two dimensions L, L shown along the bottom.","sol":"1) Reactions: ΣM_A = 0 gives B_y(L) = P(2L), so B_y = 2P up; ΣF_y gives A_y = P down. 2) Region AB can be described with any x coordinate. Simplest: x₁ measured from A, 0 ≤ x₁ ≤ L. Cut at x₁ and take the left piece: ΣM = 0 gives M₁ + P x₁ = 0, M₁ = −P x₁. 3) U_i = ∫₀ᴸ M₁² dx₁/(2EI) = ∫₀ᴸ (P x₁)² dx₁/(2EI) = P²L³/(6EI). 4) Check (optional): using x₂ from B toward A, M₂ = P(x₂ − L), or x₃ from C, M₃ = P(x₃ − 2L) over L ≤ x₃ ≤ 2L, gives the same P²L³/(6EI), so any suitable coordinate works.","answers":["U_i (region AB) = P²L³/(6EI)"]},{"id":"14.4","title":"Shear strain energy in uniformly loaded square cantilever","q":"Determine the strain energy due to shear in the cantilevered beam shown in the figure if the beam has a square cross section of side a and is subjected to a uniform distributed load w along its length L. EI and G are constant.","figs":["ex14-4.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever beam of length L fixed to a wall at the right, free at the left, with uniform downward load w over the whole length. A small square cross-section sketch labelled a by a is shown at the left.","sol":"1) Section at distance x from the free end: ΣF_y = 0 gives −V − wx = 0, V = −wx. 2) Square cross section: form factor f_s = 6/5. 3) Shear strain energy (U_i)_s = ∫₀ᴸ f_s V² dx/(2GA) = ∫₀ᴸ (6/5)(wx)² dx/(2GA) = (3w²/5GA)∫₀ᴸ x² dx. 4) Integrate: (U_i)_s = w²L³/(5GA), with A = a². Comparison (note): with I = a⁴/12 the ratio to bending energy w²L⁵/(40EI) is (U_s/U_b) = (2/3)(a/L)²(E/G); with E ≤ 3G this is at most 2(a/L)², so for L = 5a shear energy is only about 8% of bending energy.","answers":["(U_i)_s = w²L³/(5GA)"]},{"id":"14.5","title":"Strain energy in tubular shaft under two torques","q":"The tubular shaft shown in the figure is fixed at the wall and subjected to two torques as shown: 40 N·m at the free end and 55 N·m applied 750 mm from the free end (the shaft is 750 mm + 300 mm long, the 300 mm segment being next to the wall). The tube has outer radius 80 mm and wall thickness 15 mm. Determine the strain energy stored in the shaft due to this loading. G = 75 GPa.","figs":["ex14-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Hollow circular shaft fixed to a wall at the upper right. Torque 40 N·m applied at the free (lower-left) end, torque 55 N·m applied at a section 750 mm from the free end (opposite sense), and a further 300 mm segment between that section and the wall. End cross-section labelled 80 mm (outer radius) and 15 mm (wall thickness).","sol":"1) Method of sections gives constant internal torque in each region: T = 40 N·m in the 750-mm segment (free end to the 55 N·m torque) and T = 55 − 40 = 15 N·m in the 300-mm segment next to the wall. The torques are opposite in direction but the energy depends on T², so signs don\u0027t matter. 2) J = (π/2)[(0.08)⁴ − (0.065)⁴] = 36.30(10⁻⁶) m⁴ (inner radius 80 − 15 = 65 mm). 3) U_i = Σ T²L/(2GJ) = (40)²(0.750)/[2(75·10⁹)(36.30·10⁻⁶)] + (15)²(0.300)/[2(75·10⁹)(36.30·10⁻⁶)] = 220.4 + 12.4 μJ = 233 μJ.","answers":["U_i = 233 μJ"]},{"id":"14.6","title":"Three-bar truss: horizontal displacement at B by energy","q":"The three-bar truss shown in the figure is subjected to a horizontal force of 5 kip at joint B. The truss is pinned at A and on a roller at C. The cross-sectional area of each member is 0.20 in². Determine the horizontal displacement at point B. E = 29(10³) ksi.","figs":["ex14-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Right-triangle truss. A at the bottom left (pin), B directly above A at 2 ft height, C at the bottom right on a roller. Member BC (hypotenuse) is 4 ft long; angle at B between AB and BC is 60°, angle at C is 30°. A horizontal 5 kip force acts to the right at B.","sol":"1) Conservation of energy applies: a single external force, and the desired displacement is in the same direction as that force; the supports do no work. 2) Method of joints (joint B then C): N_AB = 2.89 kip (vertical, member length 2 ft), N_BC = 5.77 kip (length 4 ft), N_AC = 5 kip (length 3.46 ft); only squares matter so tension or compression doesn\u0027t matter. 3) External work = internal energy: ½P Δ = Σ N²L/(2AE). ½(5)(Δ_B)_h = [(2.89)²(2) + (5.77)²(4) + (5)²(3.46)]/(2AE) → (Δ_B)_h = 47.32 kip·ft/(AE). 4) Δ = 47.32(12)/[(0.2)(29 000)] = 0.0979 in. to the right (same direction as the force).","answers":["(Δ_B)_h = 0.0979 in. (to the right)"]},{"id":"14.7","title":"Cantilever with end load: displacement including shear energy","q":"The cantilevered beam shown in the figure has a rectangular cross section (width b, height h), length L, and is subjected to a downward load P at its free end. Determine the displacement of the load. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-7.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever beam of length L fixed to a wall at the right, with a downward point load P at the free (left) end. A small rectangular cross-section sketch shows width b and height h.","sol":"1) Section at distance x from the free end: V = −P, M = −Px. 2) Conservation of energy: ½PΔ = ∫ f_s V² dx/(2GA) + ∫ M² dx/(2EI), with f_s = 6/5. 3) Evaluate: = 3P²L/(5GA) + P²L³/(6EI) (the first term is shear energy, the second bending). 4) The shear term is much smaller than the bending term unless the beam is short and deep: requires 0.9 \u003c\u003c (L/h)² (using E ≤ 3G); for L = 5h shear energy is about 3.6% of the total, so it is neglected. 5) Hence ½PΔ = P²L³/(6EI), so Δ = PL³/(3EI).","answers":["Δ = PL³/(3EI) (shear energy neglected)"]},{"id":"14.8","title":"Aluminum pipe under weight: gradual versus sudden load","q":"The aluminum pipe shown in the figure is used to support a load of 150 kip. The pipe has outer radius 3 in., wall thickness t = 0.5 in., and length 12 in. Determine the maximum displacement at the top of the pipe if the load is (a) applied gradually, and (b) applied by suddenly releasing it from the top of the pipe (h = 0). Take E_al = 10(10³) ksi and assume that the aluminum behaves elastically.","figs":["ex14-8.webp"],"figDesc":"A hollow aluminum pipe standing on the ground, 12 in. high, outer radius 3 in., wall thickness t = 0.5 in. A heavy block hangs on a chain above the top of the pipe, labelled 150 kip, at a drop height h above the pipe top.","sol":"1) Area: A = π[(3)² − (2.5)²] = 8.639 in² (outer radius 3 in., inner 2.5 in.). 2) Part (a), gradual loading: external work ½WΔ_st equals strain energy W²L/(2AE), so Δ_st = WL/(AE) = 150(12)/[8.639(10 000)] = 0.0208 in. 3) Part (b), sudden release with h = 0: Δ_max = Δ_st[1 + √(1 + 2h/Δ_st)] = Δ_st(1 + 1) = 2Δ_st = 0.0417 in. 4) The dynamic displacement is twice the static one, i.e. impact factor n = 2.","answers":["(a) Δ_st = 0.0208 in.","(b) Δ_max = 0.0417 in."]},{"id":"14.9","title":"Steel beam struck by dropped weight: deflection and stress","q":"The A992 steel beam shown in the figure is a W10 × 39 simply supported over a span of 16 ft (8 ft each side of midspan). Determine the maximum bending stress in the beam and the beam\u0027s maximum deflection if a weight W = 1.50 kip is dropped from a height h = 2 in. onto the beam at midspan. E_st = 29(10³) ksi. (For a W10 × 39: I = 209 in⁴ and depth d = 9.92 in., so c = d/2.)","figs":["ex14-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Simply supported W-shape beam with a pin support at the left and a roller at the right, span 16 ft (two 8 ft dimensions about the midspan). A weight W hangs on a chain above midspan at height h = 2 in. above the top of the beam.","sol":"1) Static deflection under W at midspan: Δ_st = WL³/(48EI) = 1.50(16)³(12)³/[48(29 000)(209)] = 0.03649 in. 2) Impact: Δ_max = Δ_st[1 + √(1 + 2h/Δ_st)] = 0.03649[1 + √(1 + 2(2)/0.03649)] = 0.420 in. 3) Equivalent static load: P_max = 48EIΔ_max/L³ = 48(29 000)(209)(0.420)/[(16)³(12)³] = 17.3 kip. 4) M_max = P_max L/4 at midspan. σ_max = M_max c/I = P_max L c/(4I) = 12EΔ_max c/L² = 12(29 000)(0.420)(9.92/2)/[(16)²(12)²] = 19.7 ksi. (Alternate: energy balance W(h + Δ_max) = ½[48EIΔ_max/L³]Δ_max gives 20.55Δ² − 1.50Δ − 3.00 = 0, Δ_max = 0.420 in.)","answers":["Δ_max = 0.420 in.","σ_max = 19.7 ksi"]},{"id":"14.10","title":"Railroad car striking a fixed post: maximum top displacement","q":"A railroad car that is assumed to be rigid and has a mass of 80 Mg is moving forward at a speed of v = 0.2 m/s when it strikes a steel 200-mm by 200-mm post at A. The post is fixed to the ground at C; point A is 1.5 m above C, and the top of the post, B, is 1 m above A. Determine the maximum horizontal displacement of its top B due to the impact. Take E_st = 200 GPa.","figs":["ex14-10.webp"],"figDesc":"A boxcar on rails moving to the right at v = 0.2 m/s toward a vertical post. The post is labelled C at the ground, A at 1.5 m above C (impact point), and B at 1 m above A (top). Cross-section of the post is 200 mm by 200 mm.","sol":"1) Kinetic energy of the car becomes bending strain energy in region AC only (BA carries no load). Let P_max cause (Δ_A)_max at A, a cantilever of length L_AC = 1.5 m: P_max = 3EI(Δ_A)_max/L_AC³. 2) Energy balance: ½mv² = ½P_max(Δ_A)_max = ½(3EI/L³)(Δ_A)²_max, so (Δ_A)_max = √[mv²L³/(3EI)]. 3) I = (1/12)(0.2)⁴ = 1.333(10⁻⁴) m⁴. (Δ_A)_max = √[80(10³)(0.2)²(1.5)³/(3(200·10⁹)(1.333·10⁻⁴))] = 0.01162 m = 11.62 mm. 4) P_max = 3EI(Δ_A)/L³ = 275.4 kN. 5) Slope at A: θ_A = P_max L²/(2EI) = 0.01162 rad. 6) Segment AB stays straight: (Δ_B)_max = (Δ_A)_max + θ_A L_AB = 11.62 mm + 0.01162(1000) = 23.2 mm.","answers":["(Δ_B)_max = 23.2 mm"]},{"id":"14.11","title":"Virtual work: vertical displacement of truss joint C","q":"Determine the vertical displacement of joint C of the steel truss shown in the figure. The cross-sectional area of each member is A = 400 mm² and E_st = 200 GPa. The truss has pinned supports at A and D on the wall, a 100 kN downward load at B, and members AB, BC, AC, CD.","figs":["ex14-11.webp"],"figDesc":"Planar truss attached to a wall at the left by pins at D (top) and A (bottom), 2 m apart vertically. Top member DC is horizontal, 2 m long, with C above the midpoint of the bottom member AB (AB = 4 m, two 2 m dimensions). Diagonals AC and CB. A 100 kN downward load is applied at B. Height dimension 2 m shown on the right.","sol":"1) Virtual-force method: apply a vertical 1-kN virtual load at C (only), find member forces n by joints (tension positive). 2) Real forces N from the 100 kN load. 3) Table (n, N, L): AB: 0, −100 kN, 4 m; BC: 0, 141.4 kN, 2.828 m; AC: −1.414, −141.4 kN, 2.828 m; CD: 1, 200 kN, 2 m. nNL: AB 0; BC 0; AC = (−1.414)(−141.4)(2.828) = 565.7; CD = (1)(200)(2) = 400; Σ = 965.7 kN²·m. 4) 1 kN·Δ_Cv = Σ nNL/(AE) = 965.7/[(400·10⁻⁶ m²)(200·10⁶ kN/m²)]. 5) Δ_Cv = 0.01207 m = 12.1 mm, positive, so C moves downward (in the direction of the virtual load).","answers":["Δ_Cv = 12.1 mm (downward)"]},{"id":"14.12","title":"Truss with heating and fabrication error: horizontal displacement at B","q":"Determine the horizontal displacement of the roller at B of the truss shown in the figure. Due to radiant heating, member AB is subjected to an increase in temperature of ΔT = +60°C, and this member has been fabricated 3 mm too short. The members are made of steel, for which α_st = 12(10⁻⁶)/°C and E_st = 200 GPa. The cross-sectional area of each member is 250 mm². A 6 kN downward load acts at C. Geometry: AC is horizontal, AB = BC = 4 m, and the angle at A and at C is 30°.","figs":["ex14-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Triangular truss ABC. A is pinned at the upper left, C is the free upper right joint carrying a 6 kN downward load, B is the lower joint supported on a roller (horizontal motion allowed). AC is the horizontal top member; AB and BC slope down to B, each 4 m long, with 30° angles at A and C.","sol":"1) Apply a horizontal 1-kN virtual load at B; the member forces n are: AB = −1.155 kN, AC = 0, BC = 0. 2) Real forces: N_AB = −12 kN (and N_AC = 10.39, N_BC = −12 kN; the real forces in AC and BC are not needed since n = 0 there). 3) Combine the load, temperature and fabrication terms: 1·Δ_Bh = Σ nNL/(AE) + Σ nαΔT L + Σ nΔL. 4) Load: (−1.155)(−12)(4)/[(250·10⁻⁶)(200·10⁶)] = 0.001109 m. Temperature: (−1.155)(12·10⁻⁶)(60)(4) = −0.003326 m. Fabrication (3 mm too short, ΔL = −0.003 m): (−1.155)(−0.003) = +0.003465 m. 5) Sum: Δ_Bh = 0.00125 m = 1.25 mm, positive, so B moves in the direction of the virtual load (to the left).","answers":["Δ_Bh = 1.25 mm (to the left)"]},{"id":"14.13","title":"Virtual work: end deflection of uniformly loaded cantilever","q":"Determine the displacement of point B on the beam shown in the figure. The cantilever has length L, is fixed at A (right end) and free at B (left end), and carries a uniform downward distributed load w over its whole length. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-13.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever of length L, fixed to a wall at the right end A, free end B at the left, with uniform downward distributed load w over the entire length.","sol":"1) Virtual-force method: place a virtual unit downward load at B. Use one x coordinate with origin at B (no discontinuities, and no reactions needed). 2) Virtual moment: m = −1·x. 3) Real moment from the uniform load: M = −w x²/2. 4) 1·Δ_B = ∫₀ᴸ mM dx/EI = ∫₀ᴸ (−x)(−w x²/2) dx/EI = (w/2EI)∫₀ᴸ x³ dx. 5) Δ_B = wL⁴/(8EI), positive so downward (same direction as the virtual load).","answers":["Δ_B = wL⁴/(8EI) (downward)"]},{"id":"14.14","title":"Virtual work: slope at midpoint of cantilever with end load","q":"Determine the slope at point B of the beam shown in the figure. The cantilever is fixed at C (left end), has length L with B at its midpoint (L/2 from C), and carries a downward point load P at the free end A. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-14.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever fixed to a wall at the left end C, free end A at the right with a downward force P. Point B is at the midpoint; the two halves are each labelled L/2.","sol":"1) Virtual-force method for slope: apply a virtual unit couple moment at B. Two x coordinates are needed because the virtual moment is discontinuous at B: x₁ from A to B (0 ≤ x₁ ≤ L/2) and x₂ from B to C (0 ≤ x₂ ≤ L/2). 2) Virtual moments: m_θ1 = 0 in AB; m_θ2 = 1 in BC. 3) Real moments (same coordinates): M₁ = −P x₁; M₂ = −P(L/2 + x₂). 4) 1·θ_B = ∫ m_θ M dx/EI = ∫₀^{L/2} 0(−P x₁) dx₁/EI + ∫₀^{L/2} 1·{−P(L/2 + x₂)} dx₂/EI = −P(L²/4 + L²/8)/EI. 5) θ_B = −3PL²/(8EI); the negative sign means the slope is opposite to the sense of the virtual couple (the book\u0027s virtual couple is counterclockwise, so the actual rotation is clockwise).","answers":["θ_B = −3PL²/(8EI) (magnitude 3PL²/8EI, opposite to the assumed virtual couple)"]},{"id":"14.15","title":"Castigliano: vertical displacement of truss joint C","q":"Determine the vertical displacement of joint C of the steel truss shown in the figure. The cross-sectional area of each member is A = 400 mm² and E_st = 200 GPa. The truss has pinned supports at A and D on the wall, a 100 kN downward load at B, and members AB, BC, AC, CD.","figs":["ex14-15.webp"],"figDesc":"Planar truss attached to a wall at the left by pins at D (top) and A (bottom), 2 m apart vertically. Top member DC is horizontal, 2 m long, with C above the midpoint of the bottom member AB (AB = 4 m, two 2 m dimensions). Diagonals AC and CB. A 100 kN downward load is applied at B. Height dimension 2 m shown at right.","sol":"1) Castigliano\u0027s second theorem: place a variable vertical force P at C (it is set to zero afterwards). 2) Find member forces N in terms of the 100 kN load and P (method of joints; supports at A, D give reactions 200 kN + P and 100 kN + P): N_AB = −100, N_BC = 141.4, N_AC = −(141.4 + 1.414P), N_CD = 200 + P (kN, tension positive). 3) Partial derivatives ∂N/∂P: AB 0, BC 0, AC −1.414, CD 1. 4) Set P = 0 and tabulate N(∂N/∂P)L with L = 4, 2.828, 2.828, 2 m: AB 0; BC 0; AC (−141.4)(−1.414)(2.828) = 565.7; CD (200)(1)(2) = 400; Σ = 965.7 kN·m. 5) Δ_Cv = Σ N(∂N/∂P)L/(AE) = 965.7/[(400·10⁻⁶)(200·10⁶)] = 0.01207 m = 12.1 mm, positive, in the direction of P (downward).","answers":["Δ_Cv = 12.1 mm (downward)"]},{"id":"14.16","title":"Castigliano: end deflection of uniformly loaded cantilever","q":"Determine the displacement of point B on the beam shown in the figure. The cantilever has length L, is fixed at A (right end) and free at B (left end), and carries a uniform downward distributed load w over its whole length. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-16.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever of length L, fixed to a wall at the right end A, free end B at the left, with uniform downward distributed load w over the entire length.","sol":"1) Castigliano\u0027s second theorem: place a variable vertical force P at B (downward), set to zero after differentiating. 2) One x coordinate from B. Moment: M = −wx²/2 − Px; ∂M/∂P = −x. 3) Set P = 0: M = −wx²/2, ∂M/∂P = −x. 4) Δ_B = ∫₀ᴸ M(∂M/∂P) dx/EI = ∫₀ᴸ (−wx²/2)(−x) dx/EI = (w/2EI)(L⁴/4). 5) Δ_B = wL⁴/(8EI), positive so downward, same direction as P (agrees with the virtual-work method).","answers":["Δ_B = wL⁴/(8EI) (downward)"]},{"id":"14.17","title":"Castigliano: slope at midpoint of cantilever with end load","q":"Determine the slope at point B of the beam shown in the figure. The cantilever is fixed at C (left end), has length L with B at its midpoint (L/2 from C), and carries a downward point load P at the free end A. EI is constant.","figs":["ex14-17.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal cantilever fixed to a wall at the left end C, free end A at the right with a downward force P. Point B is at the midpoint; the two halves are each labelled L/2.","sol":"1) Castigliano for slope: place a variable external couple M\u0027 at B (set to zero after differentiating). 2) Two coordinates: x₁ from A to B (0 ≤ x₁ ≤ L/2) and x₂ from B to C (0 ≤ x₂ ≤ L/2), since M\u0027 creates a discontinuity at B. 3) Moments: M₁ = −P x₁, ∂M₁/∂M\u0027 = 0. M₂ = M\u0027 − P(L/2 + x₂), ∂M₂/∂M\u0027 = 1. 4) Set M\u0027 = 0 and apply θ_B = ∫ M(∂M/∂M\u0027) dx/EI = ∫₀^{L/2} (−P x₁)(0) dx₁/EI + ∫₀^{L/2} −P(L/2 + x₂)(1) dx₂/EI. 5) θ_B = −3PL²/(8EI); negative means the slope is opposite to the sense assumed for M\u0027 (assumed counterclockwise, so actual rotation is clockwise).","answers":["θ_B = −3PL²/(8EI) (magnitude 3PL²/8EI, opposite to the assumed M\u0027)"]}];
